{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=\\\"poc/data/clean-step1-gate2-memory-completion-2026-11-13/combined-records.jsonl\\\"; ids={\\\"d1-memory\\\":{1,3,4,5,7,8,9,10,11,13,14,15,16,17,18},\\\"d2-memory\\\":{1,6,7,10,14,18},\\\"d3-memory\\\":{1,7,11,15,16}}; xs=(json.loads(s) for s in open(p)); [(print(\\\"\\\\n###\\\",x[\\\"case\\\"],x[\\\"send\\\"]),print(x[\\\"attempts\\\"][-1][\\\"response\\\"][\\\"choices\\\"][0][\\\"message\\\"].get(\\\"reasoning_content\\\",\\\"\\\"))) for x in xs if x.get(\\\"arm\\\")==\\\"c8\\\" and x.get(\\\"case\\\") in ids and x.get(\\\"send\\\") in ids[x[\\\"case\\\"]]]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=\\\"poc/data/clean-step1-gate2-memory-completion-2026-11-13/combined-records.jsonl\\\"; ids={\\\"d1-memory\\\":{1,3,4,5,7,8,9,10,11,13,14,15,16,17,18},\\\"d2-memory\\\":{1,6,7,10,14,18},\\\"d3-memory\\\":{1,7,11,15,16}}; xs=(json.loads(s) for s in open(p)); [(print(\\\"\\\\n###\\\",x[\\\"case\\\"],x[\\\"send\\\"]),print(x[\\\"attempts\\\"][-1][\\\"response\\\"][\\\"choices\\\"][0][\\\"message\\\"].get(\\\"reasoning_content\\\",\\\"\\\"))) for x in xs if x.get(\\\"arm\\\")==\\\"c8\\\" and x.get(\\\"case\\\") in ids and x.get(\\\"send\\\") in ids[x[\\\"case\\\"]]]'\"","aggregated_output":"\n### d1-memory 1\nLet me carefully read the source item and identify material statements.\n\nThe item is a MarketScale article about Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack costs, $7.8M, memory being the reason, based on Morgan Stanley bill-of-materials analysis.\n\nKey material statements:\n\n1. Morgan Stanley Research estimates Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for GB300 NVL72. This is a third-party view (analyst estimate). This bears on product node (Vera Rubin first shipments) thesis? And demand/capex thesis? This is a pricing/cost estimate. It could inform clean-3 (Vera Rubin first shipments) and perhaps clean-4 (hyperscaler capex). Let me think.\n\n2. Morgan Stanley estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume. Third-party view — forecast of Nvidia's pricing. Material. Could inform clean-3 (product node) or a thesis about GPU pricing/revenue. Note: section 2 says figures of the company's own... but this is an estimate by a named party. Not the company's own figures. The CONDITION applies to company's own financial figures. These are estimate but not guidance. Should they be figures? The schema says figures: \"The company's own useful financial figures this statement states (section 2, CONDITION)\". Per CONDITION, \"a named party's or consensus estimate\" lists useful figures in figures. Hmm, the CONDITION says: \"a statement of the company's own financial figures (a reported result or balance, its guidance, or a named party's or consensus estimate) lists its useful figures in figures.\" So a named party's estimate of Nvidia's pricing counts — includes estimates. So $55,000 per Rubin GPU, $5,000 per Vera CPU are estimates that are useful. And $7.8M per rack is a third-party estimate of total system cost — not necessarily Nvidia's revenue directly, but a unit price estimate.\n\n3. First shipments are scheduled for Q3 2026, with volume ramping in Q4. This is fact/party statement? The article reports this as scheduled first shipments. This directly informs clean-3: \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments before end of calendar 2026?\" Q3 2026 first shipments would be before end of 2026. So link to clean-3.\n\nTier: The shipments schedule — is it a fact (reported plan)? It's a plan reported by the article, attributed to Morgan Stanley? \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\" The article reports it as a schedule. Since it's a plan, and reported without clear attribution... It's an official plan but reported in this analysis. Tier: fact for a reported/fact? Actually a scheduled plan is not a completed outcome. Could be party_statement if it's Nvidia's own schedule. But the item doesn't attribute it. Hmm. Let me classify as party_statement if schedule is Nvidia's; but since it's not attributed to Nvidia directly... The article states \"First shipments are scheduled for...\" This is a plan. I'll use party_statement — a plan/guidance statement. Actually could be fact since it's reported as a confirmed schedule. Rules: \"fact: an official action, published actual figure or completed transaction, including an announcement or completed transaction reported by an article.\" A shipment schedule is an announcement of plan. Hmm. I'll use party_statement since it's a forward-looking schedule/plan, and it's not clear whose statement. Actually the shipment schedule could be Nvidia's own announcement. Let me use party_statement.\n\nActually the article is summarizing Morgan Stanley. The first shipment schedule likely is from Nvidia. I'll categorize as party_statement (a plan).\n\n4. Memory cost per rack ~$2 million, a 435% jump; LPDDR5X capacity tripled to 54 TB; ~$1 million+ in 3D NAND. These are supply/input cost signals. Component costs — memory supply constraints. This informs clean-3 (Vera Rubin) and possibly a thesis about input costs / bill of materials. Does any open thesis cover input costs? clean-1 credit support, clean-2 guarantee, clean-3 shipments, clean-4 hyperscaler capex. None specifically on input costs/BOM. This could be a new thesis? But input costs (memory) — is it a \"shared_quantity\"? The price of memory (DRAM/NAND/HBM) is a quantity/price outside any one company that several companies depend on. \"next year's average contract price of a raw material.\" Hmm. But this is a component cost. Could open a shared_quantity thesis: \"What will memory (DRAM/NAND/HBM) contract prices be for AI servers in 2026-2027?\" But is that within Nvidia scope? It affects Nvidia input costs but Nvidia isn't the one buying memory — the rack OEM (Foxconn etc.) or Nvidia buys? Actually Nvidia designs the rack; memory is bought by Nvidia's supply chain. The cost of memory affects the total rack price which affects Nvidia's revenue per rack. Hmm.\n\nBut actually the memory cost is a component of the rack the customer buys, and Nvidia sells the GPU at $55k. The memory is part of the bill of materials. This is about the cost structure of the AI rack. Does it change Nvidia's revenue/profit? The rack price increase ($4M to $7.8M) — the GPU share fell from 63% to 51%. GPU revenue per rack: 51% of $7.8M = ~$3.98M; prior 63% of $4M = $2.52M. So GPU content per rack roughly rose. Interesting. But an analyst would note the GPU attach and rack value.\n\nLet me consider whether to propose a new thesis about memory/input costs or the rack pricing. Given the rules: \"Do not open a thesis about a financial measure's level, growth, margin or guidance for any period, on its own.\" The rack price is a product price — but \"the company's decision to change the price it charges\" is a product detail that's not kept as a thesis. Hmm.\n\nActually there's a strong candidate: the Morgan Stanley estimates of GPU and CPU pricing ($55k, $5k per unit) and rack cost ($7.8M) inform clean-3 (Vera Rubin shipments) and clean-4 (hyperscaler capex — higher rack cost affects capex and demand).\n\nClean-4 asks about the combined data center capex of big four hyperscalers reaching $1 trillion in 2027. The rack cost doubling from $4M to $7.8M is relevant to how much capex buys — informs the capex thesis (higher per-rack cost means more capex for same compute, or could dampen volumes). Link it.\n\nClean-3 asks whether Vera Rubin ships before end of 2026. First shipments Q3 2026 directly relevant. Link.\n\n5. \"memory supply constraints and tight availability now warrant the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation\" — general, and the DRAM/NAND/HBM supply squeeze. \"Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, device makers including Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\" This is about memory market, affecting component costs. Could be a shared_quantity thesis on memory contract prices. But is it material enough / does it bear on Nvidia? Memory supply tightness could constrain Vera Rubin rack production/deliveries — informs clean-3 (shipment timeline risk). Also input costs.\n\n6. Hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring rack price down to ~$6.7 million. That's a procurement detail, estimate. Minor.\n\n7. The rest of the article: Uber ChatGPT, PHP flaws, cooling — these are unrelated \"next page\" content / other stories. Not material to Nvidia.\n\nLet me also consider: \"demand is not in question\" — general claim, not kept.\n\n\"GPU share of bill of materials fell from 63% to 51%\" — bill of materials composition, estimate. Material? It informs Nvidia GPU content per rack. Could be part of the same estimate item about BOM. Actually I think the $7.8M rack cost estimate and the BOM composition (GPU 51%, memory 25-30%) are one statement told in several sentences. But GPU pricing ($55k/$5k) is a separate specific estimate of Nvidia's unit pricing.\n\nLet me structure evidence items:\n\nEvidence A: Morgan Stanley estimates Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack at ~$7.8M per unit for hyperscalers, up from ~$4M GB300 NVL72; memory ~$2M/rack (25-30% of BOM vs 5-10%); GPU share of BOM fell from 63% to 51%. This is one estimate (BOM analysis). Tier third_party_view. Figures: rack cost $7.8M estimate, prior $4M estimate, memory content $2M estimate. Links: clean-3 (product node — Vera Rubin), clean-4 (capex per unit). Actually the BOM share is composition — could combine. But \"GPU share fell from 63% to 51%\" and \"memory 25-30%\" — I'll fold into the rack cost item.\n\nHmm, but let me separate the memory cost figure as its own? The rule: \"one deal, hire, programme or reported measure told in several sentences, with its terms, figures and growth rates, is one evidence item; keep the fuller wording.\" The rack cost is the reported measure; its sub-components (memory $2M, GPU 51%) are terms. So one item covering the BOM estimate.\n\nActually there might be two distinct estimates: (1) the rack total cost $7.8M, (2) Nvidia's unit pricing $55k GPU / $5k CPU. These are separate estimates of separate measures — I'll keep them as two items. The unit pricing is a distinct measure (Nvidia's charge per GPU/CPU).\n\nEvidence B: Morgan Stanley estimates Nvidia will charge ~$55,000 per Rubin GPU and ~$5,000 per Vera CPU at volume. Third-party estimate. Figures: $55,000 per Rubin GPU (estimate), $5,000 per Vera CPU (estimate). Links: clean-3? This is about Vera Rubin pricing. Yes informs clean-3 and maybe clean-4. Actually pricing of the GPU bears on revenue and on clean-4 capex. I'll link clean-3 and clean-4. Hmm, clean-4 is aggregated capex of hyperscalers reaching $1T — GPU pricing informs whether capex reaches that. And clean-3 is shipments. Pricing alone doesn't settle shipments but informs the product-node thesis (revenue magnitude). The product_node thesis is about first shipments timing, not pricing. Pricing doesn't inform timing. Does it inform clean-4? Higher GPU prices → more capex for same units. Marginally. Let me link to clean-4 as the value-per-unit estimate that bears on the capex aggregate. And maybe link clean-3 too since it's the product carrying value. I think linking both is defensible. But I should be careful — the product_node thesis clean-3 is specifically about timing of first shipments, and pricing does not inform timing. Better to link clean-4 (cale of AI infrastructure spend). Hmm, but clean-4 is a specific aggregate. Pricing per GPU is a component. I'll link clean-4.\n\nActually, wait — is there a thesis for Nvidia's GPU pricing/AI revenue per rack? None open. Would an analyst bet separately on Nvidia's per-GPU pricing for Rubin? That's a pricing thesis — but \"the company's decision to change the price it charges\" is not kept as a thesis, and a financial measure (price) on its own isn't a thesis. So no new thesis; link to clean-4 as it bears on the infrastructure spend scale, or leave linked to clean-3.\n\nEvidence C: First shipments of Vera Rubin scheduled for Q3 2026, volume ramp Q4. Plan. Tier party_statement. Links: clean-3 directly.\n\nEvidence D: Memory costs — memory content per rack ~$2M, 435% jump, LPDDR5X capacity tripled to 54 TB/rack, ~$1M+ 3D NAND. This is part of the BOM estimate (Evidence A). Actually it's the driver of the cost increase. I could fold into A. But it's a distinct measure (memory content). The rule says one reported measure with terms is one item. I'll fold memory content into A since it's the reason for the rack cost. But then figures list includes memory $2M and 435% and 54TB. Let me include them in A. Hmm but 54TB capacity and 3D NAND are component specs — \"a product specification with no business scale\" is not kept. 54TB LPDDR5X capacity is a spec — not kept on its own. So I'll not pull that out. The $2M memory content is a cost figure with business scale, part of the rack cost estimate. Include in A.\n\nEvidence E: Memory supply constraints / DRAM, NAND, HBM tight supply against AI demand; contract DDR5 pricing climbed sharply; Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into products. This is about memory market tightness — a supply constraint relevant to Vera Rubin rack build (input cost/scheduling). Does it inform a thesis? clean-3 (shipments schedule — supply risk could delay). Also could inform clean-4 (cost). It's arguably a general industry supply tightness claim. Is it \"the steelmaker expects shortage to peak next year\" type dated forecast of shortage — kept. This is a \"reported movement of a named price... even without a figure\" — contract DDR5 pricing climbed. Kept. Link to clean-3 (supply risk to Vera Rubin ramp).\n\nHmm, but does it inform clean-3 which is about timing of first shipments? Tight memory supply is a scheduling risk that could affect shipments. Yes, relevant evidence. Link clean-3.\n\nEvidence F: Hyperscaler self-sourcing memory modules could bring rack price to ~$6.7M. Procurement estimate. Minor. Could fold into A (procurement detail). I'll fold it or make separate? It's a specific estimate of $6.7M. It's about how procurement structure moves pricing \"$1+ million per rack\". I'll fold into A as a term, or leave it. Let me include in A's summary/figure? A figure of $6.7M is a distinct scenario estimate. I think keep A simpler. Actually I'll include $6.7M as a separate small estimate? \"procurement structure able to move final pricing by $1+ million per rack\" plus $6.7M. I'll fold into A.\n\nNow, should I propose any new thesis?\n\nCandidate 1: Memory/input-cost thesis — \"What will DRAM/NAND/HBM contract prices be in 2026-2027, given the AI-driven squeeze?\" This is a shared_quantity (a price outside any one company several companies depend on, next calendar year). Nvidia depends on memory prices as rack cost. But is this within the company's scope and would an analyst bet on it separately for Nvidia? The rack cost bears on Nvidia's product cost competitiveness. Hmm. But the memory price is more of a supplier (Micron/SK Hynix/Samsung) matter. For Nvidia, memory is part of the rack BOM but Nvidia doesn't directly buy/sell it. Actually who bears the memory cost? If Nvidia sells the full rack (NVL72 rack) to hyperscalers, Nvidia is buying the memory and selling the rack. Does Nvidia sell full racks? Nvidia sells the rack-scale system, so Nvidia's BOM includes memory. So memory costs affect Nvidia's product cost and could affect its gross margin or its ability to price. That's significant! But wait — Morgan Stanley says hyperscalers pay $7.8M per rack and GPU is $55k×72=~$3.96M, CPU $5k×36=... So Nvidia sells the GPU/CPU and the OEM integrates? Actually Nvidia's rack includes everything. Hmm.\n\nIf Nvidia sells full racks, memory cost is Nvidia's input cost, affecting its gross margin. That's material. But would an analyst bet separately on \"will memory contract prices rise, squeezing Nvidia's rack gross margin\"? That could be a shared_quantity thesis (memory contract price). But is that Nvidia-specific enough / does the item support a settle date? The item gives no explicit contract price forecast. It says \"contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply.\" The shared_quantity would be \"average contract price of DRAM/NAND in 2026-2027.\" This is more a Micron thesis. For Nvidia, I'd link memory cost evidence to... there's no open thesis on Nvidia gross margin or input costs. \n\nHmm, the question is whether an analyst following Nvidia would hold a separate card for \"Nvidia's memory input costs / rack BOM margin.\" I think it's borderline. The item frames it as a cost for infrastructure buyers (hyperscalers), not Nvidia's margin necessarily. Actually the rack price is what hyperscalers pay; the memory is included. Whether Nvidia or the OEM buys memory — for NVL72, Nvidia and partners build the rack. Nvidia's revenue is the GPU+CPU+networking. Memory is often sourced by the OEM/ODM. So memory cost may not be Nvidia's gross margin. I'll avoid opening a memory-cost thesis for Nvidia because it's not clearly Nvidia's revenue/profit driver and no clean settle date.\n\nBut the rack cost doubling — does that bear on clean-4 (hyperscaler capex $1T)? Yes: higher rack prices mean a given capex buys fewer racks, or the capex must grow to buy the same compute. This informs clean-4. Link.\n\nCandidate 2: A thesis on the Vera Rubin rack price / AI system pricing as a product pricing node? Not a valid thesis reason. Skip.\n\nSo no new thesis. All evidence links to existing theses (clean-3, clean-4).\n\nWait — let me reconsider clean-3 vs the BOM/rack cost. clean-3 is timing of first shipments. Evidence C (Q3 2026 shipments) directly. Evidence A (rack cost) doesn't tell timing but tells the product's value/scale — it's the product node carrying value. I'd link A to clean-3 (product carrying value/scale) AND clean-4 (capex aggregate). Reasonable.\n\nLet me reconsider whether clean-4 covers the rack cost. clean-4: combined DC capex of big four hyperscalers reaching $1T in 2027. The rack price is per-unit cost that bears on how much capex is absorbed. Higher rack prices → capex dollars per rack up. This informs the capex figure. Link.\n\nNow let me also double check the memory supply item (E) — does it inform clean-4? Higher memory costs raise total AI infrastructure cost, which bears on the capex aggregate. And clean-3 (supply risk to shipment). I'll link E to clean-3 (scheduling risk to Vera Rubin ramp) primarily, and maybe clean-4. I'll link clean-3.\n\nLet me now consider evidence items and writing.\n\nLet me re-examine the text for figures precisely:\n- \"$7.8 million\" per rack, \"up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation\"\n- \"about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume\"\n- \"memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump\"\n- \"threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage\"\n- \"memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent\"\n- \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth\"\n- \"self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million\"\n- \"Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply\"\n- \"Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products\"\n\nNow evidence items:\n\nE1 (rack cost + BOM): Summary: Morgan Stanley Research estimates Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers ~$7.8M per unit, up from ~$4M for GB300 NVL72; memory content ~$2M per rack (435% jump), memory's BOM share rose to 25-30% from 5-10% while GPU share fell to ~51% from ~63%. Tier third_party_view. Statement date: null (no date; article 2026-07-06). figures: rack cost $7.8M (estimate), $4M GB300 (estimate), memory $2M (estimate). Links: clean-3, clean-4.\n\nWait, statement_date: The Morgan Stanley analysis — no specific date given. The article date is 2026-07-06 but that's not the original statement date. So statement_date null, statement_date_text null. Actually we could set statement_date to null. Fine.\n\nE2 (GPU/CPU pricing): Summary: Morgan Stanley estimates Nvidia will charge ~$55,000 per Rubin GPU and ~$5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume. Tier third_party_view. figures: $55,000 per Rubin GPU (estimate), $5,000 per Vera CPU (estimate). Links: clean-3, clean-4? Pricing per GPU — the value of the product carrying Nvidia's value. I'll link clean-3 (product value) and clean-4. Hmm, but clean-3 is about timing of shipments. Pricing doesn't inform timing. I'll link to clean-4 only? Actually clean-4 is about hyperscaler capex aggregate reaching $1T; GPU unit price is a component but clean-4 is about hyperscaler's own capex, and GPU price affects it. I think link clean-4. But also the product_node thesis is \"will the product carrying the company's value reach first shipments.\" Pricing is a separate attribute. I'll link clean-4 for pricing since it bears on the scale of the capex. Let me link both to be safe? The rule: link to every thesis it informs, none it doesn't. Pricing informs the capex scale (clean-4) and the product's revenue potential (clean-3 is timing only though). I'll link clean-4 only for pricing. Hmm, but actually the product_node thesis outcome is \"first shipments.\" Pricing truly doesn't inform timing. So clean-4.\n\nActually reconsider: does GPU pricing inform clean-4? clean-4 is hyperscalers' combined capex. GPU price is what they pay Nvidia, part of capex. So yes marginally. But clean-4 is an aggregate of their capex that they report — GPU pricing is an input to that spending but the thesis is about the total they spend. I think it's reasonable to link. I'll link clean-4.\n\nE3 (shipments schedule): Summary: Vera Rubin first shipments are scheduled for Q3 2026, with volume ramping in Q4. Tier: party_statement (plan/schedule). Actually could be fact since it's a reported schedule. It's a plan → party_statement. Links: clean-3.\n\nHmm, but is the shipment schedule from Nvidia or Morgan Stanley? The passage: \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\" In context of Morgan Stanley analysis. It's a reported schedule likely from Nvidia. I'll attribute original_party to Nvidia (as the schedule) with reporting_speaker MarketScale. Or attribute to Morgan Stanley? The preceding sentences are Morgan Stanley estimates, but the schedule seems like a known Nvidia timeline. I'll set original_party \"Nvidia\" — hmm, but I'm not sure. Safer: original_party null? The statement is a plan probably of Nvidia. I'll set original_party \"Nvidia\" and note it's a shipment schedule. Actually if it's reported by MarketScale summarizing, and it is Nvidia's schedule, party_statement fits. Let me set original_party \"NVIDIA\" (the schedule's subject). reporting_speaker \"MarketScale\". \n\nE4 (memory supply tightness / DDR5 pricing): Summary: The article reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs: contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, tying the AI buildout to the same memory supply squeeze affecting consumer device prices. Tier: third_party_view (journalist's account) or fact for the reported price movement? \"Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply\" — a reported movement of a named price → fact? It's a reported market price movement. The tier classification: fact = published actual figure/completed transaction. A reported price movement is an actual reported fact. But no figure. It's a journalist's report of a market development. I'd lean third_party_view since it's the article's characterization/synthesis. Hmm. Actually \"contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply\" is a reported market fact. But attribution unclear. I'll use third_party_view (journalist's market account). Links: clean-3 (supply/scheduling risk to Vera Rubin ramp), and maybe clean-4 (raises AI infrastructure cost). I'll link clean-3 and clean-4.\n\nWait, does E4 inform clean-4? Memory cost squeeze raising total AI system cost bears on capex needed. Marginal. I'll link clean-3 primarily. Let me link clean-3 and clean-4? I'll link clean-3 (supply risk to shipments) and clean-4 (cost pressure on infrastructure budgets). Actually clean-4 is specifically the $1T capex number; memory costs are a small part. I'll link clean-3 only to be precise. Hmm, but the memory cost squeeze directly raises the per-rack cost (E1) which is clean-4-adjacent. I'll link both for completeness. Let me link clean-3 and clean-4.\n\nActually, let me be more disciplined. E4 about memory supply being tight and DDR5 prices climbing — this is an input-cost/supply signal for the AI rack. It informs (a) whether Vera Rubin shipments stay on schedule (clean-3) and (b) the cost of AI infrastructure spending (clean-4). Both reasonable. Link both.\n\nE5 (self-sourcing $6.7M): This is folded into E1 basically. The passage \"self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million\" — I'll fold into E1 as a term, but it's a separate scenario. I'll include in E1 summary maybe. Actually let me keep E1 focused on the headline rack cost and BOM. The $6.7M procurement scenario is a distinct estimate. I could include as a figure in E1. To keep clean, I'll add it to E1 figures? It complicates. I'll mention in summary \"procurement structure could lower rack price to ~$6.7M via self-sourcing memory.\" and add figure. Hmm, that's a lot in one. Let me just fold it in E1 briefly. Actually I'll leave E5 as a small separate evidence? It's an estimate that procurement structure moves pricing $1M+. Material? It's a planning consideration for buyers, not Nvidia's own revenue. Marginal. I'll fold into E1 and not separate.\n\nLet me finalize E1 to include rack cost, BOM shares, memory content. Include $6.7M maybe not. I'll keep E1 as above without $6.7M, and skip E5.\n\nNow check: Should E1 and E2 be separate? Yes, distinct measures (rack total cost vs Nvidia per-unit