king to clean-4? clean-4 is quite specific: \"combined data center capex reach $1 trillion in 2027.\" The rack cost/GPU pricing are the unit costs; they inform the dollar aggregate. I'll keep clean-4 links for cost/price items.\n\nLet me finalize.\n\nnew_theses: [] (empty).\n\nNow write JSON.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider whether to propose a new thesis on the Vera Rubin rack COST/pricing as a \"product_node\" event. No — product_node is about reaching first shipments/revenue. clean-3 already covers Vera Rubin shipments. Pricing is not a separate node. So no.\n\nOne more: The article says \"demand is not in question.\" General claim, not kept.\n\nAlso \"This story was produced through MarketScale\" boilerplate — not kept.\n\nFinal answer.\n\nLet me write summaries carefully and standalone.\n\nE1 summary: \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. Per the bank's bill-of-materials analysis, memory content is about $2 million per rack (a 435% jump from the prior generation), and as a share of the bill of materials memory rose from 5–10% on GB200 to 25–30% on VR200 while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\"\n\noriginal_party: \"Morgan Stanley Research\". reporting_speaker: \"MarketScale\". tier third_party_view.\n\nE2 summary: \"Morgan Stanley estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\"\n\noriginal_party: \"Morgan Stanley\". reporting_speaker: \"MarketScale\". tier third_party_view.\n\nE3 summary: \"Vera Rubin first shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\" original_party \"Nvidia\" reporting_speaker \"MarketScale\" tier party_statement. evidence_period \"Q3 2026 for first shipments; Q4 2026 volume ramp\". statement_date null.\n\nE4 summary: \"The article reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market: contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\" original_party null (journalist market account) or \"MarketScale\". reporting_speaker \"MarketScale\". tier third_party_view. Actually could be fact for the price rise. I'll use third_party_view.\n\nevidence_period for E4: \"2026\"; statement_date null.\n\nLet me set evidence_period for E1 \"2026 (VR200 generation)\"; for E2 \"at volume / VR200 generation\"; E3 \"Q3–Q4 2026\".\n\nI'll write periods as strings.\n\nNow produce JSON.\n\n### d1-memory 5\nLet me analyze the source item and the open theses.\n\nThe source item: MarketScale article about Nvidia Vera Rubin rack costs. Key facts from Morgan Stanley bill-of-materials analysis:\n- Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for current GB300 NVL72.\n- Memory content per rack ~$2 million, 435% jump, driven by 3x increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 TB per rack plus ~$1 million+ 3D NAND.\n- GPU share of BOM fell from 63% to 51%, memory rose to 25-30% from 5-10%.\n- Nvidia will charge ~$55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU in volume.\n- First shipments scheduled for Q3 2026, volume ramping Q4.\n- Memory supply constraints.\n- Morgan Stanley notes a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring rack price down to ~$6.7 million.\n\nNow, which statements are material?\n\nStatement 1: Morgan Stanley estimates Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost ~$7.8 million per unit vs ~$4 million for GB300, first shipments Q3 2026, volume Q4. This relates to clean-3 (Vera Rubin first shipments before end of calendar 2026). This is third_party_view. But also the pricing ($55,000 per GPU, $5,000 per CPU) is a named party's estimate about the company's produ