m 5-10% to 25-30%\". \n\nActually, I think the cleanest approach: keep several evidence items:\n- Rack cost estimate ($7.8M vs $4M, GPU $55k, CPU $5k) → link clean-3.\n- Shipment timing (Q3 2026 shipping, Q4 ramp) → link clean-3.\n- Memory cost surge (435% to ~$2M, 25-30% of BOM) → this is a cost/supply signal. Could link to clean-3 (product economics/supply risk for Vera Rubin) or leave unlinked or propose thesis.\n\nLet me reconsider the whole thing. The item is essentially a cost breakdown analysis for Vera Rubin. The main thesis-relevant facts:\n1. Vera Rubin ships Q3 2026, ramps Q4 2026 — clean-3.\n2. Rack costs $7.8M, GPU $55k — product economics.\n3. Memory 25-30% of cost, supply tight.\n\nFor clean-4 (hyperscaler capex $1T in 2027), the rack cost per unit is relevant to how much capex translates to NVDA revenue but not directly the aggregate capex. I don't think clean-4 is informed; clean-4 is about hyperscaler capex aggregate, not NVDA pricing.\n\nNow, should I propose a new thesis about memory? The item strongly emphasizes memory as a strategic budget variable, supply constraints. A senior analyst following NVDA would care: does memory supply constrain Vera Rubin shipments, and does memory cost pressure NVDA's margins? But this is arguably covered by clean-3? clean-3 asks whether Vera Rubin begins first shipments by end 2026. Memory supply constraint is a risk to that. So it informs clean-3.\n\nActually I think the memory story is a distinct question: \"Will memory (DRAM/NAND/HBM) supply constraints limit NVDA's Vera Rubin rack production ramp in 2H 2026/2027?\" But clean-3 is only about first shipments. The ramp is separate. Hmm.\n\nLet me be careful not to over-propose. The guidance says: don't open a thesis about a feature/price point of a product; do open product_node for main product generation reaching revenue. Clean-3 already covers Vera Rubin first shipments. Memory supply is evidence informing that (could delay shipments). I'll link memory evidenc