, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\nAnd: \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\nHmm, these two passages are about different aspects (supply constraint / pricing vs self-sourcing). Could be one item about memory supply/cost leverage, or split. I'll keep as one item about memory market tightness and its effect on rack pricing/procurement. Actually the self-sourcing $6.7M is a distinct estimate. But it's about the rack price under different procurement — related to memory cost. I'll keep together. Links: clean-3.\n\nNow, should any of these link to somewhere else or be left unlinked? The rack cost and memory cost are product economics for Vera Rubin — arguably the most directly relevant thesis is clean-3 (Vera Rubin product node). Link there.\n\nBut wait: is clean-3's question \"will Vera Rubin begin first shipments before end 2026\" really informed by the rack cost figure? The cost figure doesn't answer whether shipments begin. But it informs the product economics — the value of the product that carries NVDA's value. The relevance is \"the rack cost/pricing sets the revenue scale of the Vera Rubin product whose first shipments clean-3 tracks.\" That's a reasonable link. But one could argue the rack cost doesn't inform the shipment timing question. The instruction: \"Link one statement to every thesis it informs, and to none that its own relevance says it does not inform.\" Does rack price inform clean-3? Marginally. The pricing tells us revenue potential, not timing. Hmm.\n\nBut there's no other thesis. If I judge it material and no thesis covers it, it's kept with empty links. Is the rack cost material enough on its own to keep? Yes, it's a big figure about NVDA's product. But if no thesis covers it, empty links. \n\nLet me think: an analyst would write \"$7.8M Vera Rubin rack price vs $4M GB300, GPU $55k\" in notes because it bears on NVDA's revenue per unit and gross margin. Does any open thesis cover NVDA's revenue/margin path? clean-1 and clean-2 are credit support. clean-3 is shipment timing. clean-4 is hyperscaler capex. None about revenue/margin per product.\n\nSo the pricing/cost figures are material but no thesis covers them → keep with empty links. Hmm, but the guidance: \"A material statement that no open thesis covers and that is not itself a big matter is returned with empty links.\" The rack price is product pricing, a price point of a product — arguably not a thesis matter but material evidence. Keep with empty links? \n\nBut wait, clean-3 is the product_node thesis for Vera Rubin. The whole product economics belongs there. I lean toward linking the cost/pricing to clean-3 because clean-3 is about the Vera Rubin product line reaching revenue, and pricing/cost is part of what that revenue looks like. The product_node thesis is the natural home for product-level pricing and cost data of that node.\n\nActually, re-reading product_node definition: \"when the generation of the main product that carries the company's value reaches the step that brings revenue, usually first shipments.\" clean-3 is exactly this. Evidence about the rack's cost/price informs the scale/economics of that product node. Yes — I'll link to clean-3. That's defensible: the pricing and BOM determine the revenue and margin once shipments begin.\n\nI'll link E1, E2, E3, E4 all to clean-3.\n\nNow let me reconsider whether E2 and E4 memory items would be better as empty-link or a new thesis. I think linking to clean-3 as \"supply/cost risk to the Vera Rubin node\" is fine.\n\nActually, let me reconsider opening a shared_quantity thesis. The strongest candidate: memory contract prices for 2027. The item: \"Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply\" and memory is a strategic budget variable. But this is re