M/NAND/HBM) touches NVDA's input costs — it's genuinely part of NVDA's supply chain (memory is in its racks). So it's not merely adjacent. Kept, and it's the item's core theme actually. Good.\n\nNow, one more: The item says \"memory now accounting for 25-30% of total cost instead of 5-10%\" and \"GPU share of bill of materials fell from 63% to 51%.\" These are covered in E2.\n\nLet me also reconsider: does E2's memory cost data inform clean-1 or clean-2? No.\n\nFinal answer structure:\n\nnew_theses: []\nevidence: [E1, E2, E3, E4]\n\nLet me write summaries.\n\nE1 summary: \"An analysis by Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia's forthcoming Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers about $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, with GPUs not the main driver of the increase. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume, and notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million.\"\n\nE2 summary: \"In the same Morgan Stanley bill-of-materials analysis, memory content is estimated at about $2 million per Vera Rubin VR200 rack, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent before. Memory's share of the bill of materials rose from 5–10% on the GB200 to 25–30% on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\"\n\nE3 summary: \"The article (citing the Morgan Stanley/industry analysis) reports that first shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin platform are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\"\n\nE4 summary: \"The article attributes the rising rack price to constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, noting contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products; it says memory supply is now tight.\"\n\nGood. Original party for E4: marketscale.com (its own synthesis). Reporting speaker marketscale.com.\n\nNow let me finalize links:\nE1: [] (empty)\nE2: []\nE3: [{existing, clean-3, ...}]\nE4: [{existing, clean-3, ...}]\n\nWait—should E1 link to clean-3? No.\n\nShould E4 also link to clean-3 AND maybe nothing else. Yes.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider whether E1 (price) should really be unlinked. Is there any open thesis about NVDA revenue/margin? No. clean-1 credit support, clean-2 backstop, clean-3 shipments, clean-4 capex. None about pricing/margin. So unlinked. Good.\n\nShould E2 inform clean-3? The memory share/cost — no direct bearing on shipment timing. Unlinked. Although \"memory supply constraints\" is E4. Keep E2 unlinked.\n\nLet me reconsider E4 link to clean-3 one more time for appropriateness. clean-3 asks specifically about \"first shipments before end of calendar 2026.\" E4 says memory supply is constrained/tight. That's a risk that could delay first shipments. As evidence it makes the thesis's \"yes\" less certain. I'll keep the link with relevance explaining it's a supply-side risk, not proof of delay. Good.\n\nActually, I want to reconsider whether E4 deserves to be linked to clean-3 or whether it's better unlinked to avoid overreach. The item does not state that memory constraints will delay Vera Rubin shipments; it's a general market condition and the article's advice is that buyers should plan. Linking could be seen as inference. But the instructions allow linking evidence that informs the question even as a supply signal. The relevance field lets me distinguish source claim (memory supply tight) from inference (could risk schedule). I'll link it. This is defensible.\n\nAlternatively, keep E4 unlinked to be conservative. Hmm. The guidance says \"Link one statement to every thesis i