tem is from marketscale.com, dated 2026-07-06, titled \"Nvidia Vera Rubin rack costs $7.8M — memory is why.\"\n\nKey facts:\n- Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation.\n- The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\n- Memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump, driven by threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 TB per rack plus ~$1 million or more in 3D NAND storage.\n- First shipments are scheduled for Q3 2026, with volume ramping in Q4.\n- Memory rose from 5-10% to 25-30% of BOM, GPU's share fell from ~63% to 51%.\n- Hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring rack price down to ~$6.7 million.\n\nNow let's evaluate the open theses:\n- clean-1: credit support/guarantees to AI cloud partners. Not covered by this item.\n- clean-2: guarantee/backstop for OpenAI Ohio data center lease. Not covered.\n- clean-3: Vera Rubin first shipments before end of calendar 2026. This item says \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\" This bears on clean-3 — shipments. So evidence for clean-3.\n- clean-4: combined data center capex of big four AI hyperscalers reaching $1T in 2027. The item doesn't give the $1T figure. It's about rack costs. Not directly. Could rack cost figure bear on capex? It's a per-unit price, not total capex. I'd say not directly. Hmm, but higher rack cost could mean capex. But the thesis is about combined capex reaching $1 trillion in 2027. The item doesn't address that aggregate. So not linked.\n\nNow what material statements are there?\n\n1. Morgan Stanley estimate: Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers ~$7.8M per unit, up from ~$4M for GB300 NVL72. This is a named party's estimate (third_party_view). It's a 