ublished actual figure or completed transaction, including an announcement or completed transaction reported by an article. A shipment schedule is a forward plan, not completed. party_statement = company's guidance/management statement. If Nvidia announced Q3 2026 shipments, that's a party_statement (plan). The article reports it. I'll call original_party Nvidia, tier party_statement. Actually the text: \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\" No explicit attribution to Nvidia but it's the platform's shipment plan. Reasonable to attribute to Nvidia as the plan. But it might be Morgan Stanley's estimate. Hmm. Given ambiguity, and it's in the MS analysis paragraph, I'll say original_party = Morgan Stanley? No... \"First shipments are scheduled\" sounds like the product roadmap. I'll treat as party_statement, original_party Nvidia. Actually, safer: the article doesn't attribute; it's a statement in a Morgan Stanley-sourced article. I'll say reporting_speaker = marketscale.com, original_party = Nvidia (product shipment plan), tier = party_statement. Hmm, but if I can't confirm Nvidia said it, maybe third_party_view.\n\nLet me reconsider. clean-3 thesis outcome: \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\" The item says Q3 2026 first shipments. That directly informs clean-3. Tier — I'll pick fact? No. It's a plan/schedule. party_statement fits \"company's guidance... A forecast is a statement, not a completed outcome.\" A shipment schedule is a plan/forecast. I'll use party_statement with original_party Nvidia.\n\nHmm, but actually clean-3's period says \"End of calendar 2026, the date the item supports ('could begin shipping later this year' relative to its 2 July 2026 date).\" That prior item said \"could begin shipping later this year.\" Now this item says \"scheduled for Q3 2026, volume ramping Q4.\" So it's a more specific schedule. Good, links to clean-3.\n\nNow evidence items summary. Let me also capture:\n- Memory content per rack ~$2M, 435% jump; LPDDR5X 54 TB (threefold); 3D NAND ~$1M+; memory share 5-10%→25-30%, GPU share 63%→51%. This is BOM data (Morgan Stanley). Material - analyst would note the cost structure. But is it a business-scale material statement for Nvidia? It concerns Nvidia's product cost structure. Keep as evidence (third_party_view). Links: none (no margin thesis). Or link to clean-3? No.\n- GPU/CPU per-unit pricing: $55,000 per Rubin GPU, $5,000 per Vera CPU. Material — Nvidia's product ASPs (third_party_view estimate). Keep. Links: none.\n- Hyperscaler self-sourcing memory brings rack to ~$6.7M; procurement structure moves pricing by $1M+ per rack. Material? It's a scenario. Keep maybe merged with memory/pricing. Could keep as separate.\n- DDR5 contract pricing climbed sharply; Samsung, Apple expected to pass higher memory costs to products. This is broader market — memory price movement. It bears on Nvidia's input costs. Keep? \"a reported movement of a named price\" is kept. Keep as evidence.\n\nNow, we must avoid \"same fact said again in other words.\" The rack cost $7.8M is repeated (headline, MS estimate paragraph). The memory $2M/435% repeated. I'll consolidate.\n\nLet me now consider linking memory/rack cost statements to any thesis. clean-4 (hyperscaler capex)? The rack price affects build economics but not the aggregate figure. I'll not link.\n\nHmm, but consider: is there a real open thesis these could inform? None on input costs or margins. So empty links per rules — \"A material statement that no open thesis covers and that is not itself a big matter is returned with empty links.\"\n\nBut wait — I should reconsider whether the memory cost inflation warrants a shared_quantity thesis. Let me re-read shared_quantity: \"a quantity or price outside any one company that several companies depend on, for this or next calendar year: several companies' combined output or spending, a contract price, a