e as the company's product roadmap → original_party \"NVIDIA\", tier \"party_statement\". reporting_speaker = marketscale.com.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: The whole MS breakdown paragraph is Morgan Stanley. The shipment schedule sentence could be MS's understanding of Nvidia's roadmap. In articles about semiconductor roadmaps, \"first shipments are scheduled for Q3\" typically reflects the vendor's roadmap (Nvidia's). I'll go with Nvidia party_statement.\n\nE3: Memory content per rack ~$2M, up 435%; LPDDR5X capacity to 54 TB (threefold); ~$1M+ 3D NAND; memory share rose from 5-10% to 25-30%, GPU share fell 63%→51%. (MS third_party_view). figures: memory content $2M estimate; 3D NAND $1M+ estimate. Links empty.\n\nActually the share percentages are BOM shares — not a financial measure of Nvidia per se but cost structure. I'll describe.\n\nE4: Per-GPU $55,000 and per-Vera-CPU $5,000 pricing. (MS third_party_view). figures: $55,000 per Rubin GPU estimate; $5,000 per Vera CPU estimate. Links empty.\n\nE5: Hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring rack price down to ~$6.7M; procurement structure can move final pricing by $1M+ per rack. (MS third_party_view). figures: $6.7M estimate. Links empty. Actually this might be merged into rack cost item. But it's a distinct scenario/statement (procurement). Keep separate is fine per \"a deal... its own amount.\" But it's more of a scenario. I'll keep as separate evidence to preserve the $6.7M and $1M+ figure.\n\nE6: Contract DDR5 pricing climbed sharply; Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into products; memory supply tight. (third_party_view / reporting). Links empty. figures empty (no Nvidia figure; it's other companies). Actually \"contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply\" no figure. Keep. tier: third_party_view (journalist's/analyst). Original party? The price movement is market; could be attributed to the article (journalist). I'll set original_party = null? It's \"the force behind