\n\nNow double-check evidence tier for E1, E3, E4, E5: third_party_view (Morgan Stanley's estimates) — yes.\n\nSource passages must be verbatim contiguous. Let me extract.\n\nE1 passages:\n\"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation.\"\nAlso headline first para: \"Nvidia's next-generation Vera Rubin rack will cost hyperscalers $7.8 million—nearly double the prior generation—with memory now accounting for 25-30% of total cost instead of 5-10%.\"\nI'll use the MS paragraph as primary.\n\nE2 passage: \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\"\n\nE3 passages: \n\"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\"\n\"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n\nE4 passage: \"The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\"\n\nE5 passage: \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\nAlso \"procurement structure able to move final pricing by $1+ million per rack\" from key facts.\n\nE6 passage: \"But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n\nNow statement dates: The item is dated 2026-07-06. The MS analysis date isn't given specifically; it's reported in this article. For MS estimate, statement_date: the article's date? The rule: do not use article date as original unless statement made in originating party's own release. The MS estimate is reported by the article; we don't know when MS published. The article is 2026-07-06. I could set statement_date null and statement_date_text null. Actually the article reports MS analysis; we don't have the MS report date. So statement_date null. Hmm but commodity that the article date might not equal MS report date. I'll set null, statement_date_text null. For E2 shipment schedule, statement_date null too (no date given, roadmap). evidence_period: E2 period = \"Q3–Q4 2026 (first shipments Q3 2026, volume ramp Q4 2026)\".\n\nE1 evidence_period: the estimate is for \"current/next generation\" — the VR200 rack pricing going forward, no specific period. Maybe \"Vera Rubin generation (from Q3 2026 shipments)\" or null. I'll set \"Vera Rubin VR200 generation (upcoming)\". Hmm evidence_period should be period the fact covers. It's per-unit price of VR200 — period: \"Vera Rubin VR200 generation\". I'll put \"Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 generation (upcoming shipments from Q3 2026)\".\n\nActually let me keep evidence_period simpler: null or \"VR200 generation\". I'll say \"Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 generation\".\n\nLet me finalize figures with exact values.\n\nE1 figures:\n- measure \"Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack price per unit\", period \"Vera Rubin (VR200) generation\", value \"$7.8 million\", kind estimate, basis \"per rack; Morgan Stanley Research estimate for hyperscale cloud providers\".\n- measure \"GB300 NVL72 rack price per unit\", period \"current GB300 generation\", value \"$4 million\", kind estimate, basis \"per rack; prior generation; 'roughly'\".\n\nE3 fi