if a thesis plausibly covers it. There's genuine ambiguity. Let me reconsider clean-3's outcome: \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\" The $7.8M price is not about whether shipments start. Empty links is defensible and correct.\n\nNow, could I instead argue the pricing/memory should inform clean-3? No. Keep empty.\n\nLet me reconsider whether to open a thesis. Actually, re-examining: is there a \"shared_quantity\" thesis about memory that would be genuinely valuable and supported? The item repeatedly emphasizes memory as a strategic cost variable. But rules say a thesis about \"a financial measure's level, growth, margin or guidance for any period, on its own\" is excluded. Memory price is not Nvidia's own financial measure though. But we lack a specific forward figure/period. I'll not open. Good.\n\nActually, let me reconsider clean-4 once more. The item's doubling rack cost could be a headwind to hyperscaler capex efficiency. But clean-4 is about aggregate reaching $1T. Not evidenced here. Also \"Revisit the build-versus-rent math\" — if rack prices climb toward $8M, hyperscalers may rent rather than buy, which could reduce Nvidia GPU sales? That's speculative; item doesn't assert. Skip.\n\nNow let me write summaries.\n\nE1 summary: \"An analysis by Morgan Stanley Research, reported by MarketScale, estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation — nearly double the prior generation.\"\n\nE2 summary: \"NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform first shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter, per the MarketScale article.\"\n\nE3 summary: \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus 