th.\n\nAlright. Also should I include the headline first-paragraph statement \"memory now accounting for 25-30% of total cost instead of 5-10%\"? That's captured in E3's share sentence. Good.\n\nNow, evidence_period values:\nE1: \"Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 generation (upcoming, shipments from Q3 2026)\" — hmm mixing. I'll put \"Vera Rubin VR200 generation\". For GB300 $4M, current generation. Put \"VR200 vs current GB300 generation\".\nE2: \"Q3–Q4 2026\".\nE3: \"VR200 generation (vs prior GB200 generation)\".\nE4: \"VR200 generation\".\nE5: \"VR200 generation\".\nE6: \"as of the July 2026 article; ongoing\".\n\nLet me write the JSON now.\n\nFor links on E2: target_type existing, target_id clean-3, relevance: \"Reports Vera Rubin first shipments scheduled for Q3 2026 with volume ramping in Q4 2026, supporting the thesis that Vera Rubin reaches first shipments before end-2026.\"\n\nAll other evidence: links [].\n\nLet me double-check no new thesis required. Good.\n\nOne more: Should E1/E3/E4/E5 be linked to clean-3 anyway because they concern Vera Rubin which clean-3 tracks? The instructions: \"Do not exclude evidence solely because it concerns a related period, population or product variant. Assess whether it can inform the open question.\" Can the rack price inform \"will shipments start by end 2026?\" Marginally, high price / demand could support demand but not timing. I'll keep them unlinked but... hmm. Actually, an argument: the pricing is part of the Vera Rubin commercialization which is exactly what clean-3 tracks (the product that brings revenue). But clean-3's outcome is a discrete milestone (first shipments). The pricing doesn't inform whether that milestone occurs. I'll leave unlinked. But to hedge, maybe link E1 to clean-3 with relevance clarifying it concerns Vera Rubin economics but not timing? The instruction: link to every thesis it informs, none it doesn't. Price doesn't inform shipment timing. So don't link. Empty.\n\nBut then a lot of central figures go unlinked. That is allowed: \"A material statement that no open thesis covers and that is not itself a big matter is returned with empty links.\" These figures are material but no thesis covers them. Good.\n\nActually, wait — re-examine whether the price figures could inform clean-4 (hyperscaler capex). A doubling rack price is arguably evidence bearing on the cost of AI infrastructure that hyperscalers fund. But clean-4's outcome is combined capex = $1T in 2027. Higher per-rack cost means each capex dollar buys fewer racks; it doesn't tell us the aggregate. It doesn't inform whether the aggregate reaches $1T. So no.\n\nFinal answer.\n\n### d1-memory 3\nLet me analyze this item for NVDA evidence.\n\nThe item: Morgan Stanley bill-of-materials analysis of Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack costing ~$7.8M, up from ~$4M for GB300 NVL72. Memory ~$2M per rack, up 435%. GPUs ~$55,000 per Rubin GPU, $5,000 per Vera CPU. Memory share rose from 5-10% to 25-30%, GPU share fell from ~63% to 51%. First shipments scheduled for Q3 2026, volume ramping in Q4. Hyperscaler self-sourcing memory could bring rack price down to ~$6.7M.\n\nOpen theses:\n- clean-1: credit support committed to AI cloud partners\n- clean-2: NVIDIA financial guarantee for OpenAI Ohio lease\n- clean-3: Will Vera Rubin begin first shipments before end of calendar 2026?\n- clean-4: combined capex of big four AI hyperscalers reaching $1 trillion in 2027\n\nLet me identify material statements.\n\nStatement 1: Morgan Stanley estimates Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8M per unit, up from ~$4M for GB300 NVL72, with GPU at ~$55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU. This is a third_party_view (estimate). It relates to... Vera Rubin pricing. Does it inform clean-3? clean-3 is about first shipments. The price estimate doesn't directly bear on shipments but it's about the same product. Hmm. It also bears on demand/capex (clean-4) — a doubling of rack price affects hyperscaler cape