\n\nLet me reconsider: maybe the cleanest is to have evidence items as:\n\n1. First shipments Q3 2026, volume Q4 2026 (Morgan Stanley). → clean-3.\n2. Rack cost $7.8M vs $4M, memory 25-30%, GPU 51%, per-GPU $55k, per-CPU $5k. → clean-4? (cost driver of hyperscaler capex)\n3. Memory content $2M per rack, +435%, from LPDDR5X 54TB and $1M+ 3D NAND. → this is input cost. Could inform clean-4 too (cost driver) or left unlinked.\n4. Hyperscaler self-sourcing memory to ~$6.7M rack. → cost variable, no thesis.\n\nHmm, but I should avoid splitting the same fact into too many. The rack cost, memory cost, GPU/CPU prices are all from \"one bill-of-materials analysis.\" Are they \"one statement told in several sentences\"? They are related but distinct figures: overall rack price, memory cost, GPU price. The rules say a deal/programme told in several sentences with its terms is one evidence item; keep fuller wording. Here it's a BOM analysis with multiple figures. I think I can keep it as one item with figures list, or split into (a) overall system price and per-chip prices, and (b) memory cost detail. \n\nActually the CONDITION about figures: \"a statement of the company's own financial figures.\" These are Morgan Stanley's estimates, not the company's own. So figures list is for the company's own financial figures. Here Morgan Stanley's estimate is a third_party_view, not the company's own — so figures should probably be empty? Let me re-read.\n\n\"figures: The company's own useful financial figures this statement states (section 2, CONDITION); empty for any other statement.\"\n\nThe per-GPU price ($55,000 NVIDIA will charge) is arguably NVIDIA's pricing, an estimate by Morgan Stanley though. It's an estimate, not the company's own guidance. The CONDITION says: \"a statement of the company's own financial figures (a reported result or balance, its guidance, or a named party's or consensus estimate).\" So a named party's estimate counts as \"the company's own financial figures\