procurement-sensitivity detail with no thesis covering it.\n\nActually, reconsider: is E5 \"material\"? A scenario that self-sourcing memory cuts rack price by ~$1.1M. It's a sensitivity an analyst budgeting AI infra would note. Keep, unlinked.\n\nNow let me also double check: the article's opening line \"Nvidia's next-generation Vera Rubin rack will cost hyperscalers $7.8 million—nearly double the prior generation—with memory now accounting for 25-30% of total cost instead of 5-10%.\" This is a summary repeating E1/E2. It's the same fact said again — don't make separate item. Good, I use the detailed passages.\n\nLet me verify passages are verbatim contiguous from the text.\n\nE1 passage 1: \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation.\" — Note the text has \"Nvidia 's\" with space. I'll copy exactly: \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation.\" Good.\n\nE1 passage 2: \"The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\" Verbatim. Good.\n\nE2 passage 1: \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\" Verbatim (starts after \"The memory line is where the cost concentrates.\"). Good. I'll include as one passage starting \"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts...\" Actually I can start at \"Morgan Stanley puts memory...\" That's contiguous and fine.\n\nE2 passage 2: \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\" Verbatim. Good.\n\nE3 passage: \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\" Verbatim. Good.\n\nE4 passage 1: \"But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\" Verbatim contiguous. Good.\n\nE4 passage 2: \"With memory representing a quarter or more of rack value and supply tight, availability and lead times deserve the same planning attention as accelerator allocation.\" Verbatim. Good. (preceded by \"Treat memory supply as a scheduling risk, not only a cost.\" I can include that too: \"Treat memory supply as a scheduling risk, not only a cost. With memory representing a quarter or more of rack value and supply tight, availability and lead times deserve the same planning attention as accelerator allocation.\" Let me include both sentences.)\n\nE5 passage: \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\" Verbatim. Good.\n\nNow figures periods. Let me finalize.\n\nLet me write the JSON now.\n\nFor E1 figures basis: \"per unit rack price for hyperscale cloud providers; Morgan Stanley estimate\". For GPU \"per GPU in volume\".\n\nstatement_date null, statement_date_text null for all.\n\nLet me write.\n\nActually, for E3, original_party: the shipment schedule is NVIDIA's plan as reported by Morgan Stanley. But the item attributes to Morgan Stanley's analysis implicitly. I'll put original_party \"Morgan Stanley\". reporting_speaker \"MarketScale\"