ity to 54TB per rack plus ~$1M+ 3D NAND.\n3. First shipments scheduled for Q3 2026, with volume ramping in Q4.\n4. Memory share rose from 5-10% on GB200 to 25-30% on VR200, GPU share fell from 63% to 51%.\n5. Hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring rack price down to ~$6.7 million.\n\nNow let me match to theses:\n\n- clean-3: \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\" First shipments Q3 2026 is directly on point. Link statement 3 to clean-3.\n\n- clean-4: \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027...\" The rack price ~$7.8M up from $4M - is this about hyperscaler capex? Hmm. The rack cost affects how much capex buys. But it's not directly the combined capex figure. Actually it informs the cost side. This is a third-party estimate (Morgan Stanley) about NVIDIA's product pricing. Does it bear on hyperscaler capex? It could inform whether capex reaches $1T because higher rack prices mean more spend per rack. But it's not directly the aggregate capex. I'd consider linking maybe. Actually the thesis clean-4 is specifically about the combined data center capex of big four hyperscalers reaching $1 trillion. The rack price estimate informs what a given capex buys, i.e., demand translation. It bears on the aggregate? Marginally. Let me think - the item doesn't give hyperscaler capex figures. It's about the per-rack cost. I think it more informs demand/cost structure. But do I link? The instruction says link to every thesis it informs. The rack price is a supply-side product pricing matter. clean-4 is about aggregate capex. Higher rack prices could push capex higher (price effect) or reduce unit volumes. This is an indirect relationship. I think I could link it but weakly. Let me be careful - the relevance must be specific. I'll consider whether to link.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The core statements:\n\nStatement A: Morgan Stanley estimates VR200 NVL72 rack costs ~$7.8M, up from ~$4M GB300; GPUs $55k per Rubin GPU and $5k per Vera CPU. This is a third-party estimate about NVIDIA's product pricing. This bears on NVIDIA's revenue/product economics. Which thesis? clean-3 is about Vera Rubin first shipments. This informs the price/economics of Vera Rubin. Link to clean-3? clean-3 asks about shipments timing, not pricing. But the estimate concerns the same product. Hmm, \"market by underlying question or outcome, subject and scope.\" clean-3's question is shipments timing. Pricing is a different question. But the evidence about Vera Rubin product could inform clean-3's subject (Vera Rubin) even though the outcome is shipments. Actually clean-3 is narrowly about whether shipments begin. The pricing estimate doesn't bear on whether shipments begin, but it's about the same product. The linking should be about informing the answer to the question. Pricing doesn't inform shipment timing much. Hmm.\n\nBut wait - the item's statement about \"First shipments are scheduled for Q3 2026, with volume ramping in Q4\" directly informs clean-3. That's the key link.\n\nThe pricing estimate - does it inform clean-3? Not really the timing question. Does it inform clean-4 (capex)? The rack price affects how capex dollars translate to units. Could inform clean-4. Let me link statement A to clean-4 maybe, since higher rack costs affect the capex needed. Actually clean-4 asks whether combined capex reaches $1T. The rack price is a per-unit cost; if racks cost more, capex per unit is higher. This is relevant to whether $1T is reached because cost inflation drives capex. I'll link statement A to clean-4 with a caveat that it's per-rack cost not aggregate capex.\n\nHmm, but I should be careful not to over-link. Let me think about what a senior analyst would do. The Morgan Stanley BOM analysis is really about NVIDIA's product pricing and cost structure. For NVIDIA's revenue, the rack price matters. But clean theses:\n- clean-1: credit support to cloud partners\n- clean-2: guarantee for OpenAI Ohio lease\n- clean-3: Vera Rubin first shipments by end 2026\n- clean-4: hyperscaler combined capex $1T in 2027\n\nThe rack price estimate is about NVIDIA's product economics but none of these theses is directly about NVIDIA's ASP/revenue per rack. Is there a thesis about revenue? No. So the pricing estimate that doesn't fit any thesis... would be a \"NO THESIS YET\" statement kept with empty links, unless it's big enough to merit a new thesis.\n\nCould I propose a new thesis? A thesis is a question worth a card. What question does the rack price raise? \"Will the Vera Rubin rack price / NVIDIA's per-system ASP exceed $X?\" That's a financial measure's level - \"do not open thesis about a financial measure's level, growth, margin or guidance for any period, on its own.\" So no new thesis for the price level.\n\nThe \"first shipments Q3 2026\" - covered by clean-3.\n\nMemory cost, memory share - these are cost structure / spec details. The memory cost 435% jump, $2M per rack. These inform NVIDIA's BOM cost. But NVIDIA buys memory; this is input cost. Higher memory cost could squeeze NVIDIA margin or raise rack price. But margin is a financial measure - do not open a thesis. Is there a thesis about input costs/supply? No. So kept as evidence with empty links, if material.\n\nActually wait - is the memory cost statement about NVIDIA's input cost? Memory is a component NVIDIA buys (or its ODM buys) to put into racks. So 435% memory cost increase affects NVIDIA's COGS and pricing. This bears on NVIDIA's profitability. But no thesis covers profitability. So kept empty.\n\nThe \"self-sourcing brings price down to $6.7M\" - procurement detail. Same.\n\nLet me now decide evidence items and tiers.\n\nThe Morgan Stanley estimates are third_party_view (analyst estimate). The \"First shipments are scheduled for Q3 2026\" - Morgan Stanley's/indus