egins first shipments before end of calendar 2026.\"\n\nGood.\n\nNow, is there any statement I should add? The \"Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply... Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs\" — a reported movement of a named price (DDR5) and industry dynamic. Could be evidence with empty links. Should I include it? It's material context about memory input cost/inflation. It's a movement of a named price. Keep? It informs memory supply/cost. But it's \"the same fact said again\" partly — the memory cost inflation is already captured in E2 (435% jump). This DDR5 sentence adds the industry-wide angle (Samsung, Apple). It's somewhat background/general. I could include as a separate evidence item with empty links. But is it new/material beyond E2? It adds that the memory squeeze is industry-wide and other device makers passing costs. For NVDA, that's context on input cost persistence. Marginal. I'll skip it to avoid general background (it's a \"general statement... supply tight\" plus a movement). Actually \"Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply\" is a reported movement of a named price, which the closing check keeps. But it's not quantified and it's about memory broadly, informing the same memory-cost story as E2. I'll omit it — E2 covers the memory cost concretely, and this sentence is generic industry context. Keeping it would be near-duplicate. I'll leave it out.\n\nAlso there are the appended unrelated stories (Uber, PHP, cooling) — not about NVDA, drop. Good.\n\nNow, let me reconsider if E3 tier might better be \"fact\" because it's a scheduled plan reported as fact. The tier guidance: \"party_statement: a company's guidance, management statement... A forecast is a statement.\" Here the schedule is not attributed to NVIDIA's own statement in this item; it's part of the Morgan Stanley analysis. So third_party_view. OK.\n\nAlright, also reconsider: Should E1 be split so that the GPU/CPU unit prices are separate from total rack price? They'r