sis. Fine.\n\nNow let me reconsider whether E2 (memory composition) and E1 (rack price) and E4 (memory supply) risk being flagged as \"the same fact said again.\" They are distinct: total price, memory's share/cost, and memory supply tightness. I'll keep them.\n\nLet me reconsider once more whether I'm over-extracting. The article is essentially one Morgan Stanley BOM analysis. The distinct material statements:\n- Rack price $7.8M vs $4M; chip prices. (E1)\n- Memory content $2M, 435%, LPDDR5X 54TB, NAND $1M+; memory share 25-30% vs 5-10%; GPU share 63%→51%. (E2)\n- Shipment schedule Q3/Q4 2026. (E3) ← directly informs clean-3.\n- Memory supply tight, DDR5 pricing up sharply, scheduling risk. (E4)\n- Self-sourcing memory → $6.7M. (part of E1)\n\nI think 4 items is reasonable. \n\nNow links:\n\nE1 → clean-3 (per-unit price/scale of the Vera Rubin product whose shipments the thesis tracks; establishes revenue magnitude per rack at launch). Also → clean-4? Let me decide. clean-4 is about hyperscaler capex $1T 2027. The rising rack price ($7.8M vs $4M) is directly relevant to how much capex hyperscalers must spend to deploy AI capacity — it's part of the mechanism pushing the capex bill up. I'll link E1 to clean-4 with relevance \"inference: higher per-rack system cost raises the capex hyperscalers need per rack, bearing on the $1T capex aggregate.\" That's a legitimate connection.\n\nActually, let me reconsider clean-4. Its description: \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\" So it's about the aggregate capex figure. The rack cost is a per-unit price, not the aggregate. But the article explicitly frames it as \"AI infrastructure budgeting must focus on total system cost.\" Higher per-rack cost contributes to the aggregate. I think a light link is defensible. I'll include it but keep relevance focused