st's stated schedule). Hmm, but a shipment schedule could be a fact about the plan. The tier order: rumour (no), fact (an official action/completed... this is future plan, not completed), party_statement (company's guidance/statement — this is not clearly the company's), third_party_view. Third_party_view fits.\n- E4: third_party_view (article/Morgan Stanley characterization of market).\n\nstatement_date for all: null (no full day; the analysis is reported 2026-07-06 but the original statement date unknown). statement_date_text: null.\n\nCould I use \"first half of 2026\" — article says \"budgeting AI infrastructure for the second half of 2026 and beyond.\" Not a statement date.\n\nevidence_period:\n- E1: \"Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 generation; current GB300 NVL72 for comparison\" — I'll write \"Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 generation (with current GB300 NVL72 as prior generation).\"\n- E2: same product generation.\n- E3: \"Third quarter 2026 (first shipments); fourth quarter 2026 (volume ramp).\"\n- E4: \"2026 and beyond (current market).\"\n\nNow source_passages: verbatim contiguous from text.\n\nFor E1: \n\"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\"\nNote the text has \"Nvidia 's\" with a space — I'll copy verbatim including that. And the self-sourcing passage: \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\" I'll include as a second passage if I include that figure. Let me include it since I mention $6.7M.\n\nFor E2:\n\"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\"\nAnd share passage: \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n\nFor E3:\n\"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\"\n\nFor E4:\n\"Only a handful of companies buy racks at this scale. But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products. The AI buildout and the price of the memory in a laptop are now tied to the same supply squeeze.\"\nPlus scheduling risk passage: \"Treat memory supply as a scheduling risk, not only a cost. With memory representing a quarter or more of rack value and supply tight, availability and lead times deserve the same planning attention as accelerator allocation.\"\n\nGood. These support E4.\n\nNow let me reconsider — is E2's \"GPU share fell from 63% to 51%\" and \"memory rose to 25-30%\" a slightly different claim than \"memory content $2M\"? Both about memory cost. Merging is fine as one evidence item about BOM composition.\n\nBut wait — one evidence item records one statement. Am I merging two statements (memory absolute cost + share)? They're closely related BOM composition facts from the same analysis. I'll merge as \"BOM composition\" — acceptable per \"a measure told in several sentences... is one evidence item; keep the fuller wording.\" These are the fuller wording of the memory-cost story.\n\nLet me reconsider: is E1 the rack total price AND chip prices one statement? Yes, same MS estimate of pricing.\n\nNow, one more: should E1 and E2 merge? E1 total price, E2 memory detail. They're both BOM. But E1 is total + chip prices, E2 is memory detail. Could be one big \"Morgan Stanley BOM analysis\" item. But that's a lot. I'll keep separate as they're distinct measures (total/chip vs memory). Actually merging E1 (which includes chip prices) and E2 (memory) would be \"the full BOM.\" Hmm. The guidance allows keeping the fuller wording. But I'll keep two items: one for rack total + chip ASPs, one for memory composition. Reasonable granularity.\n\nNow let me write summaries carefully, naming actor and attribution.\n\nE1 summary: \"According to a Morgan Stanley Research bill-of-materials analysis cited by MarketScale, Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, with Nvidia estimated to charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume; the analysis notes a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could lower the rack price to around $6.7 million.\"\n\nE2 summary: \"Morgan Stanley's analysis puts memory content for Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack at about $2 million per rack, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory's share of the bill of materials rose from 5–10% on the GB200 to 25–30% on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\"\n\nE3 summary: \"The Morgan Stanley-cited analysis states that first shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\"\n\nE4 summary: \"The article reports constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, noting contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, tying AI buildout demand and laptop memory prices to the same supply squeeze; it flags memory supply tightness as a scheduling risk for rack availability and lead times alongside accelerator allocation.\"\n\nLinks with relevance strings.\n\nE1 links:\n- clean-3: \"Establishes the per-unit price/scale of the Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 product whose first shipments the thesis tracks; Morgan Stanley estimate, not company guidance.\"\n- clean-4: \"Inference: a near-doubling of per-rack system cost ($7.8M vs $4M) raises the capex hyperscalers must spend per rack, bearing on the aggregate AI capex the thesis tracks.\"\n\nE2 links:\n- clean-3: \"Details the bill-of-materials cost structure (memory ~$2M, 25–30% of the rack) of the Vera Rubin product whose launch the thesis tracks; estimate, not company figure.\"\n\nE3 links:\n- clean-3: \"Directly supports: first shipments scheduled Q3 2026, volume Q4 2026 — within the thesis's end-of-2026 window.\"\n\nE4 links:\n- clean-3: \"Tight DRAM/NAND/HBM supply and sharply higher contract DDR5 pricing are flagged as a scheduling risk to rack availability, bearing on whether Vera Rubin shipments stay on the Q3/Q4 2026 schedule.\"\n\nNow, do any of these evidence items belong to clean-4 more than clean-3? E1 to both. Fine.\n\nNo new theses.\n\nLet me double-check I'm not opening a thesis about memory prices — I decided not to. Good.\n\nAlso double-check: no figure field needed. I'll return figures: [] for each.\n\nLet me reconsider evidence_period values to be clear.\n\nE1: period described = Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 generation (future), comparison GB300 NVL72 (current). evidence_period: \"Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 generation; comparison to current GB300 NVL72\".\n\nE2: \"Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 generation; memory share vs prior GB200\".\n\nE3: \"Q3 2026 (first shipments); Q4 2026 (volume ramp)\".\n\nE4: \"2026 (current memory market conditions)\".\n\nNow write JSON.\n\nOne concern: For E1, the passage includes \"Nvidia 's\" — I must copy verbatim. The raw text: \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation.\" Yes there's a space before 's. I'll preserve.\n\nLet me also make sure to include the self-sourcing sentence as a separate passage for E1. The passage: \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\" I'll add as second passage.\n\nGood. Final answer.\n\nLet me reconsider count and whether E2 and E3 both needed and E4. I'll produce 4 evidence items.\n\nActually, reconsider whether E2 (memory BOM composition) is worth keeping given it's fairly granular and mainly supports E1's story. But the memory share is a distinctive data point the article emphasizes (\"Memory is why\"). I'll keep it.\n\nAlso the \"budget for the full system rather than the chip\" — that's advice/opinion, not kept.\n\nAlright, write it.\n\n### d1-memory 16\nLet me analyze the source item.\n\nThe item is about NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack costs, per a Morgan Stanley bill-of-materials analysis. Key facts:\n\n1. Morgan Stanley estimates Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers ~$7.8 million per unit, up from ~$4 million for current GB300 NVL72. GPUs not the driver. Bank estimates NVIDIA charges ~$55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU in volume. → This is a third_party_view (analyst estimate). Material. Links to: clean-3 (Vera Rubin first shipments)? It's about pricing of the next-gen platform. Also clean-4 (hyperscaler capex)? The rack price bears on hyperscaler capex per unit. Hmm.\n\n2. Memory content per rack ~$2 million, a 435% jump from prior generation, driven by threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 TB per rack plus ~$1 million or more in 3D NAND. First shipments scheduled for Q3 2026, with volume ramping in Q4. → Material. This directly informs clean-3 (Vera Rubin first shipments before end of 2026) — Q3 2026 first shipments, Q4 ramp. Yes, link to clean-3.\n\n3. Memory share of BOM rose from 5-10% on GB200 to 25-30% on VR200, GPU share fell from ~63% to 51%. → Material, competitor/supplier-ish, it's Morgan Stanley estimate. Informs clean-3 (product specs/economics of Vera Rubin) and clean-4 (capex). Actually cost structure informs demand and hyperscaler capex.\n\n4. Constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, HBM; contract DDR5 pricing climbed sharply; Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs. → This is a general statement about memory supply tightness. The row says \"a general statement that demand stays strong or a product stays popular is not one\" but \"a reported movement of a named price...even without a figure\" is kept. \"Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply\" - reported movement of a named price. Kept. But is it material to NVIDIA? It bears on input costs. Does any thesis cover it? clean-3 potentially (Vera Rubin product economics). Hmm, it's about memory supply constraints affecting NVIDIA's rack costs. This could be a shared_quantity thesis? \"next year's average contract price of a raw material\" - memory prices. But this is a reported movement, not a forecast of a specific period. Could there be a new thesis about memory supply constraints affecting NVIDIA? Actually a shared_quantity thesis: contract DRAM/NAND prices for 2026-2027. Hmm. But careful not to over-propose.\n\nLet me think about what theses to propose.\n\nClean-3 is about Vera Rubin first shipments. The item says first shipments Q3 2026, volume ramp Q4. That directly informs clean-3. Good.\n\nClean-4 is about combined hyperscaler data center capex reaching $1 trillion in 2027. The rack price ($7.8M vs $4M) bears on the capex math — higher per-unit cost could mean capex per rack higher, informing the $1T projection. Link.\n\nClean-1 and clean-2 are about credit support/guarantees — not addressed by this item. No link.\n\nNow new theses. The item raises: memory cost as a share of AI infrastructure cost. Could propose a shared_quantity thesis about memory prices? The rule: shared_quantity is \"a quantity or price outside any one company that several companies depend on, for this or next calendar year: ... a contract price.\" Memory/DRAM contract pricing — several companies depend on it. But does the item support a release date and period? \"Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply\" — no figure. Hmm. This is more of an input cost matter.\n\nActually, is there a thesis worth proposing about Vera Rubin rack cost/economics? Clean-3 already covers Vera Rubin first shipments but not cost. The question of \"what will the Vera Rubin rack cost per unit / will memory cost share...\" — hmm. But \"a financial measure's level, growth, margin or guidance\" don't open a thesis. Rack cost is essentially a price point / product economics. The do-not-open list: \"a feature, content or software update, price point, regional service or variant of a product already on sale.\" Vera Rubin is not yet on sale though. But rack cost is a price point.\n\nLet me reconsider. The item is essentially a Morgan Stanley BOM analysis estimating the VR200 rack price. The core questions:\n- Does Vera Rubin ship in 2026 (clean-3)?\n- Hyperscaler capex reaching $1T (clean-4)?\n\nI think the main material statements are:\nA. Morgan Stanley estimate: VR200 NVL72 rack ~$7.8M, up from ~$4M; Rubin GPU $55K, Vera CPU $5K. → link to clean-3 (product pricing/ramp) and maybe clean-4 (capex). Actually this is a price point estimate, but it's a named party's estimate with figures — kind of. Is it \"another company's matter\"? It's NVIDIA's own product pricing estimated by an analyst. Kept as third_party_view. Link to clean-3 and clean-4.\n\nB. Memory content ~$2M/rack, +435%, LPDDR5X 54TB, $1M+ 3D NAND; first shipments Q3 2026, volume Q4. → link clean-3 (shipment timing) and possibly a memory supply thesis.\n\nC. Memory BOM share 25-30% vs 5-10%; GPU share 63%→51%. → link clean-3.\n\nD. DRAM/NAND/HBM supply constrained, contract DDR5 pricing climbed sharply; Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher costs. → shared_quantity memory price thesis? Or link to clean-3? Hmm.\n\nLet me think about whether to propose a new thesis. The candidate: \"Will contract prices for DRAM/NAND/HBM (memory) continue rising / what will they be in 2026-2027?\" shared_quantity. But the item gives no figure and no release date for a specific period. It says contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply. A shared_quantity needs to be \"for this or next calendar year.\" It's a bit thin. Also the movement is not a forecast with a specific period.\n\nAlternatively propose a new thesis about NVIDIA's Vera Rubin rack cost and gross margin? No, that's a financial measure.\n\nHmm. Actually let me reconsider the strongest new thesis candidate: memory as an input cost constraint on NVIDIA's AI systems. But that's really about the industry-wide memory supply squeeze. Given the do-not-open guidance is strict, and the closest open thesis is clean-3 (Vera Rubin shipments), I think memory supply tightness could inform clean-3's outcome (could it delay shipments? raise costs?). I'll link it to clean-3 as input-cost/scheduling risk evidence.\n\nBut could it move independently? The timing of Vera Rubin shipments vs memory price levels can move apart. But is memory price a thesis an analyst would bet separately for NVIDIA? It's a shared quantity that many companies depend on. Hmm, this is borderline.\n\nLet me consider the instruction: \"Open only matters an analyst would bet on separately.\" Memory contract pricing is a genuine shared_quantity — the item talks about contract DDR5 pricing climbing and m