-10%), GPU share 63%→51% (cost structure).\n- Item C: First shipments Q3 2026 / volume Q4 (milestone) → clean-3.\n\nThat's clean. I'll fold BOM share into B.\n\nNow do I keep the DDR5 statement? Let me decide: Drop it. Reasons: it's about consumer memory (laptop DDR5, Samsung/Apple) and the broader market; it repeats the memory tightness theme already captured by the memory cost item; it doesn't give a specific figure tied to NVIDIA. The connection to NVIDIA is only via \"same supply squeeze,\" which the item states but doesn't quantify for NVIDIA. I'll drop as general supply-tightness / other companies' products. Actually... the item explicitly ties it: \"The AI buildout and the price of the memory in a laptop are now tied to the same supply squeeze.\" That's a characterisation, not a new figure for NVIDIA. Drop.\n\nWait, but \"Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply\" is a named price movement. Per checklist, reported movement of a named price is kept. But kept where? For whom? For NVIDIA, DDR5 isn't a major input (it uses LPDDR5X/HBM). DDR5 is more server DRAM. Actually NVIDIA's Grace/rack could use LPDDR5X (mobile variant) — 54TB LPDDR5X per rack. DDR5 is different. The relevance to NVIDIA is thin. And it's about the memory market broadly. I'll drop it as \"another company's matter / general supply tightness that touches NVIDIA only tangentially.\" \n\nActually, I realize I should think about whether memory cost/input costs constitute a legitimate material evidence item for NVIDIA at all. The memory content cost ($2M/rack) is a MAJOR input cost for the product NVIDIA sells. Even though NVIDIA charges $7.8M, the $2M memory cost affects its gross margin if NVIDIA bears memory price risk, or its ASP if passed through. So it's material for NVIDIA's profitability. Yes, keep.\n\nOK let me also reconsider whether the rack cost item should be linked to clean-3 given clean-3 says \"the product line that carries its value.\" Actually clean-3's outcome is only shipment timing. I'll keep rack cost empty.\n\nBut wait — should I link the rack cost to clean-4 more seriously? Let me re-read clean-4: \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\" The rack cost is the per-unit price hyperscalers pay. Higher per-rack cost → higher capex for a given number of racks. But that's a different driver than what the item discusses. The item doesn't say capex will be higher because racks cost more; it says buyers must budget for total system cost. Actually the title \"What Infrastructure Buyers Should Budget For\" speaks to capex budgets. Hmm. A $7.8M rack vs $4M means to deploy the same compute, capex is different. I think there's a weak but real connection: the item informs how capex translates to NVIDIA product spend. But clean-4's outcome (whether total capex hits $1T) is not answered by per-rack cost. I'll leave it out to respect \"narrowest relevant thesis\" and \"no thesis its own relevance says it does not inform.\"\n\nNow, an important consideration: The instructions say material statements with no covering thesis and not themselves a big matter should be returned with empty links and kept. Good — Items A and B get empty links.\n\nBut wait: \"A material statement that no open thesis covers and that is not itself a big matter (section 3) is returned with empty links.\" So Items A and B: empty links, kept. Good.\n\nNow let me reconsider if Item A (rack cost + GPU pricing) is \"big enough\" — it's an estimate of NVIDIA's flagship product price, ~$55k/GPU. Under CONDITION, it's a named party's estimate (estimate) with useful figures that can change revenue expectations. So keep it with figures. Good.\n\nLet me draft the evidence items with figures.\n\nEvidence A: Rack cost & ASp.\nsummary: \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\"\nsource_passages: \n- \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation.\"\n- \"The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\"\n(Note the item has \"Nvidia 's\" with a space — I must copy verbatim. The passage is \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\" I'll copy contiguous. Actually two sentences separated by \"The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase.\" I can include that or split passages. Since passages must be contiguous verbatim, I'll take the first sentence as one passage and the last sentence as another.)\n\nfigures:\n- measure: \"Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack cost per unit\", period: null (or \"VR200 generation\"), value: \"$7.8 million\", kind: estimate, basis: \"Morgan Stanley Research estimate; hyperscale cloud providers; VR200 vs GB300\"\n- measure: \"GB300 NVL72 rack cost per unit\", value: \"roughly $4 million\", kind: estimate, basis: \"current generation\"\n- measure: \"Price per Rubin GPU (volume)\", value: \"$55,000\", kind: estimate, basis: \"sold in volume\"\n- measure: \"Price per Vera CPU (volume)\", value: \"$5,000\", kind: estimate, basis: \"sold in volume\"\n\ntier: third_party_view. statement_date: null (item date 2026-07-06 but Morgan Stanley estimate date unknown). statement_date_text: null. evidence_period: \"VR200 generation (vs current GB300 generation)\" or null.\n\noriginal_party: \"Morgan Stanley Research\". reporting_speaker: \"MarketScale\" (the outlet reporting). Actually reporting_speaker should be the person/outlet making or reporting the statement. The item is by MarketScale. original_party = Morgan Stanley Research (whose estimate). reporting_speaker = MarketScale.\n\nEvidence B: Memory content & BOM share.\nsummary: \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content at about $2 million per Vera Rubin rack, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage. As a share of bill of materials, memory rose from 5–10% on the GB200 to 25–30% on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\"\nsource_passages:\n- \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\"\n- \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\nfigures:\n- measure: \"memory content per Vera Rubin rack\", value: \"about $2 million\", kind: estimate, basis: \"Morgan Stanley; per rack\"\n- measure: \"memory content increase vs prior generation\", value: \"435%\", kind: estimate\n- measure: \"LPDDR5X capacity per rack\", value: \"54 terabytes\", kind: estimate\n- measure: \"3D NAND storage per rack\", value: \"roughly $1 million or more\", kind: estimate\n- measure: \"memory share of bill of materials\", value: \"25 to 30 percent\", kind: estimate, period \"VR200\" vs \"5 to 10 percent\" GB200\n- measure: \"GPU share of bill of materials\", value: \"51 percent\", vs 63% GB200\nHmm many figures. I'll list key ones. Maybe keep it manageable: memory content $2M, +435%, memory share 25-30%, GPU share fell to 51%. I'll list a few.\n\ntier: third_party_view. dates null. evidence_period: \"VR200 generation vs GB200/GB300\".\n\nEvidence C: First shipments schedule.\nsummary: \"Morgan Stanley indicates that first shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin platform are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\"\nsource_passages: \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\"\ntier: third_party_view. statement_date null. evidence_period: \"Q3–Q4 2026\".\nlinks: clean-3, relevance: \"Evidence on whether/when Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (the step that brings revenue); the schedule points to Q3 2026 first shipments, consistent with the thesis that first shipments occur before end of calendar 2026, though it is a Morgan Stanley expectation, not confirmed shipments.\"\n\nGood. Now do Items A and B have any link? Let me reconsider linking memory cost to clean-4 or clean-3. No. Empty links.\n\nHold on — should Item A (rack price) link to clean-3 because clean-3 involves \"the product line that carries its value\" and the pricing is evidence of the product's commercialization? No, clean-3's outcome is purely shipment timing. Keep empty.\n\nNow, is there truly no thesis for the rack price? What about clean-1 or clean-2 (credit support / guarantees)? No, unrelated.\n\nSo final: no new theses; evidence = 3 items, one linked to clean-3, two empty.\n\nWait, but I should double-check: does the memory cost / GPU pricing relate to clean-4 (capex)? Let me think once more if a senior analyst would link the \"$7.8M rack, memory is now 25-30% of cost\" to the hyperscaler capex thesis. clean-4 is about the TOTAL capex reaching $1T. The rack cost doesn't answer that; it's about composition. I'll keep it out.\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether