n volume.\"\n- \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n\nFigures: rack $7.8M, GB300 $4M, GPU $55k, CPU $5k, self-source $6.7M.\n\nOK. Now evidence B (memory).\n\nLet me finalize.\n\nFor links: A → empty; B → empty; C → clean-3.\n\nWait, should B (memory cost) link to anything? It's about input cost / BOM. No open thesis on margins/input costs. Empty. But actually, is B material enough? The memory being 25-30% of rack cost and rising—this could affect NVIDIA's gross margin if it bears memory costs, or it's just pass-through in the ASP. It's a cost-structure data point about NVIDIA's flagship product. I'll keep it, empty.\n\nLet me reconsider — should A or B link to clean-3? I keep concluding no. Fine.\n\nNow, one more: Could the whole \"rack cost / memory\" be considered \"product specification with no business scale\" that should be dropped? No — $7.8M rack price and $2M memory cost are business-scale figures, not benchmark scores. Keep.\n\nCould the GPU/CPU prices be \"a comparison the company... publishes\"? No, it's Morgan Stanley's estimate. Keep.\n\nAlright. Let me also reconsider whether to open a new thesis around \"NVIDIA's ASP/pricing for Vera Rubin\" — not among four reasons. Skip.\n\nShould I open a thesis about memory input cost being a shared quantity (DRAM/NAND contract prices several companies depend on)? The item doesn't provide a specific aggregate price/period to check. It's vague (\"climbed sharply\"). Can't set settled_by date/quantity. Skip.\n\nFinal answer: new_theses = [], evidence = [A, B, C].\n\nWait — let me reconsider whether clean-3 is the right and only link for C. C is the shipment schedule. Yes clean-3.\n\nLet me reconsider the tier of C. \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026\" — attributed to Morgan Stanley's analysis. third_party_view. Yes.\n\nActually, could \"scheduled\" imply NVIDIA's official schedule → fact? The context: \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content... First shipments are scheduled for...\" It's within the MS analysis paragraph. I'll treat as third_party_view (analyst estimate). Reasonable.\n\nNow writing figures for B. Let me include: memory content $2M per rack (estimate), 435% jump (estimate), 54 TB LPDDR5X (estimate), memory share 25-30% VR200 vs 5-10% GB200 (estimate), GPU share 51% vs 63% (estimate). \n\nLet me write final JSON.\n\nFor evidence_period of A: \"VR200 generation (vs current GB300 generation)\". For B: \"VR200 generation vs GB200\". For C: \"Q3–Q4 2026\".\n\nstatement_date: null for all (no specific original date; item dated 2026-07-06 but that's publication, not statement date). Actually the Morgan Stanley report date isn't given. So null. statement_date_text: null.\n\noriginal_party for all three: \"Morgan Stanley Research\" (for C, the schedule is MS's). reporting_speaker: \"MarketScale\".\n\nLet me write it.\n\nFor clean-3 relevance: clarify it's a Morgan Stanley expectation pointing to Q3 2026 first shipments, bearing on the \"before end of calendar 2026\" question but not confirming shipments occurred.\n\nLet me now produce JSON.\n\n### d1-memory 13\nLet me analyze the item and extract material statements.\n\nThe item is about NVIDIA's Vera Rubin rack costs, based on Morgan Stanley bill-of-materials analysis. Let me identify material statements.\n\nKey facts:\n1. Morgan Stanley estimates Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers ~$7.8 million per unit, up from ~$4 million for GB300 NVL72. Morgan Stanley estimates Nvidia will charge ~$55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU in volume.\n2. Memory content per rack ~$2 million, 435% jump, driven by threefold increase in LPDDR5X to 54TB per rack plus ~$1M+ 3D NAND storage. First shipments scheduled Q3 2026, volume ramping Q4.\n3. Memory share of BOM rose from 5-10% on GB200 to 25-30% on VR200; GPU share fell from ~63% to 51%.\n4. Hyperscaler self-sourcing memory could bring rack price to ~$6.7 million.\n5. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply; Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products — but this is more about memory markets, not directly NVIDIA.\n6. The Tom's Hardware / Wccftech etc. are just links to other articles, not statements.\n\nNow the material statements relative to the company (NVDA):\n\nStatement A: Morgan Stanley estimate that VR200 NVL72 rack costs ~$7.8M per unit, up from ~$4M, with per-GPU ~$55k and per-CPU ~$5k pricing estimates. This is a third_party_view (Morgan Stanley analyst estimate). It bears on NVIDIA's revenue/pricing per unit. But is it the company's own financial figures? No, NVIDIA isn't stating it. This is a third-party estimate about NVIDIA's pricing. It bears on the demand/revenue path. Which thesis? clean-3 is about Vera Rubin first shipments. This pricing estimate informs clean-3 (Vera Rubin reaching first shipments — the product node). Actually the pricing/rack specifics inform the product thesis and also potentially demand. Hmm.\n\nWait, clean-3 asks whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments by end 2026. The item says first shipments scheduled for Q3 2026, volume ramping Q4. That's evidence for clean-3.\n\nAlso the pricing per GPU ($55k) is a third-party estimate — informs the revenue path. But is there an open thesis about NVIDIA revenue/earnings path? Not explicitly. clean-4 is about hyperscaler capex. The rack cost being roughly double bears on hyperscaler capex budget (how much they spend). Actually clean-4 is about combined data center capex of big four hyperscalers reaching $1 trillion in 2027. The rack price of $7.8M bears on how much capex translates to racks, but it's more about unit economics. Hmm.\n\nLet me think about what statements to keep.\n\nStatement 1: Morgan Stanley estimates VR200 NVL72 rack will cost ~$7.8M per unit up from ~$4M for GB300 NVL72, with ~$55k per Rubin GPU and ~$5k per Vera CPU in volume. Tier: third_party_view. This informs clean-3 (Vera Rubin product node) and possibly clean-4 (hyperscaler capex — the cost per rack affects how much capex is spent on GPU infrastructure). Actually the cost of the rack directly bears on the demand/capex question. But clean-4 is specifically about the combined capex reaching $1 trillion. The rack price informs how much of that spend goes to NVIDIA, but not whether capex hits $1T. Hmm. I'd link to clean-3 primarily.\n\nStatement 2: Memory content per rack ~$2M, 435% jump from prior gen, driven by threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54TB plus ~$1M+ 3D NAND storage. Tier third_party_view. This is about memory cost, which reduces NVIDIA's GPU share of BOM but memory cost is passed through? Actually this is a cost of the system, not NVIDIA's revenue. This informs... the product economics. Does it inform clean-3? Somewhat, as it's about Vera Rubin configuration/cost. But the memory cost is a component cost — reinforces total system cost. Could inform the demand thesis (clean-4) — higher system cost affects capex. Hmm.\n\nStatement 3: First shipments scheduled Q3 2026, volume ramping Q4. Tier: this is Morgan Stanley's estimate/report of schedule; actually \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026\" — attributed to Morgan Stanley estimate. Could be considered fact or party_statement. It's a schedule from analyst. I'd call third_party_view. But it bears directly on clean-3 (Vera Rubin first shipments by end 2026). This is key evidence.\n\nStatement 4: GPU share of BOM fell from 63% to 51%, memory rose from 5-10% to 25-30%. This is the same fact cluster as statement 2 about BOM composition. Might be same evidence item or separate. It's about NVIDIA's GPU value share. Informs product economics.\n\nStatement 5: Hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring rack price down to ~$6.7M. Third_party_view. Informs clean-3/product economics or demand.\n\nNow, let me decide how to structure evidence items. One statement per item, but a single coherent statement told in several sentences is one item. \n\nI think:\n- Evidence A: The rack price estimate ($7.8M vs $4M, $55k/GPU, $5k/CPU) — third_party_view. Links: clean-3 (Vera Rubin product node — pricing of the product), maybe clean-4.\n- Evidence B: Memory cost/BOM composition ($2M, 435%, LPDDR5X 54TB, GPU share 63→51%, memory 5-10→25-30%) — third_party_view. This is about the rack cost structure. \n- Evidence C: First shipments Q3 2026, volume Q4 — links clean-3.\n\nActually wait — Evidence A and B are both about the VR200 rack BOM from Morgan Stanley. But they are distinct claims: overall price vs memory composition. I could keep them separate. Let me keep the overall price as one, and memory composition as one.\n\nNow clean-4: hyperscaler capex. The rack cost being ~$8M and memory being a large share could inform the capex question because higher rack prices mean more spend per unit. But clean-4 is about total capex reaching $1T. The rack price is a component. I think the rack cost does bear on clean-4 indirectly (higher per-rack cost means capex buys fewer racks, or drives capex higher). Actually it could inform clean-4: the price of the compute infrastructure that hyperscalers buy. I'll link the rack price to clean-4 as well? Hmm, but the connection is weak. Let me consider whether it \"informs\" clean-4's question. clean-4 asks whether combined capex reaches $1T. The rack cost is one input to capex budget. An analyst tracking capex would note the higher rack prices. I think it's reasonable to link to clean-4 with a note that higher per-rack cost affects the translation of capex into NVIDIA compute. But careful not to over-link.\n\nActually let me reconsider. The relevance to clean-4: capex is what hyperscalers spend; NVIDIA's revenue depends on it. The $7.8M rack price is what each rack costs hyperscalers. This does bear on the capex figure and NVIDIA revenue per rack. I'll link A to clean-4 as well as clean-3.\n\nNow, is there a new thesis? The pricing per GPU ($55k) is an estimate. clean-3 covers the product node (first shipments). The rack pricing itself could be within the product node thesis. No new thesis needed for pricing — it's an estimate/forecast, and financial measure forecasts are not opened as theses. \n\nThe DDR5 contract pricing climbing and Samsung/Apple passing memory costs — that's about memory market, not NVIDIA's own market directly, though it's the input cost squeeze. Does it bear on NVIDIA? Memory supply constraints affect NVIDIA's ability to ship racks (scheduling risk). But this is a general statement about memory cost. Could inform a supply thesis, but no open thesis about memory supply. Is it big enough for a new thesis? It's a shared quantity? \"contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply\" — that's a price movement, could be a shared_quantity thesis. But that's about the memory market, not really NVIDIA-specific. Hmm. Actually the memory supply constraint for NVIDIA racks is relevant — it affects NVIDIA's supply/ability to ship Vera Rubin. But this is more of a general market observation. \n\nLet me think: is there a material statement about memory supply being tight that warrants a thesis? \"memory supply constraints and tight availability now warrant the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation\" and \"constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand.\" This is a shared_quantity / supply issue. Would an analyst bet separately on \"will memory (HBM/DRAM/NAND) supply constraints limit NVIDIA's Vera Rubin rack shipments in 2026?\" That's a real question, but the item doesn't give a specific quantity or price for a shared measure beyond \"435% jump\" and \"contract DDR5 pricing climbed sharply.\" The 435% is memory content per rack cost. Hmm.\n\nActually, the instruction says a shared_quantity thesis needs a quantity or price outside any one comp