ory costs into upcoming products. The AI buildout and the price of the memory in a laptop are now tied to the same supply squeeze.\" Yes matches.\n\nGood. Now write final.\n\nFor E1 reporting_speaker \"MarketScale\". original_party \"Morgan Stanley Research\".\n\nE2, E3, E4, E6 original_party \"Morgan Stanley Research\". E5 \"MarketScale\".\n\nLet me finalize relevance text.\n\nE1 → clean-4: \"Morgan Stanley's per-unit VR200 rack price ($7.8M, up from ~$4M) and per-chip prices ($55k GPU, $5k CPU) are the unit cost of the AI systems hyperscalers buy; higher unit cost raises the dollar capex needed for a given build, informing the calendar-2026 hyperscaler capex outcome. It is a per-unit estimate, not an aggregate spend figure.\"\n\nE2 → clean-4: \"Memory content (~$2M/rack, 435% jump) is a major component of the VR200 system price, so it bears on the total dollar cost of the AI infrastructure hyperscalers are budgeting, informing the capex outcome; source is an estimate.\"\n\nE3 → clean-4: \"The BOM shift (memory 5-10%→25-30%, GPU 63%→51%) shows how the total VR200 system cost — hence hyperscaler AI capex per system — is composed; informs the capex question. Source estimate.\"\n\nE4 → clean-2: \"Morgan Stanley's schedule that VR200 first shipments begin Q3 2026 supports (does not confirm) that Vera Rubin begins shipping by end-2026; it is a forecast, not an NVIDIA shipment announcement.\" \n→ clean-4: \"Q3/Q4 2026 shipment timing places hyperscaler spending on these racks within calendar 2026, informing the 2026 capex outcome.\"\n\nE5 → clean-2: \"Memory supply tightness is flagged as a scheduling risk; if it extends, it could threaten the Q3 2026 VR200 shipment schedule — a risk to the shipping-by-end-2026 thesis, though the item still schedules shipments for Q3 2026.\" \n→ clean-4: \"Rising DRAM/NAND/HBM prices and DDR5 contract price increases raise the input cost of AI systems, which bears on the dollar capex hyperscalers must spend.\"\n\nE6 → clean-4: \"Self-sou