in rack price down to around $6.7 million from the ~$7.8 million estimate, i.e., procurement structure can move final pricing by $1 million+ per rack.\"\nPassage: \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\nAnd \"with procurement structure able to move final pricing by $1+ million per rack\". I'll include both passages? The second is in the key-facts bullets. Both support. Fine.\n\nHmm, is E5 worth keeping? It's a named estimate of pricing sensitivity. It's an analyst's calculation. I'll keep it; low harm. Actually, \"a named analyst's reading of an event already recorded that adds no new fact or calculation\" — this adds a new calculation ($6.7M). So keep.\n\nE2: Memory content.\nSummary: \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\"\nPassages: \"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\"\nAlso the bullet \"Memory costs jumped 435% to roughly $2 million per rack due to increased LPDDR5X capacity and 3D NAND storage...\" — that's a repeat within the item of the same fact. Keep the fuller wording.\n\nE3: BOM share shift.\nSummary: \"Morgan Stanley's bill-of-materials analysis puts memory at 25-30% of the Vera Rubin VR200 rack cost versus 5-10% on the GB200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\"\nPassages: \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\" and maybe \"GPU share of bill of materials fell from 63% to 51% while memory rose to 25-30%, requiring infrastructure planners to model AI spend on total system cost not accelerator count\" — repeat. Use the fuller.\n\nE4: Shipment schedule.\nSummary: \"Morgan Stanley Research expects Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 racks to begin shipping in the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\"\nPassage: \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\"\nTier: third_party_view. Link: clean-2.\n\nE6: DDR5 contract pricing climbing / memory supply squeeze raising component costs.\nSummary: \"The item reports that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply and that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, with device makers including Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\nPassages: \"Only a handful of companies buy racks at this scale. But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\nTier: fact? It's a reported market movement — reported by MarketScale, attributed vaguely. \"Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply\" — reported actual movement. Tier: fact? Or third_party_view? It's a journalist's report of a market price movement — it's not a company's guidance nor an analyst's opinion. Hmm, \"fact: an official action, published actual figure or completed transaction, including an announcement or completed transaction reported by an article.\" A price movement reported without a figure... Kind of a fact reported by the article. I'd say fact with original_party null? Or third_party_view (journalist's observation, unattributed). The rule: \"If none matches and the claim is unattributed, use rumour.\" Hmm, \"Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply\" is unattributed in terms of source. It's a market observation. If I classify strictly: not a rumour (not unnamed sources/unconfirmed), it's not party_statement, not third_party_view... it's the article's own reporting of a market development. I think fact is reasonable — a reported actual development (price trend). But \"sharply\" without figure. Hmm. Alternatively third_party_view as the publication's market read. I'll go with fact, original_party null, reporting_speaker MarketScale. Hmm, risk. Let me use third_party_view? The tier is about confirmation. A reported market price trend by a media outlet... I think \"fact\" fits better than \"third_party_view\" (which is for opinions/estimates). But without a figure it's a qualitative market claim. Actually maybe skip E6 entirely? It's about memory market generally, not NVIDIA specifically. The read-back: would an analyst write \"contract DDR5 prices have climbed sharply; Samsung and Apple passing memory costs\" in NVIDIA notes? Maybe as context for memory input cost inflation, which is 25-30% of rack BOM. It's arguably relevant to NVIDIA's rack cost. But it's about laptops/consumer devices too. Hmm. I'll keep it — it informs input-cost pressure on the rack BOM. Links empty.\n\nActually, do I risk noise complaints? The instruction says most items are background but a page with eight statements yields eight items. I'll keep it.\n\nShould I also keep the \"memory supply constraints and tight availability now warrant the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation\" — that's advice/opinion, skip.\n\nAlso \"demand is not in question\" — skip.\n\nDo I keep \"Uber deployed ChatGPT Work to more than 30,000 employees