st? Contract DDR5 price climbing affects memory costs which affect rack costs which NVDA passes through. Marginally relevant. I'll keep it unlinked.\n\nLet me write final JSON.\n\nFor statement_date: The item is dated 2026-07-06 (publication). Morgan Stanley analysis has no date. All statement_dates null. statement_date_text null.\n\nevidence_period:\n- E1: \"Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 generation (successor to GB300 NVL72)\"\n- E2: same\n- E3: \"Q3–Q4 2026\"\n- E4: \"Vera Rubin VR200 generation\"\n- E5: null? \"current\" — could say null. Or \"2026\". I'll put null.\n\nNow write summaries with actor and reporting speaker.\n\nE1 summary: \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers about $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and that Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume, per a bill-of-materials analysis reported by MarketScale.\"\n\nE2 summary: \"Per Morgan Stanley, memory content in the Vera Rubin VR200 rack is about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage; memory's share of the bill of materials rose from 5–10% on the GB200 to 25–30% on the VR200 while the GPU's share fell from about 63% to 51%.\"\n\nE3 summary: \"Morgan Stanley's analysis states that first shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\"\n\nE4 summary: \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin VR200 rack price down to around $6.7 million from about $7.8 million, showing procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n\nE5 summary: \"MarketScale reports that contract DDR5 memory pricing has climbed sharply and that device makers including Samsu