shipment schedule) vs cost. I'll keep Evidence 3 separate. Good.\n\nLet me write summaries.\n\nEvidence 1 summary: \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and that NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\"\nPassages: \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\"\nNote there's a typo \"Nvidia 's\" with a space—copy verbatim. I'll copy exactly including the odd space? Verbatim means exactly. I'll reproduce as in text: \"Nvidia 's\". Let me keep it.\n\nActually to be safe, copy the exact contiguous passage. The text: \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation.\" Second passage: \"The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\"\n\nEvidence 2 summary: \"Morgan Stanley Research puts memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a roughly threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory's share of the rack bill of materials rose from 5–10% on GB200 to 25–30% on VR200 while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\"\nPassages: \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\" and \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n\nEvidence 3 summary: \"NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 first shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter, according to Morgan Stanley Research.\"\nPassage: \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\"\nOriginal party: NVIDIA. reporting_speaker: Morgan Stanley Research (via MarketScale). Tier party_statement.\n\nHmm, is \"First shipments are scheduled...\" attributed to NVIDIA or Morgan Stanley? The preceding sentence \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content...\" Then \"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million... First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\" So the shipment schedule appears as a general statement, possibly NVIDIA's disclosed schedule. I'll attribute original to NVIDIA, reporting speaker Morgan Stanley/MarketScale. Tier party_statement.\n\nEvidence 4 summary: \"The article reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is driving up memory prices—contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply—and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\nPassages: \"But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\nOriginal party: MarketScale (outlet's market report). reporting_speaker: MarketScale. Tier third_party_view.\n\nNow figures:\n\nEvidence 1 figures:\n- {\"measure\":\"Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack cost to hyperscalers\",\"period\":\"per unit\",\"value\":\"around $7.8 million per unit\",\"kind\":\"estimate\",\"basis\":\"Morgan Stanley Research BOM estimate; per rack\"}\n- {\"measure\":\"GB300 NVL72 rack cost (prior generation)\",\"period\":null,\"value\":\"roughly $4 million\",\"kind\":\"estimate\",\"basis\":\"Morgan Stanley; per rack, prior generation\"}\n- {\"measure\":\"Rubin GPU price\",\"period\":null,\"value\":\"about $55,000\",\"kind\":\"estimate\",\"basis\":\"sold in volume, per GPU\"}\n- {\"measure\":\"Vera CPU price\",\"period\":null,\"value\":\"$5,000\",\"kind\":\"estimate\",\"basis\":\"sold in volume, per CPU\"}\n\nEvidence 2 figures:\n- {\"measure\":\"memory content per VR200 rack\",\"period\":\"VR200 generation\",\"value\":\"about $2 million\",\"kind\":\"estimate\",\"basis\":\"435% jump from prior generation; Morgan Stanley\"}\n- {\"measure\":\"memory share of bill of materials\",\"period\":\"VR200 vs GB200\",\"value\":\"25 to 30 percent\",\"kind\":\"estimate\",\"basis\":\"up from 5 to 10 percent on GB200\"}\n- {\"measure\":\"GPU share of bill of materials\",\"period\":\"VR200\",\"value\":\"51 percent\",\"kind\":\"estimate\",\"basis\":\"fell from roughly 63 percent\"}\n\nValue must be copied exactly with unit. \"25 to 30 percent\" — passage says \"25 to 30 percent on the VR200.\" value \"25 to 30 percent\" ok. \"51 percent\" — passage \"fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\" value \"51 percent\" ok. Hmm the value should be exact copy. \"51 percent\" appears. Good.\n\nEvidence 3 figures: [].\nEvidence 4 figures: [].\n\nNow links:\n\nEvidence 1:\n- {\"target_type\":\"existing\",\"target_id\":\"clean-2\",\"relevance\":\"Morgan Stanley's per-unit pricing for the Vera Rubin VR200 rack and its Rubin GPU / Vera CPU prices describe the economics of the p