icing alone.\"\n\n\"Memory costs jumped 435% to roughly $2 million per rack due to increased LPDDR5X capacity and 3D NAND storage, making it a strategic budget variable across all technology purchases\"\n\n\"GPU share of bill of materials fell from 63% to 51% while memory rose to 25-30%, requiring infrastructure planners to model AI spend on total system cost not accelerator count\"\n\n\"Memory supply constraints and tight availability now warrant the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move final pricing by $1+ million per rack\"\n\nThen body:\n\"According to a Morgan Stanley bill-of-materials analysis, memory now accounts for roughly a quarter of the total, and the overall system price has nearly doubled generation over generation.\"\n\n\"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation.\"\n\n\"The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\"\n\n\"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems. First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\"\n\n\"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n\n\"Only a handful of companies buy racks at this scale. But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products. The AI buildout and the price of the memory in a laptop are now tied to the same supply squeeze.\"\n\n\"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n\n\"Nvidia's Vera Rubin platform will deliver a step change in performance, and demand is not in question. What has changed is where the money goes. The AI infrastructure bill is increasingly a memory bill, and the organizations planning capacity for the next two years will be the ones that budget for the full system rather than the chip on the front of the box.\"\n\nNeed separate statement \"First shipments scheduled Q3 2026, volume Q4.\" It is in same paragraph as memory content. Could be one item? The memory content and shipment schedule are separate statements, but same passage. The schema allows source_passages multiple; one item records one statement. We should separate because different claims. But if we use the same passage for both, that's okay. Each item can cite the same passage. Need not worry.\n\nEvidence item for rack cost:\nSummary: \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation.\"\nPassage exact. Original party: Morgan Stanley Research. Reporting speaker: MarketScale? Or article author unknown. reporting_speaker could be \"MarketScale\" or null. It says \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates\"; reporting_speaker maybe \"MarketScale\" (outlet reporting). But original party Morgan Stanley. statement_date: article date? The estimate was published in this article on 2026-07-06, but original statement date unknown. We should use null or 2026-07-06? The rules: statement_date is original statement's full day when known; do not use article date unless statement was made in originating party's own release/filing/call. Here Morgan Stanley estimate reported by MarketScale; no date of Morgan Stanley report. So statement_date null. statement_date_text null. evidence_period: \"Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 generation; compared with current GB300 NVL72 generation\" or \"2026 and beyond\" maybe. The cost estimate is for the Vera Rubin generation, not a calendar period. Could set evidence_period \"Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 generation (2026 first shipments)\"? The item says first shipments Q3 2026. But the cost estimate is per unit for that generation. We can set \"Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 generation\". Tier third_party_view. Figures: measure \"Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack price per unit\", period \"Vera Rubin generation\", value \"$7.8 million\", kind \"estimate\", basis \"hyperscale cloud providers; per unit\". Also measure \"GB300 NVL72 rack price per unit\", period \"current generation\", value \"roughly $4 million\", kind \"estimate\", basis \"comparison\". Is the second figure useful? It's a named party's estimate. Yes list all useful figures. But figures should be the company's own useful financial figures? Section 2 CONDITION says \"a statement of the company's own financial figures... Its results, guidance and estimates...\" Wait figures field description: \"The company's own useful financial figures this statement states (section 2, CONDITION); empty for any other statement.\" This is not company's own financial figures; it's Morgan Stanley estimate of product price. Hmm the schema says figures field is for the company's own useful financial figures, empty for any other statement. But section 2 CONDITION: \"a statement of the company's own financial figures (a reported result or balance, its guidance, or a named party's or consensus estimate) lists its useful figures in figures.\" It includes named party's estimate. So for third-party estimate of Nvidia product price, it is a named party's estimate about the company. Are these \"financial figures\"? Product price per unit is a financial figure. It can change revenue expectations. So list in figures. But the field description says \"company's own useful financial figures\" not \"estimates\". Actually \"a statement of the company's own financial figures ... or a named party's ... estimate\" from section. So yes list. But for BOM memory cost, it's not Nvidia's own revenue/cost; it's customer's BOM. Is it a useful financial figure of the company? It states memory content cost in Nvidia's rack, but Nvidia doesn't pay memory? Actually Nvidia may buy memory and sell integrated rack? The article says \"memory content per rack\" and \"Nvidia will charge per GPU/CPU\"; rack price includes memory. If Nvidia sells the full rack, memory is a component cost and revenue. But it's not clear Nvidia sells memory; it sells rack. The BOM is cost to hyperscaler, maybe Nvidia's revenue. It can change Nvidia revenue. But figures field maybe for financial figures like revenue/earnings, not product prices. The schema says \"financial figures\" and \"a retailer's quarterly same-store sales, an airline's guidance...\" Product price estimate is not a reported financial result. But section 2 says \"a named party's or consensus estimate\" lists useful figures. It might include EPS, revenue estimates, not product price. Hmm.\n\nNeed be cautious: figures field should include \"The company's own useful financial figures this statement states.\" For product price estimates, it's not a financial figure of the company's results, but it is a price. It can change revenue. However \"company's own\" seems to mean about the company, not necessarily reported by company. The examples: \"analysts' consensus earnings for a bank\" are conditions. Product prices per GPU/CPU could be useful. I'd list them if keeping as condition? The evidence item about per-GPU pricing likely has figures. But if figures field is strictly company financial figures, maybe empty. The instruction: \"a statement of the company's own financial figures (a reported result or balance, its guidance, or a named party's or consensus estimate) lists its useful figures in figures.\" So a named party's estimate of Nvidia's product price is not a reported result, balance, guidance, or earnings estimate; it's a product price estimate. It could be a \"figure\" but not a financial measure? The measure \"Rubin GPU price per unit\" is a financial figure. I'll include.\n\nNeed maybe not keep per-GPU/CPU pricing because it's not material? It is a named party's estimate with figure. An analyst would note Morgan Stanley's estimate of Nvidia's ASP for Rubin GPU/CPU. It informs revenue per unit and product economics. Keep. Links empty because no thesis. Could it link to clean-2? The product node is Vera Rubin; per-GPU pricing is about the product whose shipments clean-2 tracks. But clean-2 outcome is first shipments. The price does not make shipping more/less likely. Could link as \"provides ASP context for the Vera Rubin product; does not address timing.\" The instruction says link every thesis it informs. Does it inform clean-2? Clean-2 asks \"Will platform begin shipping by end 2026?\" Pricing could be relevant to demand but not shipping. The evidence might not inform the answer. I'd leave unlinked. But maybe the analyst would use it to model revenue if shipments occur, but the thesis is binary shipment timing. The link relevance would be indirect. The rule says \"use narrowest relevant thesis only when choosing between theses that cover the same thing.\" It doesn't say don't link contextual product economics to product thesis. However \"Link one statement to every thesis it informs, and to none that its own relevance says it does not inform.\" If the thesis is only about timing, pricing does not inform timing. So no link.\n\nSimilarly rack cost and memory BOM not link to clean-2. The only direct link is first shipments.\n\nWhat about \"First shipments scheduled Q3 2026\" - this directly informs clean-2. Also maybe clean-5? No, clean-5 is Kyber NVL144 with Rubin Ultra in 2027. This article is VR200 NVL72 with Vera Rubin, not Kyber. It could be evidence that the current Vera Rubin platform is on schedule for Q3 2026, while Kyber (next-next) delayed. But does it inform clean-5? Clean-5 asks Kyber shipping by end 2027 vs 2028. The fact that VR200 first shipments are Q3 2026 doesn't say anything about Kyber. No.\n\nCould \"Memory supply constraints and tight availability now warrant the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation\" link to clean-2 as supply risk. I think yes, because it's about timing/ramp of VR200. But the statement is an advisory from Morgan Stanley, not a reported supply disruption. It says constrained supply of DRAM/NAND/HBM. It could make first shipments less likely on time. Link to clean-2. But is it \"the same fact said again\" with contract DDR5 pricing? We can combine memory supply tightness and DDR5 pricing into one item: \"The article reports constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and HBM against AI-driven demand, and contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply; Morgan Stanley says memory supply constraints warrant scheduling attention.\" That is a material statement about supply/input costs. It links to clean-2? Maybe also no thesis. The direct question clean-2 is first shipments. Supply constraints are a risk. Keep and link to clean-2 with caveat.\n\nNeed not link to clean-4 because hyperscaler capex? The cost per rack and memory cost could affect hyperscaler capex budgets. Clean-4 asks combined capex in calendar 2026. A unit price increase nearly doubles rack cost; if hyperscalers buy a fixed number of racks, spend rises, but the thesis is actual capex. The item says \"For any organization budgeting AI infrastructure for the second half of 2026 and beyond, that shift changes how the numbers should be built.\" It doesn't give aggregate. Does it inform whether hyperscaler capex will approach $1T? It suggests costs are rising, which could increase capex for same capacity, but not necessarily total. Could link to clean-4? The thesis outcome is combined calendar-2026 data-center capex. A higher rack price is a driver of capex. It could inform the level. But the item doesn't state hyperscaler capex plans or aggregate. The analyst might use rack price to model capex, but the thesis asks actual spend. The evidence is a unit cost, not spend. The instruction: \"a customer's action or reported figure with a size, date or name that bears on the company's demand, supply, input costs, competition, prices or products.\" This is Nvidia's product price, not customer spend. For clean-4, a higher price per rack could mean hyperscaler capex is higher for same rack count. But no quantities. I'd avoid linking to clean-4 to not overreach. The clean-4 thesis is about aggregate capex and $1T projection; the article doesn't mention that. No link.\n\nNeed consider clean-1? No.\n\nNeed maybe no new theses. But let's revisit possible new thesis: \"Will Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack cost hyperscalers less than $8M in 2026?\" No. \"Will memory contract prices rise in 2026?\" Could be shared_quantity. The article explicitly says memory costs jumped 435% to ~$2M per rack, contract DDR5 pricing climbed sharply. It's a big matter for Nvidia and memory makers. Should we propose a thesis \"How much will contract DDR5/DRAM/NAND prices rise in calendar 2026?\" It opens as shared_quantity, settled by memory contract price reports (TrendForce, etc.), period 2026. Closest open thesis: clean-2 (Vera Rubin shipments) or clean-4 (hyperscaler capex). Why separate? Memory prices can move independently of Nvidia's shipment timing and hyperscaler capex; they affect BOM costs and margins. But is it about NVDA? Yes, memory is 25-30% of rack cost. However section 3 says a shared_quantity is \"a quantity or price outside any one company that several companies depend on, for this or next calendar year: several companies' combined output or spending, a contract price, an interest rate.\" Memory contract price is a contract price. The item gives a specific movement (435% jump in memory content per rack, contract DDR5 climbed sharply). Could be a valid new thesis. But the item doesn't give an actual contract price or a precise forecast for 2026. It says \"contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply\" and \"memory costs jumped 435% to roughly $2 million per rack.\" A thesis could ask \"How much will memory content cost per Vera Rubin rack in 2026?\" But that's not outside one company. Or \"What will contract DDR5 prices be in 2026?\" The item says pricing has climbed, but not by how much or to what level. A question can be \"Will contract DDR5 prices rise further in 2026?\" It is a shared quantity. Settled by DRAM contract price data. But would an analyst following NVDA hold a card for memory contract prices? Yes, because memory is a major cost component and supply constraint for AI systems. But the source item is a promotional article citing Morgan Stanley. It provides enough to open? The rule: \"Before proposing a new thesis, name its reason (opens_as), the public material that will settle it (settled_by), and the closest open thesis with why an analyst would bet on the two separately because their answers can move apart.\" Could do. But the \"do not open\" list: \"another estimate, source or wording of what an open thesis tracks\" no; \"an annual quantity after next year\" no; \"a financial measure's level, growth, margin or guidance for any period, on its own\" Wait this is a financial measure? Memory price is a commodity price, not company financial measure. Shared_quantity allowed. \"a feature, content or software update, price point, regional service or variant of a product already on sale\" Not product price point? It says do not open thesis about a price point of a product already on sale. This is memory contract price, not Nvidia product price. Maybe okay.\n\nBut the item's main headline \"Nvidia Vera Rubin rack costs $7.8M — memory is why\". The important research question is whether memory cost inflation changes Nvidia's rack economics and demand. Is that a thesis? It could be \"What will the memory content cost be per Vera Rubin NVL72 rack in 2026?\" But that's not a shared quantity? It's a component cost of Nvidia's product. It could be product_node? No. It is a cost, not revenue milestone. The four reasons are closed. So if we want to track memory, must frame as shared_quantity (DRAM/NAND contract prices). The item does supply evidence for memory contract prices. But is the question about the company? It bears on Nvidia's input costs/supply. The guidelines allow shared_quantity outside company. Might be valid.\n\nHowever the answer should not over-propose. The open theses already cover Vera Rubin shipping and Kyber. The memory cost article is likely intended as evidence for clean-2? Or maybe no thesis. The prompt likely expects extracting statements and linking first shipment to clean-2, maybe no new thesis. They might not want a memory price thesis because it's not big enough? But memory 25-30% of rack and 435% jump is big. Yet \"a shared quantity\" requires \"for this or next calendar year\". The item says \"second half of 2026 and beyond\" and \"next two years\". Could open for 2026. But no exact price to track. The outcome could be \"the year-over-year change in contract DRAM/NAND prices in 2026\". That is a quantity. Public material: TrendForce/DRAMeXchange contract price data. But the item doesn't mention TrendForce. It does mention contract DDR5 pricing. Settled_by could be \"memory market contract price reports (e.g., DRAMeXchange/TrendForce) and Samsung/Micron/SK Hynix earnings disclosures.\" But we shouldn't invent sources? It says public material that will settle it