k to clean-2 (risk to VR200 ramp) maybe clean-4 (cost).\n- E DDR5: \"Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\" This is a reported movement/forecast. Link to clean-2? Maybe. But it's more general memory market, not Nvidia-specific. Could be background? It is a named price movement (contract DDR5 pricing climbed sharply) and expected pass-through. The rule says a reported movement of a named price, rental rate, lead time or order book is kept even without figure. So yes. It informs NVDA's input costs. No open thesis on input costs. Link to clean-4? It can affect AI infrastructure capex, but the thesis is hyperscaler capex. Memory prices are a component. Link to clean-4? The evidence is not about hyperscaler spend, but it affects cost per rack. Could link to clean-4 as cost driver. Also clean-2 as supply risk. Hmm.\n\nMaybe we should limit to direct evidence:\n- E1 rack cost $7.8M (link clean-4? and maybe no clean-2)\n- E2 memory content $2M / 435% (link clean-4? maybe)\n- E3 memory share/GPU share (link clean-4? maybe)\n- E4 GPU/CPU pricing (empty)\n- E5 first shipments Q3 2026 (link clean-2)\n- E6 self-sourcing rack price $6.7M (link clean-4? maybe)\n- E7 memory supply tight/DDR5 pricing (link clean-2? maybe clean-4)\n\nNeed decide final links. Let's map each to theses:\n\nclean-1: no evidence.\nclean-2: E5 direct. E7 maybe supply risk. E2? Memory content is a component of the platform; if memory supply tight, clean-2. E2 itself doesn't say supply tight, just cost. No.\nclean-3: no.\nclean-4: E1 rack price, E2 memory cost, E3 BOM share, E6 self-sourcing price, E7 memory price inflation? These all affect hyperscaler capex per rack. But clean-4 asks combined spend, not per-rack. Linking too many might be over-linking. But the article is about what infrastructure buyers should budget; that is exactly capex planning. So link rack cost and memory cost to clean