d device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\nAlso \"Memory supply constraints and tight availability now warrant the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move final pricing by $1+ million per rack\" is earlier bullet, not contiguous with this. If summary uses both, need separate passages. The schema allows multiple passages. We can include both bullet and body paragraphs separately. But one evidence item records one statement. The bullet and body repeat same supply constraint. Maybe use the body passage for supply/price. The body includes constrained supply and contract DDR5. It doesn't include \"same scheduling attention\" (that's bullet). Summary can omit scheduling attention to avoid needing second passage. But then link to clean-2 as supply risk still valid? The body says constrained supply against AI-driven demand, raising component costs. It doesn't say scheduling risk. But tight supply is implicit. The bullet explicitly says scheduling attention. We could include both passages. The summary: \"MarketScale reports constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and HBM against AI-driven demand is raising component costs, and that memory supply constraints and tight availability warrant the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation; it also reports contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply and expects Samsung and Apple to pass higher memory costs into products.\" Source passages: bullet and body. They are two separate contiguous passages. That's allowed. Original party: \"MarketScale\" or \"Morgan Stanley\"? The bullet is likely article's key facts, not attributed. Body is article synthesis. Original party \"MarketScale\" (the publisher's analysis). Reporting speaker \"MarketScale\". Tier third_party_view. Period: \"2026 and beyond\" (the article says second half 2026 and beyond). Links: clean-2 (memory supply risk to VR200 ramp) and clean-4