e is general background. We can include only the second and third sentences? The passage must be contiguous. We can quote:\n\"But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\nThat starts with \"But\". It's contiguous. Fine.\nSummary: \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market; contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\nOriginal party: \"MarketScale\" (article's synthesis). Reporting speaker: \"MarketScale\". Tier third_party_view? The contract DDR5 price is a fact, but the Samsung/Apple expectation is a forecast. Combined, third_party_view. Statement date null. Period: \"2026 and beyond\" or \"second half 2026 and beyond\". The article says \"for the second half of 2026 and beyond\" in previous paragraph, but the passage doesn't. We can set evidence_period \"2026 and beyond\" based on article. Or null? The fact is current; the expectation is upcoming products. Set \"2026 and beyond\" maybe.\nLinks: clean-2 and clean-4.\nFigures: empty.\n\nWould E7 link to clean-2 be overreach? It says memory supply constrained, which is a risk to VR200 ramp. The thesis clean-2 asks first shipments by end 2026. The article says first shipments Q3 2026 and memory supply tight. The evidence could make on-time shipments less likely? It doesn't say Nvidia lacks memory. But supply tightness is relevant. Link with caveat. Yes.\n\nWould E7 link to clean-4? The memory price inflation raises AI infrastructure costs, which bears on capex. Yes.\n\nE3 maybe link to clean-4? Let's decide final. I think E1, E2, E3, E4, E6 all could link to clean-4 because they are all BOM/cost components. But the clean-4 thesis is about combined capex, not BOM. The article is literally \"what infrastructure buyers should budget for.\" All cost details bear on budgeting. An analyst following clean-4 would write the rack price and memory cost. The per-GPU/CPU price also. The BOM share also. So maybe link all to clean-4? But the instruction says \"use the narrowest relevant thesis only when choosing between theses that cover the same thing.\" There's only clean-4. If they inform, link. The risk is over-linking. But the prompt says link every thesis it informs. The clean-4 thesis is broad enough to cover capex cost drivers. I'll link E1, E2, E6 to clean-4. E3 and E4 maybe leave unlinked as product economics, not aggregate capex. But E3 is BOM composition, which affects where spend goes, not total. E4 is Nvidia revenue per unit, not hyperscaler total. So leave E3/E4 unlinked. That seems balanced.\n\nCould E3 link to clean-2? No.\n\nNeed maybe include E3 even if unlinked. Yes, it's material.\n\nCould E4 be considered a \"product price point\" which is not kept? The closing check says not kept: \"a product, game, driver or benchmark specification with no business scale... a retail price detail... and a comparison the company... publishes; it does not cover ... the company's decision to change the price it charges.\" Hmm \"a retail price detail\" is not kept. But here it's an analyst estimate of Nvidia's ASP for a new product, with business scale ($55k per GPU, $5k per CPU). Is that a \"price point\" of a product? It's not a retail price detail; it's a BOM/ASP estimate. It has business scale. It's a named party's estimate about the company. Should be kept. The exclusion for \"price point\" in section 3 is about not opening a thesis. Evidence can be kept. Yes.\n\nWhat about \"The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase.\" That's an opinion/characterization. Not kept separately.\n\nWhat about \"memory now accounts for roughly a quarter of the total, and the overall system price has nearly doubled generation over generation.\" This is duplicate of E1/E3. Not separate.\n\nWhat about \"demand is not in question.\" Not kept.\n\nWhat about \"The AI infrastructure bill is increasingly a memory bill\" - characterization. Not kept.\n\nNeed maybe include \"Nvidia's Vera Rubin platform will deliver a step change in performance\" as product spec? No.\n\nNeed maybe include \"First shipments are scheduled...\" as fact or party_statement? It's a schedule, not a completed outcome. If from Morgan Stanley, third_party_view. If from Nvidia, party_statement. The article doesn't attribute to Nvidia. Use third_party_view. Good.\n\nNeed maybe include \"The bank estimates Nvidia will charge...\" as third_party_view. Good.\n\nNeed maybe include \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates...\" as third_party_view. Good.\n\nNeed maybe include \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content...\" as third_party_view.\n\nNeed maybe include \"As a share...\" as third_party_view. Original party Morgan Stanley? It doesn't say, but context. Could set original_party \"Morgan Stanley Research\" because the BOM analysis is Morgan Stanley. The passage is in the article after Morgan Stanley. Good.\n\nNeed maybe include \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing...\" as third_party_view.\n\nNeed maybe include \"MarketScale reports...\" for E7. Original party \"MarketScale\". Reporting speaker \"MarketScale\". Tier third_party_view. If it's the article's own synthesis, original party is the outlet. Good.\n\nNeed maybe include statement_date for E7? Article date 2026-07-06? The rules: statement_date is original statement's full day when known; do not use article date unless statement made in originating party's own release. For MarketScale's own analysis, the article is the original statement, so statement_date could be 2026-07-06. But the publication date is 2026-07-06. The instruction says \"The item's publisher and publication date come from its source metadata.\" It also says do not use the article date as the original date unless the statement was made in the originating party's own release, filing, call or analysis. For MarketScale's own analysis published in the article, the article date is the original statement date. So for E7 (MarketScale's own synthesis), statement_date 2026-07-06. For Morgan Stanley estimates reported by MarketScale, statement_date null (or could be 2026-07-06 if the article is the only publication? But original is Morgan Stanley report, unknown). For E5 (MarketScale reports schedule, no attribution), statement_date 2026-07-06? It is the article's statement. But it's a report of a schedule, original party unknown. The rules: statement_date original statement's full day when known; if the item reports it, the date is article date? \"Do not use the article date as the original date unless the statement was made in the originating party's own release, filing, call or analysis.\" For MarketScale's own reporting, the originating party is MarketScale, and the article is its release/analysis. So yes, 2026-07-06 for MarketScale-originated statements. For E7, yes. For E5, if original_party null but reporting speaker MarketScale, it's MarketScale's report; statement_date 2026-07-06? Hmm original schedule might be from Morgan Stanley, but we set original_party null. If it's the article's own statement, date 2026-07-06. But the schedule likely originates from Morgan Stanley analysis; we don't know report date. To avoid using article date incorrectly, set null for E5. For E7, it's clearly article's synthesis, so 2026-07-06. But the article date is known. We can use 2026-07-06. For E1-E4/E6, null. E5 null. E7 2026-07-06.\n\nBut the evidence tier for E7 third_party_view (journalist). The statement_date could be 2026-07-06. statement_date_text \"2026-07-06\"? No, statement_date_text is original date as stated; the article metadata has publication date, not in text. We can set statement_date_text null. The schema: statement_date full day when known. Since metadata gives publication date, for MarketScale's own analysis, statement_date 2026-07-06. Fine.\n\nFor E1-E6, could the article publication date be the date Morgan Stanley estimates are reported? The rules say don't use article date unless statement made in originating party's own release. So null.\n\nNeed maybe use \"evidence_period\" for E1: \"Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 generation (first shipments Q3 2026)\" maybe. The cost estimate is for that generation. Good.\n\nE2 period: \"Vera Rubin generation vs prior generation\" no specific year. E3 period: \"GB200 to VR200 generation comparison\". E4 period: \"Vera Rubin generation, volume pricing\". E5 period: \"Q3 2026; Q4 2026\". E6 period: \"Vera Rubin rack, conditional\". E7 period: \"2026 and beyond\".\n\nNeed maybe link E7 to clean-2 and clean-4. E7 relevance to clean-2: \"Constrained DRAM/NAND/HBM supply is a potential risk to the Q3/Q4 2026 VR200 ramp tracked by clean-2; the item does not report an actual delay or Nvidia shipment shortfall.\" E7 relevance to clean-4: \"Higher memory prices and tight supply raise the per-rack cost of AI infrastructure and could affect hyperscaler capex levels/timing tracked by clean-4; no aggregate spend is given.\" Good.\n\nE1 relevance to clean-4: \"Per-rack cost nearly doubling vs prior generation is a direct input to hyperscaler capex budgeting tracked by clean-4; it does not give the number of racks or aggregate spend.\" Good.\n\nE2 relevance to clean-4: \"Memory content is a major component of the per-rack cost and its 435% jump bears on hyperscaler AI infrastructure budgets tracked by clean-4; it does not state aggregate capex.\" Good.\n\nE6 relevance to clean-4: \"Shows a conditional lower per-rack price from self-sourcing memory, informing cost assumptions in hyperscaler capex tracked by clean-4; not an actual spend figure.\" Good.\n\nShould E1 also link to clean-2? Maybe no. But what if the grader expects all Vera Rubin statements linked to clean-2? The open thesis clean-2 is the only Vera Rubin thesis. The item's title \"Nvidia Vera Rubin rack costs $7.8M — memory is why\". The first shipment sentence is direct. The cost data is about the product, but the thesis question is shipment timing. The instruction: \"Link one statement to every thesis it informs, and to none that its own relevance says it does not inform.\" The cost data does not inform shipment timing. So no. Good.\n\nCould E2 link to clean-2 because memory supply/cost could affect shipment? E2 is memory cost, not supply. It doesn't say supply tight. E7 covers supply. So no.\n\nCould E3 link to clean-2? No.\n\nCould E4 link to clean-2? No.\n\nCould E5 link to clean-4? It informs timing of when hyperscalers receive racks, but clean-4 is calendar 2026 total. First shipments Q3 2026 means some revenue/capex in 2026. It could inform. But the thesis is total capex, not timing. It could make 2026 capex more likely to include VR200 purchases. But not enough. I'd leave only clean-2.\n\nNeed maybe include \"New theses\" empty. But let's revisit if any new thesis should be proposed for memory supply. The prompt likely expects sometimes new theses. The source item is about a major shift: memory now 25-30% of AI rack cost, memory costs up 435%. This is a big matter. No open thesis covers memory supply/cost. Could be a new shared_quantity thesis. Should we propose? Let's evaluate against section 3 rigorously.\n\nA thesis asks one quantity for one period, or one event. Candidate: \"How much will contract prices for DRAM, NAND and HBM memory rise in calendar 2026?\" This is a quantity (price) outside any one company, several companies depend on (Nvidia, hyperscalers, memory makers), for this calendar year. Public material: contract price reports from DRAMeXchange/TrendForce, memory makers' earnings. It can settle on dates (monthly/quarterly). Closest open thesis: clean-4 (hyperscaler capex) or clean-2 (Vera Rubin shipments). Why separate: memory prices can move independently of Nvidia's shipment timing and hyperscaler capex; they affect Nvidia's product cost and pricing, and memory makers' revenue. An analyst would bet on memory prices separately from Nvidia's product launch. This seems valid. The item gives evidence: memory content cost jumped 435% to ~$2M; contract DDR5 pricing climbed sharply; supply constrained. It supports the thesis. It is not a financial measure's level/growth/margin/guidance of the company; it's a commodity price. It is not a product price point of Nvidia's product. It is shared_quantity. So maybe we should propose.\n\nBut the item doesn't give a specific contract price value or a specific period beyond \"second half 2026 and beyond\". It says \"Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply\" — that is a movement, not a level. The thesis could be \"Will contract DDR5 prices rise further in 2026?\" But no baseline. Or \"How much will memory content cost per Vera Rubin rack be in 2026?\" Not shared. The shared quantity could be \"the 2026 contract price of DDR5 memory.\" The item gives evidence of a sharp climb but no exact. A thesis can be opened even if the item gives no exact figure? The rules: \"A shared quantity takes its release date and a product event the date its outcome can be checked, only when the item supports one.\" The item supports \"second half of 2026 and beyond\" and \"next two years\" but not a specific release date. It says \"Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply\" — we could check at end-2026. The period could be \"calendar 2026\". It is a shared quantity. It might be valid.\n\nBut the article's memory cost is specifically per Nvidia rack, not a market contract price index. It mentions contract DDR5 pricing as a related market force. The main quantity is Nvidia's rack BOM, not memory contract price. A new thesis about memory contract prices might be too far from the item's core. The prompt: \"new theses: the important research questions this item raises that no open thesis covers.\" The item raises \"memory is now a strategic budget variable\" and \"memory supply constraints.\" An analyst following NVDA would care about memory supply/cost because it affects Nvidia's product cost and shipments. A thesis on memory supply could be valid. But the open clean-2 covers Vera Rubin shipments; memory supply is a risk to that. The open clean-4 covers hyperscaler capex; memory cost is a driver. Is a separate memory thesis warranted? The guidelines say \"Related questions can be separate when their outcomes matter independently.\" Memory prices matter independently of Nvidia's shipments and hyperscaler capex. So maybe yes.\n\nHowever, the \"do not open\" list includes \"a financial measure's level, growth, margin or guidance for any period, on its own.\" A commodity price is not a financial measure? It is a price. Shared_quantity is explicitly allowed. So okay.\n\nWould an analyst hold a card for \"What will contract DDR5 prices be in 2026?\" Maybe a memory analyst would, but a senior hedge-fund analyst covering NVDA would track memory costs as a factor, yes. But the company in scope is NVDA; the thesis can be about a shared quantity. The prompt allows evidence about other parties when it informs research on the company. A shared quantity thesis is exactly that.\n\nIf we propose, we need link E7 (memory supply/contract DDR5) to it. Maybe also E2 (memory content cost) to it? E2 is Nvidia rack memory cost, which is a consequence of memory prices. It could inform the memory price thesis. But E2 is an estimate of memory content per rack, not contract price. It shows the impact. Could link to new thesis as evidence of memory cost inflation. But the new thesis asks contract prices; E2 is not a contract price. It could still be evidence of cost impact. Hmm.\n\nNeed name closest open thesis. Which one? clean-4 (hyperscaler capex) is about AI infrastructure spending; memory price inflation affects cost. clean-2 is Vera Rubin shipments; memory supply affects production. The closest maybe clean-4 because both are about AI infrastructure cost/spend. Why separate: memory prices can rise or fall independently of hyperscaler capex plans and Nvidia shipment timing; they affect Nvidia's BOM and memory makers. The answers can move apart. Settled_by: \"Memory contract price reports (e.g., DRAMeXchange/TrendForce monthly/quarterly DDR5, DRAM, NAND and HBM contract prices) and memory makers' earnings disclosures.\" Period: \"Calendar 2026, checked at end-2026.\" Opens_as: shared_quantity. Description: \"How much will contract prices for DRAM, NAND and HBM memory rise in calendar 2026, and will tight supply keep memory a major share of AI rack costs?\" That's two quantities? Better one: \"What will the year-over-year change in contract prices for DRAM, NAND and HBM memory be in calendar 2026?\" One quantity. But the item gives no specific memory type. Maybe \"Will contract DDR5 memory prices rise further in 2026?\" That's one quantity/event. But DDR5 is only one type; article also mentions NAND/HBM. Could ask \"Will contract memory prices (DDR5, NAND and HBM) rise further in 2026?\" That's a composite. Maybe too broad.\n\nA thesis must be clear. \"What will the average contract price of DDR5 DRAM be in calendar 2026?\" This is one quantity, one period. The item supports DDR5 pricing climbed sharply. Settled by DRAMeXchange/TrendForce. Closest clean-4. Why separate: DDR5 contract prices can move independently of hyperscaler capex and Nvidia's Vera Rubin shipment schedule. This is valid. But does it cover the item's memory content cost? It covers the DDR5 part, not NAND/HBM. The article's main memory cost includes LPDDR5X and 3D NAND. DDR5 is different. Hmm.\n\nMaybe \"Will contract prices for AI-server memory (LPDDR5X, HBM and 3D NAND) rise in 2026?\" Composite. Not ideal.\n\nMaybe no new thesis because the item's memory cost is about Nvidia's rack BOM, and no clean shared quantity is named with enough specificity. The prompt says \"a vague or noisy topic with no outcome to watch is not proposed.\" The memory price topic is a bit vague. Better not propose.\n\nLet's see if there is any other new thesis candidate: \"Will Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack price be $7.8M?\" Not one of four. \"Will Nvidia charge $55k per Rubin GPU?\" No. \"Will hyperscalers self-source memory modules to lower rack price to $6.7M?\" That's a procurement decision, not one of four. \"Will memory supply constraints delay VR200 volume ramp?\" This is a product node but clean-2 covers first shipments. Not separate. So no new theses.\n\nThus final new_theses empty. Evidence items with links as chosen.\n\nNeed ensure response conforms to schema. Only JSON object. No markdown.\n\nLet's refine evidence summaries and passages.\n\nE1 passage exact: \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation.\" Note there is a space between Nvidia and 's in source. Copy exactly. Good.\n\nE2 passage: \"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\" This is contiguous. Good.\n\nE3 passage: \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\" Good.\n\nE4 passage: \"The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\" Good.\n\nE5 passage: \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\" Good.\n\nE6 passage: \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\" Good.\n\nE7 passage: \"But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\" Good.\n\nShould E7 also include \"Memory supply constraints and tight availability now warrant the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move final pricing by $1+ million per rack\" as a second passage? If we include, summary should mention. But we decided not. The first passage already says constrained supply. Good.\n\nNeed link E7 to clean-2 and clean-4. Is E7 original party MarketScale? The body sentence \"But the force behind the price...\" is article's analysis. Yes. Tier third_party_view. statement_date 2026-07-06? Since it's the article's own analysis, yes. But the article date is 2026-07-06. The statement_date_text null. evidence_period \"2026 and beyond\" maybe. The article says \"second half of 2026 and beyond\" in previous paragraph. We can set \"2026 and beyond\" to be safe. Or \"second half 2026 and beyond\". Use \"2026 and beyond (article addresses budgeting for the second half of 2026 and beyond)\". The evidence_period field should be the period the fact/plan/forecast covers. For contract DDR5 pricing, it's current/upcoming products, no explicit period. But article context says second half 2026 and beyond. Set \"2026 and beyond\". Good.\n\nE1 evidence_period: \"Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 generation (first shipments Q3 2026)\"? The cost estimate is for the generation. The first shipments Q3 2026 is from E5.