utlet's own analysis. Allowed.\n\nFor E1 original_party: \"Morgan Stanley Research\". reporting_speaker: \"MarketScale\". Good.\nE2 original_party: \"Morgan Stanley\". reporting_speaker: \"MarketScale\".\nE3 original_party: \"Morgan Stanley Research\" maybe. The passage doesn't say, but context. Use \"Morgan Stanley Research\" to be consistent.\nE4 original_party: \"Morgan Stanley\" (the bank).\nE6 original_party: \"Morgan Stanley\".\n\nNeed maybe include \"statement_date_text\": null for all.\n\nLet's generate.\n\nWait, E1 summary says \"up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation.\" The source says \"current GB300 NVL72 generation.\" Good.\n\nE2 summary says \"prior generation\" not \"GB300\". The source says prior generation. Fine.\n\nE3 summary says \"GB200 to VR200\" but source says \"GB200\" and \"VR200\". Good.\n\nE7 summary \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply...\" The original party is MarketScale. Good.\n\nNeed maybe include \"figure value\" exactly as passage. For E3, value \"5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200\" but passage says \"from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200\". Use exact substring? The schema says \"copied exactly from one of this item's passages, with its unit.\" We can use \"5 to 10 percent\" and \"25 to 30 percent\" separately. Better.\n- measure \"Memory share of bill of materials (GB200)\", value \"5 to 10 percent\"\n- measure \"Memory share of bill of materials (VR200)\", value \"25 to 30 percent\"\n- measure \"GPU share of bill of materials (GB200)\", value \"roughly 63 percent\"\n- measure \"GPU share of bill of materials (VR200)\", value \"51 percent\"\nThis is more precise. But too many. Maybe one measure with value \"5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200\" is not a single exact figure. The schema wants value copied exactly. It can be a range phrase. Use separate measures.\n\nE2 values: \"about $2 million\", \"435%\", \"roughly $1 million or more\". Good.\nE1 values: \"around $7.8 million\", \"roughly $4 million\".\nE4: \"about $55,000\", \"about $5,000\". Source says \"about $55,000\" and \"$5,000\" (no about before second? \"and $5,000\"). Use exactly \"about $55,000\" and \"$5,000\".\nE6: \"around $6.7 million\".\n\nE7 no figures.\n\nLet's write final.\n\n### d3-memory 1\nLet me read the item carefully. The item is about Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack costs ($7.8M per rack), memory costs, Morgan Stanley BOM analysis, first shipments Q3 2026, volume ramp Q4. Also GPU price $55,000 per Rubin GPU, $5,000 per Vera CPU. Memory cost ~$2 million per rack, 435% jump. GPU share fell from 63% to 51%. Memory share rose to 25-30%.\n\nAlso other stories in the item: Uber ChatGPT Work rollout, PHP RCE flaws, UNICOM cooling, Schneider Electric liquid cooling estimate. These are unrelated matters — not about Nvidia's markets... Uber deployment of ChatGPT Work is OpenAI, not Nvidia. PHP flaws irrelevant. Cooling — could bear on data center infrastructure but it's about cooling technologies, generally not Nvidia specific. Schneider Electric single-phase liquid cooling share of 2026 liquid cooling market — that's a market adjacent but a \"named analyst's estimate about industry\". Does it bear on Nvidia? Marginally — cooling for AI racks. It's a general market share estimate for liquid cooling technologies, not Nvidia-specific. I think it's noise: not a named price/volume in company's market... Actually cooling is part of rack BOM. But the estimate is about cooling technology mix (single-phase vs two-phase vs immersion), not about Nvidia's demand/supply/prices/products. I'd drop it as background typical claim. The Uber item: unrelated, other company's matter that touches none of Nvidia's markets? Uber using ChatGPT Work is OpenAI demand → not Nvidia. Drop.\n\nNow material statements:\n\n1. Morgan Stanley estimate: Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscalers ~$7.8 million per unit, up from ~$4 million for GB300 NVL72. This is a third_party_view (analyst estimate). It bears on Nvidia's product pricing/revenue per rack, and on clean-3 (Vera Rubin shipping) — well, the pricing bears on revenue per unit; the first shipment schedule Q3 2026 bears directly on clean-3.\n\n2. Nvidia will charge ~$55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU in volume — third party estimate (Morgan Stanley). This is pricing of the company's product — bears on revenue path. Which thesis? clean-3 (Vera Rubin shipments)? Pricing informs revenue but clean-3 is about shipping start. Hmm. There is no open thesis about Nvidia's rack pricing/revenue. Could propose a thesis? Section 3 do-not-open: a financial measure's level, growth, margin or guidance on its own. Pricing of a product isn't a thesis reason. Shared_quantity? A \"contract price\" outside any one company... The rack price is Nvidia's own product price. product_node is about generation reaching first shipments — clean-3 covers Vera Rubin shipping. So pricing evidence links to clean-3 loosely? The pricing doesn't inform whether shipments begin. Hmm, but it's material: cost per rack. I'd link the $7.8M rack cost estimate to clean-3 as evidence about the Vera Rubin product's economics... The relevance: pricing of the platform whose shipment date clean-3 tracks. That's a stretch but acceptable — arguably the price bears on revenue expectation at first shipments. Alternatively leave unlinked. I think linking to clean-3 is defensible: it informs the Vera Rubin launch's revenue economics. Actually maybe better: memory cost / BOM is about input costs, not covered by any open thesis. Keep unlinked?\n\nLet me structure evidence items:\n\nE1: Morgan Stanley estimates VR200 NVL72 rack cost ~$7.8M vs ~$4M for GB300 NVL72; GPU $55,000, Vera CPU $5,000; memory ~$2M per rack, up 435%; GPU share 63%→51%, memory 5-10%→25-30%. That's a lot in one statement — but these are all one BOM analysis. The instruction: \"One evidence item records one statement\".