nd is not in question\" — unargued view, drop. \"Nvidia's Vera Rubin platform will deliver a step change in performance\" — unargued claim, drop.\n\nE6: Memory supply constraints warrant same planning attention as GPU allocation; contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply; Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into products. The DDR5 pricing is a reported price movement in memory market — outside Nvidia but relevant to input costs. Kept? \"a reported movement of a named price... even without a figure\" is kept per rules. So contract DDR5 pricing climbing sharply — a named price movement. But it's about memory not Nvidia's product. It bears on input costs of Nvidia racks. I'd include as evidence with empty links, or fold into the memory item. Hmm — it's a separate statement (broad memory market pricing). I'd combine? Separate item: \"Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply... Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into products\" — the Samsung/Apple part is other companies' matters; the DDR5 price movement bears on input costs of Nvidia racks. Keep as one item with empty links, tier third_party_view? The item states it as fact (\"has climbed sharply\") without attribution — this is the publisher's own report. tier: third_party_view (journalist's) or fact? A published actual market movement... It's the publisher's assertion without a figure. I'd call third_party_view.\n\nHmm, is this worth keeping? The closing check: a reported movement of a named price (contract DDR5 pricing) is kept per rules. Yes, keep. It informs Nvidia's rack input costs → no thesis. Kept, empty links.\n\nActually wait — could it link to clean-3? No.\n\nAlso: is there a thesis about Nvidia's memory supply risk being a constraint on Vera Rubin shipments? clean-3 \"will first shipments begin by end of 2026\" — memory supply tightness could affect that. Hmm, arguably memory supply constraints are a scheduling risk for Vera Rubin rack shipments → informs clean-3's timing question. That's a reasonable link: tight memory supply could delay rack shipments. I'll link the memory supply tightness statement to clean-3 with appropriate relevance framing (\"source says memory supply is tight, a scheduling risk for rack builds; inference that it could affect Vera Rubin shipment timing\"). Hmm, be careful not to overreach. I think it's acceptable and useful. Actually the relevance field asks how evidence informs the thesis, distinguishing source claims from inference. So I can link memory supply tightness to clean-3 noting inference.\n\nLet me decide: \n- Memory cost/BOM share item → no link? or clean-3? The cost content doesn't affect shipment timing. Leave unlinked.\n- Memory supply tightness item (DRAM/NAND/HBM constrained, contract DDR5 pricing up sharply) → link to clean-3 as potential scheduling risk? It's a stretch but defensible. Alternatively no link. I'll link to clean-3 with clearly labeled inference, saying memory supply tightness is a stated scheduling risk for building these racks, which bears on whether first shipments land in Q3 2026. Hmm. I worry about overreach. I'll include it — the item explicitly says \"Treat memory supply as a scheduling risk\" in rack context.\n\nAlso Uber and PHP items: drop.\n\nAlso the cooling item (UNICOM/Schneider): spec/industry estimates, no business scale for Nvidia. Drop. Although \"55% of the 2026 liquid cooling market\" is a market-share estimate for a technology — not Nvidia's market. Drop.\n\nNow, does the $7.8M rack price support a new thesis? Section 3 allows shared_quantity \"a contract price\" — no, rack price is Nvidia's own product price, and do-not-open says a financial measure's level... Actually a price point of a product already on sale is not open (\"a feature, content or software update, price point, regional service or variant of a product already on sale\"). Vera Rubin isn't on sale, but product_node covers the generation reaching first shipments, already tracked by clean-3. So no new thesis. Good — no new theses at all.\n\nLet me double check whether any of the four reasons gives a new thesis: \n- product_node: Vera Rubin first shipments → clean-3 already covers. Kyber → clean-7. \n- binding_obligation: none (hyperscaler self-sourcing is hypothetical, not Nvidia's obligation).\n- shared_quantity: hyperscaler capex → clean-6. Memory price? Not \"for this or next calendar year\" with a figure. No.\n- policy_stage: none.\n\nSo new_theses = [].\n\nNow figures. The CONDITION applies only to \"the company's own financial figures (a reported result or balance, its guidance, or a named party's or consensus estimate)\". Hmm: \"a named party's or consensus estimate\" of the company's financial figures. Morgan Stanley's $7.8M rack price, $55,000 GPU price, memory content — these are estimates of the company's product prices/costs. Are they \"financial figures\" for the figures field? The figures field says \"The company's own useful financial figures this statement states (section 2, CONDITION); empty for any other statement.\" A named party's estimate of Nvidia's product pricing... $55,000 per Rubin GPU is a price estimate — arguably a useful financial figure (revenue per unit). I think including them as estimates is reasonable: measure \"price per Rubin GPU\", value \"$55,000\", kind \"estimate\", basis \"sold in volume, Morgan Stanley estimate\". And rack price \"$7.8 million per unit\". Memory content \"$2 million per rack\". Hmm, memory content is a cost estimate, not Nvidia's financial figure. I'll include the ones that are Nvidia's own revenue/cost-per-unit figures: rack price $7.8M (estimate), GPU $55,000, CPU $5,000, memory content ~$2M. Yeah.\n\nLet me write evidence items.\n\nE1: Rack price estimate.\nSummary: \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\"\nPassages: \"According to a Morgan Stanley bill-of-materials analysis, memory now accounts for roughly a quarter of the total, and the overall system price has nearly doubled generation over generation.\" plus \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\"\n\nAlso the opening: \"Nvidia's next-generation Vera Rubin rack will cost hyperscalers $7.8 million—nearly double the prior generation\". Contiguous passages; two or three passages okay.\n\nTier: third_party_view. statement_date: null (no date given; article 2026-07-06). statement_date_text: null. evidence_period: \"Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 generation, priced per unit at volume; compared with GB300 NVL72\" — period maybe \"VR200 generation (successor to GB300 NVL72)\". I'll say \"Per-unit pricing for the Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 generation, compared with the current GB300 NVL72 generation\".\n\nFigures: rack cost per unit $7.8 million estimate; GB300 $4 million actual? It's an estimate of prior-gen price too — kind estimate. GPU $55,000 estimate; CPU $5,000 estimate.\n\nLinks: empty? or clean-3. Let me think once more. clean-3 asks whether Vera Rubin begins shipping by end-2026. Pricing doesn't bear on that. I'll leave E1 unlinked.\n\nHmm, but wait: \"link each one to every thesis it informs\" — does the price inform clean-3 at all? An analyst tracking clean-3 wouldn't update on price. Right, unlinked.\n\nE2: Memory content and BOM share shift; memory +435%, LPDDR5X tripled to 54TB per rack, ~$1M+ 3D NAND; memory share 25-30% from 5-10%; GPU share 63%→51%. Links empty.\n\nE3: Vera Rubin first shipments