d bring rack price down to ~$6.7 million.\n6. Later paragraphs are unrelated: Uber ChatGPT Work, PHP CVEs, UNICOM cooling. Those touch none of Nvidia's markets? Actually Uber's ChatGPT Work deployment is not Nvidia; not in Nvidia's markets - skip. PHP flaws - skip. Cooling (liquid cooling for AI racks) - UNICOM compares cooling; Schneider Electric engineers cite single-phase cold plates ~55% of 2026 liquid cooling market. Hmm, this touches AI racks - cooling for AI racks. Does it bear on Nvidia? It's about cooling infrastructure, adjacent to AI rack deployments. The rule: \"another company's matter that touches none of the company's markets ... or that sits next to the company only by sharing an article\". Cooling market share for AI racks — arguably relevant to AI datacenter buildout but it's a peripheral supplier market; it doesn't clearly inform Nvidia revenue/demand. I'd say it's a specification/market share of a cooling technology; it's evidence about AI rack cooling tech, not about Nvidia. I'll likely skip those. Hmm, could be considered a shared_quantity (liquid cooling market)? No — it's an estimate of a technology's share of the cooling market, not a shared quantity several companies depend on... Actually the question \"will single-phase cold plates hold 55% of the 2026 liquid cooling market\" would be a shared quantity? It's not a quantity several companies depend on for their revenue? Well, cooling suppliers depend on it. But it doesn't matter much. I think it's peripheral noise for NVDA. Skip.\n\nNow let me map the kept statements to theses.\n\nExisting theses:\n- clean-1: credit support to AI cloud partners\n- clean-2: CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in inference 2026\n- clean-3: Vera Rubin first shipments by end of calendar 2026\n- clean-4: Huawei/SMIC chip output 2026\n- clean-5: H200 China sales\n- clean-6: hyperscaler 2027 data center capex ~$1 trillion\n- clean-7: Kyber NVL144 shipments 2028\n\nNow evidence items:\n\nE1: Morgan Stanley BOM analysis: VR200 NVL72 rack ~$7.8M vs ~$4M GB300. Plus memory $2M, +435%, 54TB LPDDR5X, ~$1M+ 3D NAND. Plus GPU ~$55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU in volume. Should I split into multiple items? Rules: one evidence item records one statement. These are distinct statements: (a) rack cost estimate $7.8M vs $4M; (b) per-GPU/per-CPU price estimate; (c) memory content $2M, 435%, capacity; (d) BOM share shift 63%→51%, memory 5-10%→25-30%; (e) first shipments Q3 2026, volume Q4. (f) self-sourcing $6.7M.\n\nNote the \"same fact said again in other words\" rule — the article repeats $7.8M and memory figures multiple times. E.g., \"Memory costs jumped 435% to roughly $2 million per rack\" (bullets) and later \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump\" — same fact, keep the fuller wording, one item.\n\nAlso \"first shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth\" — this is a key statement for clean-3 (Vera Rubin first shipments by end of calendar 2026). It's a third_party_view (Morgan Stanley estimate/schedule) — actually it says \"First shipments are scheduled for...\" — the schedule is arguably Nvidia's, reported by MarketScale citing Morgan Stanley. Tier: it's a forecast/schedule reported; the article attributes the BOM to Morgan Stanley. The shipment schedule isn't explicitly attributed to Nvidia. It's likely third_party_view (Morgan Stanley's estimate) — but \"scheduled for Q3 2026\" is a plan. Hmm. Best: tier third_party_view since the entire analysis is Morgan Stanley's, and the schedule is reported without company attribution. Actually if Nvidia scheduled it, that'd be fact/party_statement. I'll use third_party_view and note it's reported in the article without attribution beyond the Morgan Stanley analysis. That's conservative. Hmm, but the guidance \"first shipments scheduled Q3 2026, volume ramping Q4\" — this is a concrete statement about product timeline; tier third_party_view is safe.\n\nSta