eep it as a separate item, empty links, or link to clean-6? Meh. Keep with empty links.\n\nHmm, but too many marginal items could hurt. Let me think about a solid set:\n\n1. Rack cost: Morgan Stanley estimates VR200 NVL72 ~$7.8M per rack vs ~$4M for GB300; memory ~$2M (+435%); LPDDR5X 3x to 54TB; ~$1M+ 3D NAND. — Combine rack price + memory into one? The article's central claim: \"Nvidia's next-generation Vera Rubin rack will cost hyperscalers $7.8 million—nearly double the prior generation—with memory now accounting for 25-30% of total cost instead of 5-10%.\" That's one statement with several figures. And later detail: memory ~$2M, 435% jump, 54TB, $1M NAND; GPU share 63%→51%. I could do two items: (i) total rack cost nearly doubling to $7.8M; (ii) memory content $2M per rack (+435%) and BOM share 25-30% vs 5-10%, GPU share 63%→51%. Yes, two items.\n\n2. Per-unit pricing: ~$55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU in volume (Morgan Stanley estimate). Figures: two estimates.\n\n3. Vera Rubin first shipments Q3 2026, volume ramp Q4. → clean-3.\n\n4. Self-sourcing memory cuts rack price to ~$6.7M.\n\n5. DDR5 contract pricing climbed sharply; Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\n\nIs 4 too granular/derivative? It gives a specific figure and a mechanism. I'll keep. Actually, hmm — \"a comparison the company, a partner or a third party publishes\"? No, it's not a comparison of products. It's an analyst's alternative cost figure. Keep.\n\nShould I keep the cooling item? \"A Motivair by Schneider Electric engineer puts single-phase cold plates at an estimated 55% of the 2026 liquid cooling market and expects them to dominate for five to 10 years.\" This is a market-share estimate for a cooling technology. Does it bear on Nvidia's demand/supply/products/prices? Only tangentially (liquid cooling in AI racks). It doesn't name Nvidia. I'll drop — it's another company's matter adjacent to AI racks, with no bearing on Nvidia's dema