over generation. Tier: third_party_view. Original party: Morgan Stanley Research. Reporting speaker: MarketScale (article). statement_date null; text null. evidence_period: \"Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 generation (first shipments scheduled Q3 2026)\" — hmm, the period the estimate describes: the VR200 generation, with shipments from Q3 2026. I'll say \"Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 generation; from Q3 2026 shipments\". Figures: empty (not Nvidia's reported financials; it's a third-party estimate of system price). Hmm — \"a named party's ... estimate\" of the company's... The rack price is a price of a system Nvidia sells; is it \"the company's own financial figures\"? It's a third-party estimate of the company's product price, which could change an analyst's revenue expectation. The schema field says \"The company's own useful financial figures this statement states (section 2, CONDITION); empty for any other statement.\" Section 2 CONDITION says \"a statement of the company's own financial figures (a reported result or balance, its guidance, or a named party's or consensus estimate)\". A named party's estimate here = Morgan Stanley's estimate of Nvidia's rack price and per-GPU price. I think it's within scope. I'll include figures for item 1: measure \"Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack price\", value \"$7.8 million\", kind estimate, period \"per unit, VR200 generation\"; and \"GB300 NVL72 rack price\" value \"$4 million\" estimate. Hmm, the GB300 price is the prior generation, not really a forecast. It's an estimate of current price. kind \"estimate\" is fine.\n\nHmm, but the check for empty-linked statements with company figures: rack price per unit → could change revenue expectations → keep. OK, include figures.\n\nItem 2: memory content and BOM share.\nPassages: \"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes