figures for \"The company's own useful financial figures this statement states (section 2, CONDITION); empty for any other statement.\" A third-party estimate of Nvidia's rack price is arguably a financial figure (price) but not a reported result/guidance/estimate of revenue/earnings. The examples: \"a retailer's quarterly same-store sales, an airline's guidance for next year's unit costs and analysts' consensus earnings for a bank are\". Rack cost is a unit cost/price, could be useful. But is it the company's own figure? It's the cost to customers, not Nvidia's revenue. The article estimates Nvidia will charge $55k per GPU, $5k per CPU. That is an estimate of Nvidia's pricing, which is revenue per unit. Could list as estimate. But the schema says \"company's own useful financial figures\". Maybe yes, price per product. However, figures must be \"value copied exactly from one of this item's passages, with its unit\". We can include:\n  - \"Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack cost per unit\" value \"$7.8 million\" kind estimate period \"Vera Rubin generation / 2026\" basis \"Morgan Stanley Research bill-of-materials estimate for hyperscale cloud providers\".\n  - \"GB300 NVL72 rack cost per unit\" value \"roughly $4 million\" kind estimate period \"current generation\" basis \"Morgan Stanley Research estimate\".\n  - \"Rubin GPU price\" value \"$55,000 per Rubin GPU\" kind estimate period \"when sold in volume\" basis \"Morgan Stanley Research estimate\".\n  - \"Vera CPU price\" value \"$5,000 per Vera CPU\" kind estimate period \"when sold in volume\".\n  - \"memory content per rack\" value \"about $2 million\" kind estimate period \"Vera Rubin generation\" basis \"Morgan Stanley Research estimate; 435% jump from prior generation\".\n  - \"LPDDR5X capacity per rack\" value \"54 terabytes per rack\" kind estimate? This is not financial. Maybe not in figures. Figures are financial measures. We can include memory content cost, not capacity. \n  - \"3D NAND storage content\" value \"roughly $1 million or more\" kind estimate.\n  - \"memory share of BOM\" value \"25 to 30 percent\" kind estimate period \"VR200\" basis \"Morgan Stanley Research; up from 5 to 10 percent on GB200\".\n  - \"GPU share of BOM\" value \"51 percent\" kind estimate period \"VR200\" basis \"down from roughly 63 percent\".\n  - \"rack price if self-sourcing memory modules\" value \"around $6.7 million\" kind estimate basis \"Morgan Stanley; hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules\".\nThese are many. But if the statement is not company's own financial figures, figures should be empty. The schema explicitly says \"empty for any other statement.\" Is a third-party estimate of Nvidia's product price a \"named party's estimate\" about the company? It is a named party's estimate of Nvidia's pricing/cost. The CONDITION says \"a statement of the company's own financial figures (a reported result or balance, its guidance, or a named party's or consensus estimate) lists its useful figures in figures.\" It doesn't limit to revenue/earnings; examples include unit costs. So yes, include. But \"The company's own useful financial figures\" could mean figures for the company, not customers' cost. The rack cost is what Nvidia charges, so Nvidia's revenue per rack. Good. We'll include. For the shipment schedule item, no figures? It has Q3/Q4, not financial. figures empty.\n\nFor the memory supply item, no company financial figures. figures empty.\n\nEvidence item E1 summary: \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers about $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. It estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU in volume; memory content is about $2 million per rack, up 435%, with memory at 25-30% of the bill of materials versus 5-10% on GB200 and the GPU share falling from about 63% to 51%. It also estimates hyperscaler self-sourcing of certain memory modules could cut the rack price to around $6.7 million.\" This is one statement? It's a bundle of the bill-of-materials analysis. It includes multiple figures. That's acceptable because one analysis. But is it too many separate claims? The cost estimate and BOM shares and self-sourcing are related. The rule says one evidence item records one statement; a statement told in several sentences with terms, figures, conditions is one item. The article's Morgan Stanley analysis is one statement. Good.\n\nSource passages: Need contiguous verbatim passages. E1 can use:\n\"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\"\nAnd:\n\"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems. First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\"\nAnd:\n\"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\nAnd:\n\"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\nThese are contiguous passages, each separately. E1 summary can rest on them. Note first passage has \"Nvidia 's\" with space due text. Copy exactly. We should preserve.\n\nBut