mory in a laptop are now tied to the same supply squeeze.\"\nAnd maybe:\n\"Treat memory supply as a scheduling risk, not only a cost. With memory representing a quarter or more of rack value and supply tight, availability and lead times deserve the same planning attention as accelerator allocation.\"\nThis is advice but supports supply tightness. The material factual statement: constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, HBM; contract DDR5 pricing climbed sharply. It's a third-party analysis. Is it about Nvidia? It says \"force behind the price\" referring to Nvidia rack price. It informs Nvidia's input costs/supply. Keep. Link to clean-3? \"Memory supply tightness could pose a scheduling risk to Vera Rubin rack shipments\" yes. Link to clean-6? \"Rising memory costs feed into hyperscaler AI infrastructure budgets\" yes. Maybe also clean-3. We'll link both.\n\nBut E3 includes \"device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\" This is a forecast about other companies. It is part of memory price inflation. Does it bear on Nvidia? It shows broad memory inflation. But it's not Nvidia-specific. Could include in summary but maybe not. The summary can focus on constrained memory supply and DDR5 price climb. The passage includes Samsung/Apple, but the summary need not mention them? It can, but they are other companies' matters. It's okay as context for memory prices. But to keep tight, summary: \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs, with contract DDR5 pricing climbing sharply; it says memory supply tightness is now a scheduling risk for AI racks.\" Original party? This is the article's analysis, maybe third-party view. It cites Morgan Stanley for rack analysis but the memory supply statement is the article's own. Original party \"MarketScale\" (reporting outlet as its own analysis). reporting_speaker \"MarketScale\". Tier thir