upply tightness and lead times could pose a scheduling risk to Vera Rubin rack shipments the thesis tracks.\"\n- clean-6: \"Rising memory costs are part of the AI infrastructure cost stack that feeds hyperscaler capex budgets.\"\n\nShould we include E4: \"Nvidia's Vera Rubin platform will deliver a step change in performance, and demand is not in question.\" Drop as unargued view.\n\nShould we include E4: \"First shipments are scheduled for Q3 2026\" is essentially a schedule. Yes.\n\nShould we include E4: \"Memory costs jumped 435% to roughly $2 million per rack\" already in E1.\n\nShould we include E4: \"GPU share of bill of materials fell from 63% to 51%\" already in E1.\n\nShould we include E4: \"Morgan Stanley estimates Nvidia will charge $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU\" part of E1.\n\nShould we include the headline \"Nvidia's Next AI Rack Costs Nearly Double...\"? No, duplicate.\n\nShould we include \"Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack will cost hyperscalers $7.8 million—nearly double the prior generation—with memory now accounting for 25-30% of total cost instead of 5-10%.\" This is in the standfirst; same as E1. No separate.\n\nWhat about \"This shift signals that AI infrastructure budgeting must focus on total system cost rather than GPU pricing alone.\" This is an unargued view/advice. Drop.\n\nWhat about \"Memory supply constraints and tight availability now warrant the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move final pricing by $1+ million per rack\" — included in E3? We included supply constraints but not procurement $1M. E1 includes $6.7M self-sourcing. The $1+M procurement sensitivity is part of E1 maybe. We can include in E1 summary: \"procurement structure can move final pricing by $1+ million per rack, with self-sourcing memory modules cutting price to ~$6.7M.\" Good.\n\nNeed ensure figures list for E1 doesn't include non-financial capacity. Let's craft figures:\n1. measure \"Vera Rubin VR