tier. \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026\" — the scheduler is Nvidia, reported via Morgan Stanley analysis/article. Tier options: fact (announcement/planned)? It's a schedule, a forecast/plan → party_statement if it's the company's statement; here reported by the article citing Morgan Stanley's analysis. I'd say third_party_view. Hmm. Or \"fact\" for a reported schedule? The rule: \"party_statement: a company's guidance, management statement or official's remarks. A forecast is a statement, not a completed outcome.\" The schedule is Nvidia's plan, reported secondhand. third_party_view (Morgan Stanley's analysis/article) seems safest. Actually the article states it as fact about scheduling — but its source is Morgan Stanley's estimate. I'll use third_party_view.\n\nHmm, for item 1 also consider linking to clean-3: does the rack price inform whether shipments start in 2026? No. But the article is about the VR200 platform cost. clean-3 is about first shipments. I'll leave 1 and 2 unlinked... but wait — should there be a new thesis about rack price / memory BOM? \"Do not open a thesis about: a financial measure's level, growth, margin or guidance for any period, on its own.\" Rack price is a product price point — also \"a price point... of a product already on sale\"? Vera Rubin isn't on sale yet; price point of a new product. Hmm, does that count as price point (do not open)? Yes, \"a feature, content or software update, price point, regional service or variant of a product already on sale\" — the product isn't already on sale though. But price level is a financial measure's level. So no thesis.\n\nCould a thesis be about memory supply constraint delaying VR200 shipments? product_node reason? No — product_node is about the product reaching first shipments, which clean-3 already tracks. So no new thesis.\n\nAlso the weird padding at the end of the article (Uber ChatGPT, PHP CVEs, cooling) — other companies' matters touching none of Nvidia's markets? The cooling one mentions Motivair/Schneider liquid cooling market share — AI racks cooling. Does that bear on Nvidia? It mentions \"AI racks\" cooling and single-phase holding 55% of the 2026 liquid cooling market. That's other companies' matters adjacent to the AI rack ecosystem. Per rules, another company's matter that sits next to the company only by sharing an article/ecosystem — not kept, unless supplier capacity/prices. Cooling is a component in Nvidia's racks (BOM mentions cooling). Hmm. But it's an unargued estimate about a third-party market with no tie to Nvidia's costs. It's boilerplate content-farm filler. I'd drop it.\n\nUber/ChatGPT: irrelevant to Nvidia. Drop. PHP CVEs: irrelevant. Drop.\n\nNow check whether existing theses cover any: clean-3 (Vera Rubin shipments by end 2026) — yes for the Q3 2026 shipment schedule.\n\nAny other? clean-6 hyperscaler capex — the item says \"For any organization budgeting AI infrastructure for the second half of 2026 and beyond\" — no capex figure. No.\n\nAlso \"demand is not in question\" — general statement, drop.\n\nSo no new theses. All existing links: only clean-3.\n\nHmm, one more consideration: should the rack price estimate (~$7.8M, up from $4M) inform clean-6 (hyperscaler capex ~$1T in 2027)? Rising per-rack price means the same unit purchases cost nearly double, which mechanically inflates hyperscaler capex dollar totals — that's an inference. The relevance could be \"higher per-rack pricing could inflate hyperscaler capex dollars, but the item gives no capex figure.\" Hmm, is that reasonable? clean-6's outcome is combined 2027 capex. A doubling of rack price is a direct driver of dollar capex for a given unit volume. I think linking with a careful relevance note (\"supports the direction of dollar capex; does not establish unit volumes or 2027 spending\") is defensible. But risk of over-linking. I lean to link evidence 1 to clean-6? Hmm. The instruction: \"Link one statement to every thesis it informs, and to none that its own relevance says it does not inform.\" Does the $7.8M rack price inform the question \"will hyperscaler 2027 capex approach $1T?\" It informs the price side of that spend, which is part of the answer. I'll link it with clear caveat. Actually... hmm, clean-6 is about combined capex; price per rack × units = spend. Higher price per rack raises dollar capex for the same units, so yes it bears on the capex path. I'll link with caveat.\n\nHmm, but also risk: over-linking causes noise. I'll include it with careful phrasing. Actually let me reconsider — I think it's genuinely informative: if racks cost nearly double, hyperscaler capex dollars rise even at flat unit volumes, which is relevant to whether capex approaches $1T in 2027 (though this is 2026 pricing). I'll link.\n\nFor the memory item (2): does it inform clean-6 too? Same logic but weaker. Leave unlinked.\n\nNow figures. CONDITION applies to statements of the company's own financial figures. Item 1: rack price to hyperscalers = Nvidia's revenue per unit — a named party's estimate. GPU price $55k. Include as estimate figures. Item 2: memory content $2M per rack — that's a cost, not the company's financial figure per se (it's BOM cost). Include? It's Nvidia's product BOM, not its revenue/earnings. I'd say leave figures empty for item 2 or include the memory cost as estimate. Hmm. The CONDITION applies to \"the company's own useful financial figures\"... A BOM cost isn't the company's financial figure. I'll leave empty for item 2 to be safe. But then, hmm, the rack price is also the customer's cost/BOM... which is Nvidia's revenue per rack. I'll keep those since they're the company's pricing.\n\nActually, careful: figures field is required to be present (array). For non-company-financial statements, empty array.\n\nLet me draft the evidence items.\n\nEvidence 1:\nsummary: \"Morgan Stanley Research's bill-of-materials analysis estimates that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation; the bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU in volume, and notes a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could cut the rack price to around $6.7 million.\"\nHmm, that's combining statements. Should I split the $55k/$5k into separate item? I said earlier item 1 = rack price + GPU/CPU price. They're in the same two sentences? \"$7.8M ... up from roughly $4 million...\" then \"The GPUs themselves are not the driver... The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\" These are separate statements but closely related single analysis of unit economics. I'll make them one item? The rule \"One evidence item records one statement\" — to be safe, split:\n\nEv1: rack price $7.8M vs $4M (+ potential $6.7M with self-sourced memory). Passages: two sentences — \"$7.8 million...\" and \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million\". Two passages (non-contiguous allowed as separate passages).\n\nEv2: $55,000 per Rubin GPU, $5,000 per Vera CPU in volume.\n\nEv3: memory content $2M/rack, +435%, 54TB LPDDR5X, $1M+ 3D NAND; memory BOM share 5-10%→25-30%, GPU 63%→51%.\n\nEv4: first shipments Q3 2026, volume ramp Q4 → link clean-3.\n\nEv5: contract DDR5 pricing climbed sharply, Samsung/Apple passing memory costs. Maybe merge with Ev3? It's a separate statement about market pricing. I'll keep separate or drop. Hmm. I'll keep it — it's a reported named price movement relevant to Nvidia's input costs. Actually \"Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply\" — no figure, and it's about the broader market. Is it material for Nvidia? Marginally; it supports the memory cost/supply story. Combined with \"Supply constrained... availability and lead times deserve the same planning attention as accelerator allocation\" — that suggests potential supply scheduling risk for Nvidia rack shipments. That bears on clean-3 (can Vera Rubin ship on schedule given memory supply?). Hmm, that's a reasonable link: memory supply constraints as a scheduling risk for VR200 deliveries. I could link Ev3 to clean-3 too with relevance \"memory supply constraints flagged as a scheduling risk that could affect the Q3 2026 first-shipment schedule; item gives no evidence of actual delay.\"\n\nI don't want to over-link. But it's plausible and I'll add it carefully to Ev3? Hmm. Cleaner: Ev3 empty links; Ev5 (supply constraint) — skip linking.\n\nLet me decide: Ev3 links: [] . And include in summary \"the item flags tight memory supply as a scheduling risk, not only a cost\". Then arguably it informs clean-3. I'll add a link to clean-3 with a caveated relevance. Hmm... Risk of contamination is low; the relevance statement is explicit. But linking a cost item to a shipment-timing thesis... I'll skip to keep precision. Actually no — I think the item's own framing (\"Treat memory supply as a scheduling risk\") does bear on whether shipments happen on schedule. But it's a recommendation, not a fact. Keep empty links.\n\nFinal evidence list: 5 items? Let's trim Ev5 (DDR5 pricing / Samsung Apple) — it's about laptop memory pricing, tangential to Nvidia. \"the price of the memory in a laptop are now tied to the same supply squeeze\" — this is the same supply squeeze affecting Nvidia's rack BOM, but it's other companies' matters (Samsung, Apple products) touching Nvidia's market only via a shared article. Hmm, the rule: \"another company's matter that touches none of the company's markets\" — memory supply does touch Nvidia's input costs. But the specific statement \"contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply\" touches Nvidia's LPDDR5X/DDR5 costs. I'll keep it as one item, merged into Ev3 actually? No — different measure. I'll drop it as common-knowledge-ish context (\"a typical claim... without a figure\": \"memory supply tight\" — though a price movement is kept). Ugh.\n\nDecision: keep it as a separate evidence item, tier third_party_view, empty links, no figures. It qualifies as \"a reported movement of a named price\" (kept per rule) and it bears on Nvidia's rack input costs. Fine.\n\nHmm, but is it \"common knowledge\"? The article itself lists it as context. I'll keep it — it's the causal force behind Nvidia's rack cost increase.\n\nActually, to reduce noise, I'll merge Ev5's DDR5 content into Ev3? No — different measure. Keep separate. OK.\n\nNow let's also double check: any statement about Nvidia's own figures/guidance? None — no revenue, no guidance. \"demand is not in question\" — drop (unargued view). \"Nvidia's Vera Rubin platform will deliver a step change in performance\" — boilerplate/view, drop.\n\nStatement dates: none given; the article is 2026-07-06. statement_date null, statement_date_text null. evidence_period: for Ev1 \"VR200 NVL72 generation pricing, compared with GB300 NVL72 (current generation)\" → period: \"Vera Rubin (VR200 NVL72) generation, versus current GB300 NVL72 generation\". For Ev2 same. Ev3 same. Ev4: \"third quarter of 2026 first shipments; volume ramp in fourth quarter of 2026\". Ev5: \"as of mid-2026\" — hmm, \"contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply\" no period. null.\n\nTiers: all third_party_view (Morgan Stanley estimates/reported analysis). Ev4 (shipment schedule) — third_party_view, since the article derives it from Morgan Stanley's analysis? Actually the article says \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026\" as bald fact. Could be fact (a reported plan/decision). Hmm. The article doesn't attribute the schedule. But the source is the same Morgan Stanley analysis. An undated scheduling plan reported by a media outlet → I'd call it third_party_view. Hmm, \"fact: an official action, published actual figure or completed transaction, including an announcement\". A shipment schedule isn't completed. party_statement? Not attributed to Nvidia directly. I'll use third_party_view.\n\nNow new_theses: none. Empty list.\n\nLet me write it.\n\nFor figures on Ev1:\n- measure: \"Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack price to hyperscale cloud providers\", period: \"Vera Rubin generation (vs current GB300 NVL72)\", value: \"$7.8 million\", kind: \"estimate\", basis: \"Morgan Stanley Research bill-of-materials estimate, per rack; prior GB300 NVL72 generation ~$4 million\"\nHmm, each figure needs one value. So:\n1. {measure: \"rack cost per unit\", period: \"Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 generation\", value: \"$7.8 million\", kind: \"estimate\", basis: \"Morgan Stanley Research estimate; hyperscale cloud providers; per rack\"}\n2. {measure: \"rack cost per unit\", period: \"current GB300 NVL72 generation\", value: \"$4 million\", kind: \"estimate\", basis: \"Morgan Stanley Research estimate; hyperscale cloud providers; per rack\"}\n3. {measure: \"rack price with hyperscaler self-sourcing of certain memory modules\", period: \"Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 generation\", value: \"$6.7 million\", kind: \"estimate\", basis: \"Morgan Stanley note; per rack; requires self-sourcing of memory modules\"}\n\nEv2 figures:\n- {measure: \"Rubin GPU unit price\", period: null, value: \"$55,000\", kind: \"estimate\", basis: \"Morgan Stanley estimate; volume pricing, per GPU\"}\n- {measure: \"Vera CPU unit price\", period: null, value: \"$5,000\", kind: \"estimate\", basis: \"Morgan Stanley estimate; volume pricing, per CPU\"}\n\nAre these \"the company's own useful financial figures\"? They are named-party estimates of Nvidia's selling prices. I'll include them; if code rejects, less harm than omission? Well. I'll include.\n\nNote: the CONDITION mostly expects company figures; these are pricing estimates. Fine.\n\nLinks: Ev1 → clean-6? and nothing else. Hmm, and Ev1 → clean-3? no.\n\nActually wait. Let me reconsider linking Ev1 to clean-6. clean-6 asks whether hyperscaler 2027 capex approaches $1T. Ev1 says rack price nearly doubled generation-over-generation ($4M → $7.8M, or $6.7M with self-sourcing). That's a genuine input to dollar capex. I'll link with relevance noting it is price-side only, and note it is a 2026-generation price, not a 2027 capex forecast.\n\nOK write final JSON.\n\nSource passages verbatim — check exact text:\n\n\"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation.\"\n\nNote the odd space in \"Nvidia 's\". Copy exactly.\n\n\"The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\"\n\n\"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\"\n\n\"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\"\n\n\"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n\n\"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n\n\"Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n\n\"Memory supply constraints and tight availability now warrant the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move final pricing by $1+ million per rack\"\n\nOK. Evidence item 3 passages: the \"$2 million ... 435%\" sentence and the \"As a share of the bill of materials...\" sentence.\n\nEvidence item 4 (shipments): \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\"\n\nEvidence item 5 (DDR5): the DDR5 sentence plus maybe \"The memory line is where the cost concentrates\"? No, just DDR5 sentence and maybe \"constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand\" — that is part of a sentence: \"But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market.\" I could include that sentence too. Fine.\n\nDone. Write JSON.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,re; p=\\\"poc/data/clean-step1-gate2-memory-completion-2026-11-13/combined-records.jsonl\\\"; ids={\\\"d1-memory\\\":{1,3,4,5,7,8,9,10,11,13,14,15,16,17,18},\\\"d2-memory\\\":{1,6,7,10,14,18},\\\"d3-memory\\\":{1,7,11,15,16}}; xs=(json.loads(s) for s in open(p));\nfor x in xs:\n if x.get(\\\"arm\\\")\"'!=\"c8\" or x.get(\"case\") not in ids or x.get(\"send\") not in ids[x[\"case\"]]: continue\n print(\"'\"\\\\n###\\\",x[\\\"case\\\"],x[\\\"send\\\"]); t=x[\\\"attempts\\\"][-1][\\\"response\\\"][\\\"choices\\\"][0][\\\"message\\\"][\\\"reasoning_content\\\"]; paras=re.split(r\\\"\\\\n\\\\s*\\\\n\\\",t); matches=[s for s in paras if re.search(r\\\"shared.quantity|settl|contract pric|price series|price level|new thesis.*memory|memory.*new thesis|memory.cost thesis\\\",s,re.I)];\n for s in matches: print(re.sub(r\\\"\\\\s+\\\",\\\" \\\",s)[:1700])'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,re; p=\\\"poc/data/clean-step1-gate2-memory-completion-2026-11-13/combined-records.jsonl\\\"; ids={\\\"d1-memory\\\":{1,3,4,5,7,8,9,10,11,13,14,15,16,17,18},\\\"d2-memory\\\":{1,6,7,10,14,18},\\\"d3-memory\\\":{1,7,11,15,16}}; xs=(json.loads(s) for s in open(p));\nfor x in xs:\n if x.get(\\\"arm\\\")\"'!=\"c8\" or x.get(\"case\") not in ids or x.get(\"send\") not in ids[x[\"case\"]]: continue\n print(\"'\"\\\\n###\\\",x[\\\"case\\\"],x[\\\"send\\\"]); t=x[\\\"attempts\\\"][-1][\\\"response\\\"][\\\"choices\\\"][0][\\\"message\\\"][\\\"reasoning_content\\\"]; paras=re.split(r\\\"\\\\n\\\\s*\\\\n\\\",t); matches=[s for s in paras if re.search(r\\\"shared.quantity|settl|contract pric|price series|price level|new thesis.*memory|memory.*new thesis|memory.cost thesis\\\",s,re.I)];\n for s in matches: print(re.sub(r\\\"\\\\s+\\\",\\\" \\\",s)[:1700])'\"","aggregated_output":"\n### d1-memory 1\n4. Memory cost per rack ~$2 million, a 435% jump; LPDDR5X capacity tripled to 54 TB; ~$1 million+ in 3D NAND. These are supply/input cost signals. Component costs — memory supply constraints. This informs clean-3 (Vera Rubin) and possibly a thesis about input costs / bill of materials. Does any open thesis cover input costs? clean-1 credit support, clean-2 guarantee, clean-3 shipments, clean-4 hyperscaler capex. None specifically on input costs/BOM. This could be a new thesis? But input costs (memory) — is it a \"shared_quantity\"? The price of memory (DRAM/NAND/HBM) is a quantity/price outside any one company that several companies depend on. \"next year's average contract price of a raw material.\" Hmm. But this is a component cost. Could open a shared_quantity thesis: \"What will memory (DRAM/NAND/HBM) contract prices be for AI servers in 2026-2027?\" But is that within Nvidia scope? It affects Nvidia input costs but Nvidia isn't the one buying memory — the rack OEM (Foxconn etc.) or Nvidia buys? Actually Nvidia designs the rack; memory is bought by Nvidia's supply chain. The cost of memory affects the total rack price which affects Nvidia's revenue per rack. Hmm.\nLet me consider whether to propose a new thesis about memory/input costs or the rack pricing. Given the rules: \"Do not open a thesis about a financial measure's level, growth, margin or guidance for any period, on its own.\" The rack price is a product price — but \"the company's decision to change the price it charges\" is a product detail that's not kept as a thesis. Hmm.\n5. \"memory supply constraints and tight availability now warrant the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation\" — general, and the DRAM/NAND/HBM supply squeeze. \"Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, device makers including Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\" This is about memory market, affecting component costs. Could be a shared_quantity thesis on memory contract prices. But is it material enough / does it bear on Nvidia? Memory supply tightness could constrain Vera Rubin rack production/deliveries — informs clean-3 (shipment timeline risk). Also input costs.\nEvidence B: Morgan Stanley estimates Nvidia will charge ~$55,000 per Rubin GPU and ~$5,000 per Vera CPU at volume. Third-party estimate. Figures: $55,000 per Rubin GPU (estimate), $5,000 per Vera CPU (estimate). Links: clean-3? This is about Vera Rubin pricing. Yes informs clean-3 and maybe clean-4. Actually pricing of the GPU bears on revenue and on clean-4 capex. I'll link clean-3 and clean-4. Hmm, clean-4 is aggregated capex of hyperscalers reaching $1T — GPU pricing informs whether capex reaches that. And clean-3 is shipments. Pricing alone doesn't settle shipments but informs the product-node thesis (revenue magnitude). The product_node thesis is about first shipments timing, not pricing. Pricing doesn't inform timing. Does it inform clean-4? Higher GPU prices → more capex for same units. Marginally. Let me link to clean-4 as the value-per-unit estimate that bears on the capex aggregate. And maybe link clean-3 too since it's the product carrying value. I think linking both is defensible. But I should be careful — the product_node thesis clean-3 is specifically about timing of first shipments, and pricing does not inform timing. Better to link clean-4 (cale of AI infrastructure spend). Hmm, but clean-4 is a specific aggregate. Pricing per GPU is a component. I'll link clean-4.\nCandidate 1: Memory/input-cost thesis — \"What will DRAM/NAND/HBM contract prices be in 2026-2027, given the AI-driven squeeze?\" This is a shared_quantity (a price outside any one company several companies depend on, next calendar year). Nvidia depends on memory prices as rack cost. But is this within the company's scope and would an analyst bet on it separately for Nvidia? The rack cost bears on Nvidia's product cost competitiveness. Hmm. But the memory price is more of a supplier (Micron/SK Hynix/Samsung) matter. For Nvidia, memory is part of the rack BOM but Nvidia doesn't directly buy/sell it. Actually who bears the memory cost? If Nvidia sells the full rack (NVL72 rack) to hyperscalers, Nvidia is buying the memory and selling the rack. Does Nvidia sell full racks? Nvidia sells the rack-scale system, so Nvidia's BOM includes memory. So memory costs affect Nvidia's product cost and could affect its gross margin or its ability to price. That's significant! But wait — Morgan Stanley says hyperscalers pay $7.8M per rack and GPU is $55k×72=~$3.96M, CPU $5k×36=... So Nvidia sells the GPU/CPU and the OEM integrates? Actually Nvidia's rack includes everything. Hmm.\nIf Nvidia sells full racks, memory cost is Nvidia's input cost, affecting its gross margin. That's material. But would an analyst bet separately on \"will memory contract prices rise, squeezing Nvidia's rack gross margin\"? That could be a shared_quantity thesis (memory contract price). But is that Nvidia-specific enough / does the item support a settle date? The item gives no explicit contract price forecast. It says \"contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply.\" The shared_quantity would be \"average contract price of DRAM/NAND in 2026-2027.\" This is more a Micron thesis. For Nvidia, I'd link memory cost evidence to... there's no open thesis on Nvidia gross margin or input costs. \nHmm, the question is whether an analyst following Nvidia would hold a separate card for \"Nvidia's memory input costs / rack BOM margin.\" I think it's borderline. The item frames it as a cost for infrastructure buyers (hyperscalers), not Nvidia's margin necessarily. Actually the rack price is what hyperscalers pay; the memory is included. Whether Nvidia or the OEM buys memory — for NVL72, Nvidia and partners build the rack. Nvidia's revenue is the GPU+CPU+networking. Memory is often sourced by the OEM/ODM. So memory cost may not be Nvidia's gross margin. I'll avoid opening a memory-cost thesis for Nvidia because it's not clearly Nvidia's revenue/profit driver and no clean settle date.\nLet me verify no open thesis is left uncovered that should get a new thesis. The three main facts: rack cost (BOM) → clean-3/clean-4; GPU pricing → clean-4; shipment schedule → clean-3; memory supply → clean-3/clean-4. Good.\nActually wait, is there an argument that the rack cost doubling and memory squeeze raises a question about Nvidia's own gross margin or rack-level profitability — a thesis about Nvidia's product cost/margin? \"Do not open a thesis about a financial measure's level, growth, margin or guidance for any period, on its own.\" So no margin thesis. Good, no new thesis.\n\n### d1-memory 5\n3. Memory cost surge 435%, memory now 25-30% of BOM — input cost / supply. This could relate to clean-3 product economics or a shared_quantity thesis about memory prices? There's a thesis about memory supplies. Actually memory price is a shared quantity (DRAM/NAND prices several companies depend on). Should we propose a new thesis about memory supply? The rules: shared_quantity is about a quantity/price outside any one company that several companies depend on, for this or next calendar year. Memory (DRAM/NAND/HBM) contract pricing - is that a big matter affecting NVDA? Yes, memory is 25-30% of rack cost. But is this \"a quantity or price outside any one company that several companies depend on\"? Memory prices affect NVDA, but also Samsung, Apple, etc. So it qualifies as shared_quantity. But would an analyst bet on it separately? The item reports contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply. Hmm, this could be a new thesis: \"how high will DRAM/NAND/HBM contract prices go in 2027\" affecting NVDA's input costs. But is memory an input cost for NVDA, or a component NVDA resells? For the rack, memory is part of the system NVDA sells, so it's a cost component. Tight memory supply could constrain NVDA's ability to ship Vera Rubin. This is a genuine research question.\nLet me consider whether to propose a new thesis. The rules on do-not-open: \"an annual quantity after next year\" excepted... memory price for next calendar year is allowed as shared_quantity. But is the memory price something an analyst bets on separately for NVDA? The item gives contract DDR5 pricing climbed sharply, memory 25-30% of BOM. This is about NVDA's input costs (well, its system cost). Could change NVDA's profitability on racks.\nHowever, memory prices affect many companies, and NVDA's dependency is real. I think a shared_quantity thesis could be justified: \"How much will DRAM/HBM/NAND contract prices rise in 2027, raising NVDA's Vera Rubin rack BOM/cost?\" But careful: the rule says shared_quantity takes its release date. The item doesn't give a clear release date for memory prices. Hmm. \"Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply\" — no specific figure. This is a general statement of price movement. It might be background/common knowledge territory. The item gives \"435% jump\" for memory content per rack, which is a real figure. And \"memory rose from 5-10% to 25-30%\". \nNow, should I propose a new thesis about memory? The item strongly emphasizes memory as a strategic budget variable, supply constraints. A senior analyst following NVDA would care: does memory supply constrain Vera Rubin shipments, and does memory cost pressure NVDA's margins? But this is arguably covered by clean-3? clean-3 asks whether Vera Rubin begins first shipments by end 2026. Memory supply constraint is a risk to that. So it informs clean-3.\nLet me also consider the \"contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs\" — this is about other companies and general memory market. Does it inform NVDA? It's about the industry memory supply squeeze that affects NVDA. But it's a general statement without a figure for NVDA. The rule: \"a reported movement of a named price... even without a figure.\" Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply — a reported movement of a named price. Material as shared_quantity? It's memory prices affecting NVDA. But it's background common knowledge about memory market? It's a reported movement. Hmm. I could include it linked to clean-3 or a memory thesis. But it names no figure. Let me include it as part of the memory supply evidence or skip. I'll fold it into E4 (memory supply tightness).\nNow let me reconsider proposing a new thesis on memory supply/pricing. \nOpens_as: shared_quantity (memory prices/supply is a quantity/price outside any one company that several companies depend on, for this/next calendar year). Settled_by: memory contract price data, supplier earnings (Samsung/SK Hynix/Micron), or NVDA's disclosures. Period: 2027? The item doesn't clearly give a release date. This makes it weak.\nActually, let me reconsider. The item gives contract DDR5 pricing climbed, memory 25-30% of BOM. If I propose a shared_quantity thesis about memory contract prices in 2027, settle