nd it's \"climbed sharply\" — a reported movement of a named price.\nThe item says contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply but gives no figure and no forecast for next year.\nBut no figures, undated.\nThe item gives no specific quantity/price level for a period.\nSection 3 says \"Do not open a thesis about...\non its own.\" And shared_quantity takes its release date and only when the item supports one.\nThe item doesn't give a release date.\nWithout a checkable figure, it fails \"public material will settle it on a date.\" I'll not open it.\n- shared_quantity: hyperscaler capex covered by clean-4; memory contract price could be shared quantity but no figure/date.\n\n### d2-memory 7\nWould be \"will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin rack gross margin...\" — a financial measure's margin, do not open.\nBut no figure and no specific next-year quantity; \"memory now accounting for 25-30% of BOM\" — that's specific.\n\n### d2-memory 10\nThat's a margin question → don't open.\nThis could be a shared_quantity thesis: \"What will be the average contract price of DRAM (or memory) in calendar 2026-2027?\" Settled by: TrendForce/DRAMeXchange contract price releases.\nBut the item doesn't give a clear settlement release date for the average contract price.\nA shared_quantity \"takes its release date.\" Is there a release date?\nPossibly next TrendForce/DRAM contract price report.\nSo \"settled_by\" would be inferred—I'd have to name TrendForce release, which the item doesn't support.\nThe instruction: \"A shared quantity takes its release date...\nonly when the item supports one.\" The item doesn't support a specific release date for memory prices.\nI'll not propose a shared_quantity thesis due to lack of a supportable date.\nActually, wait—could I propose it with period = calendar 2026-2027 and settled_by = \"TrendForce/DRAMeXchange quarterly contract price reports\"?\nThe item doesn't mention TrendForce.\nThe rule: settled_by should be \"public material that will settle the answer on a date (a filing, an official decision, a shipment confirmation, a statistics release).\" Memory contract prices ARE published by TrendForce/DRAMeXchange.\nas supported by the item\" and \"A shared quantity takes its release date.\" Without the item supporting a date, risky.\n\n### d2-memory 14\nHowever, the \"do not open\" list includes \"a financial measure's level, growth, margin or guidance for any period, on its own.\" A commodity price is not a financial measure?\nSettled_by: \"Memory contract price reports (e.g., DRAMeXchange/TrendForce monthly/quarterly DDR5, DRAM, NAND and HBM contract prices) and memory makers' earnings disclosures.\" Period: \"Calendar 2026, checked at end-2026.\" Opens_as: shared_quantity.\nBut the item gives no specific memory type.\nMaybe too broad.\nSettled by DRAMeXchange/TrendForce.\nThe prompt says \"a vague or noisy topic with no outcome to watch is not proposed.\" The memory price topic is a bit vague.\nBetter not propose.\n\n### d2-memory 18\nCould open a thesis: \"How high will DRAM/NAND/HBM contract prices rise in 2026?\" But the item gives no number for the price and no specific release date/figure.\nFor a thesis we need \"public material that will settle it on a date.\" Memory price indices (e.g., DRAMeXchange) get published monthly.\nI'm inclined not to open a memory-price thesis because the item gives no figure or concrete date to settle, and it risks being vague.\nBut I'll lean toward not opening; keep memory supply as evidence with empty links.\nBut is there a clean settlement date and public material?\nMemory contract prices are tracked by TrendForce/DRAMeXchange quarterly.\nBut does the item support a check date?\nIt doesn't give a release date.\nShared quantity \"takes its release date\" — the item doesn't give one.\nHmm, that's a problem: \"A shared quantity takes its release date.\" The item gives no release date for memory pricing.\nshared_quantity needs a release date.\n\n### d3-memory 1\nBut it's not really a single quantity and the item gives no figures for contract prices.\nI'd not open.\nActually a price point of a product already on sale is not open (\"a feature, content or software update, price point, regional service or variant of a product already on sale\").\n\n### d3-memory 7\nFor the shipment schedule item, no figures?\nIt states \"Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply\" but no figure.\nToo broad.\nBut no figure, and it's a journalistic assessment.\n\n### d3-memory 11\nA thesis: \"What will contract prices for LPDDR5X/DRAM memory be in calendar 2027, given AI-driven demand and tight supply?\" Period/check date: memory makers' quarterly results or TrendForce releases — item doesn't name one.\nThe rule says shared quantity takes its release date only when the item supports one; otherwise a question no date settles is rewritten or not opened.\nSettled_by: TrendForce/DRAMeXchange contract price releases, or memory makers' (Samsung/SK Hynix/Micron) quarterly results.\nCheck date: Q4 2026 memory contract price?\nThe item supports \"the second half of 2026 and beyond\" for budgeting but no release date.\nThe rule: \"A shared quantity takes its release date ...\nonly when the item supports one.\" The item doesn't name a release date.\nThen \"A question no date can settle is rewritten or not opened.\" I can rewrite: \"Will contract prices for the memory inside AI racks (LPDDR5X/3D NAND/DRAM) be higher in Q4 2026 than in Q2 2026...?\" — the item gives a directional claim (climbed sharply, supply tight) but no dates for release.\nI could set the period as \"calendar 2026–2027 memory contract pricing, checked at memory suppliers' Q4 2026 results\" — that's inventing a check date but \"the current fiscal year-end\" alternative isn't in the rules for shared quantity.\nBut there's also the \"do not open\" list: \"a financial measure's level, growth, margin or guidance for any period, on its own\" — memory contract price is not a financial measure of Nvidia; it's a market price.\nI'll not propose it.\n\n### d3-memory 15\n\"Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply\" no figure.\nNo figures.\nThat's a margin/financial measure \"on its own\" — do not open.\nThe rules say don't open a thesis about \"a financial measure's level, growth, margin or guidance for any period, on its own.\" So no.\nFor evidence 2 figures: none (DDR5 pricing \"climbed sharply\" no figure).\nI'll not open.\n\n### d3-memory 16\nSection 3 shared_quantity: \"a quantity or price outside any one company that several companies depend on, for this or next calendar year: several companies' combined output or spending, a contract price, an interest rate.\" A memory contract price (DDR5 contract pricing) — the item says \"Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply\" but no figure.\nCould propose: \"Will contract DDR5/LPDDR5X prices...?\" But no figure and no date set.\n\"Do not open a thesis about: a financial measure's level, growth, margin or guidance for any period, on its own.\" Rack price is a product price point — also \"a price point...\nHmm, does that count as price point (do not open)?\nActually \"Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply\" — no figure, and it's about the broader market.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,re; p=\\\"poc/data/clean-step1-gate2-memory-completion-2026-11-13/combined-records.jsonl\\\"; ids={\\\"d1-memory\\\":{3,11,15,17,18},\\\"d2-memory\\\":{7,14},\\\"d3-memory\\\":{1,7,15}};\nfor x in sorted(map(json.loads,open(p)),key=lambda x:(x.get(\\\"case\\\",\\\"\\\"),x.get(\\\"send\\\",0))):\n if x.get(\\\"arm\\\")\"'!=\"c8\" or x.get(\"case\") not in ids or x.get(\"send\") not in ids[x[\"case\"]]: continue\n print(\"'\"\\\\n###\\\",x[\\\"case\\\"],x[\\\"send\\\"]); t=x[\\\"attempts\\\"][-1][\\\"response\\\"][\\\"choices\\\"][0][\\\"message\\\"][\\\"reasoning_content\\\"]; ps=re.split(r\\\"\\\\n\\\\s*\\\\n\\\",t); a=[s for s in ps if re.search(r\\\"memory|DDR|DRAM|shared.quantity\\\",s,re.I) and re.search(r\\\"thesis|question|open|propos\\\",s,re.I)];\n for s in a[-4:]: print(re.sub(r\\\"\\\\s+\\\",\\\" \\\",s)[:2000])'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,re; p=\\\"poc/data/clean-step1-gate2-memory-completion-2026-11-13/combined-records.jsonl\\\"; ids={\\\"d1-memory\\\":{3,11,15,17,18},\\\"d2-memory\\\":{7,14},\\\"d3-memory\\\":{1,7,15}};\nfor x in sorted(map(json.loads,open(p)),key=lambda x:(x.get(\\\"case\\\",\\\"\\\"),x.get(\\\"send\\\",0))):\n if x.get(\\\"arm\\\")\"'!=\"c8\" or x.get(\"case\") not in ids or x.get(\"send\") not in ids[x[\"case\"]]: continue\n print(\"'\"\\\\n###\\\",x[\\\"case\\\"],x[\\\"send\\\"]); t=x[\\\"attempts\\\"][-1][\\\"response\\\"][\\\"choices\\\"][0][\\\"message\\\"][\\\"reasoning_content\\\"]; ps=re.split(r\\\"\\\\n\\\\s*\\\\n\\\",t); a=[s for s in ps if re.search(r\\\"memory|DDR|DRAM|shared.quantity\\\",s,re.I) and re.search(r\\\"thesis|question|open|propos\\\",s,re.I)];\n for s in a[-4:]: print(re.sub(r\\\"\\\\s+\\\",\\\" \\\",s)[:2000])'\"","aggregated_output":"\n### d1-memory 3\nHmm, but leaving E2 unlinked means it's \"material but no thesis takes it.\" Is memory BOM material? Yes, memory cost jump is material to understanding NVIDIA rack economics. Fine, unlinked.\nPotential new thesis: memory price / memory supply as shared_quantity? \"a quantity or price outside any one company that several companies depend on, for this or next calendar year: ... a contract price.\" Memory (DDR5/LPDDR5X/HBM) contract price is a shared quantity several companies (NVIDIA, hyperscalers, memory makers) depend on. But would an analyst bet on NVIDIA separately on memory price? The item gives \"memory 25-30% of rack, +435%\". This affects NVIDIA's input cost but is passed through. Hmm. A thesis: \"Will DRAM/HBM contract prices rise X in 2026?\" That's more a memory-maker thesis. For NVIDIA, memory cost is a component it may resell. Is this a big matter changing NVIDIA's revenue/profit/cash? If memory is passed through at cost in the rack price, it doesn't change NVIDIA margin much. The item frames it as the customer's budget variable, not NVIDIA's margin. So probably not a separate NVIDIA thesis. And clean-4 (capex) partly captures the cost side. I won't propose a new memory price thesis.\nPotential new thesis: Vera Rubin pricing/revenue per rack? Doesn't fit the four opens_as reasons (not product_node—that's shipments covered by clean-3; not obligation; not shared_quantity; not policy). So no.\nNow let me also double check: the article's opening line \"Nvidia's next-generation Vera Rubin rack will cost hyperscalers $7.8 million—nearly double the prior generation—with memory now accounting for 25-30% of total cost instead of 5-10%.\" This is a summary repeating E1/E2. It's the same fact said again — don't make separate item. Good, I use the detailed passages.\n\n### d1-memory 11\nNow, does the item raise any new thesis? Candidates: - Will Vera Rubin rack ASP/hyperscaler budget... covered by clean-3 (shipments). - Memory cost/input cost for Nvidia's racks — a new thesis? \"Do not open about a financial measure's level, growth, margin or guidance for any period, on its own\" — a thesis about Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack price? The price the company charges... Price point of product already on sale excluded. Vera Rubin is a new product generation, not yet shipping. Could be a product_node thesis: \"Will Vera Rubin first shipments occur by Q3 2026?\" — that's clean-3 already.\nWhat about the item's implied question: how much of the rack's cost is memory, i.e., BOM cost structure — not a thesis.\nIs this kept? It's a reported movement of a named price (contract DDR5 pricing) — kept per rules. Does it inform a thesis? Nvidia's input costs/supply of memory (HBM). No open thesis on Nvidia's memory supply/input costs. Could be evidence with empty links, or link to clean-3? Memory supply constraints bear on whether Vera Rubin shipments can ramp. Hmm, the item says memory supply constraints warrant same planning attention as GPU allocation, and memory is 25-30% of rack value. For Nvidia, memory availability is a supply constraint for shipping racks. That's an inference but reasonably direct: the item ties constrained memory supply to the VR200 rack cost/availability. I'll link to clean-3 with hedged relevance: memory supply tightness is a potential constraint on Vera Rubin shipment ramp; item gives no evidence of allocation to Nvidia.\nNow check for a possible new thesis: \"Will the VR200 NVL72 rack price...\" no. \"Memory cost share\" no.\n\n### d1-memory 15\nWait, but I should double-check: does the memory cost / GPU pricing relate to clean-4 (capex)? Let me think once more if a senior analyst would link the \"$7.8M rack, memory is now 25-30% of cost\" to the hyperscaler capex thesis. clean-4 is about the TOTAL capex reaching $1T. The rack cost doesn't answer that; it's about composition. I'll keep it out.\nHmm, let me reconsider one more time about whether I'm being too conservative and should keep the DDR5 statement. The closing check says: keep when \"you would write it in this week's notes because it makes some question's answer more or less likely.\" For NVIDIA, the DDR5/memory squeeze raising component costs → could bear on NVIDIA's input costs/gross margins and on the rack price. I already have the memory cost item covering that. The DDR5 consumer angle is redundant/general. I'll drop.\nWait, should B (memory cost) link to anything? It's about input cost / BOM. No open thesis on margins/input costs. Empty. But actually, is B material enough? The memory being 25-30% of rack cost and rising—this could affect NVIDIA's gross margin if it bears memory costs, or it's just pass-through in the ASP. It's a cost-structure data point about NVIDIA's flagship product. I'll keep it, empty.\nShould I open a thesis about memory input cost being a shared quantity (DRAM/NAND contract prices several companies depend on)? The item doesn't provide a specific aggregate price/period to check. It's vague (\"climbed sharply\"). Can't set settled_by date/quantity. Skip.\n\n### d1-memory 17\nIs there a separate thesis worth opening about memory being a strategic budget variable / memory costs? That's vague and about other companies (Samsung, Apple). Not about Nvidia's business directly. The memory cost is an input cost to the rack, but Nvidia's revenue is the GPU/CPU price. The memory cost is borne by... actually who bears the memory cost? The rack BOM is what hyperscalers pay; Nvidia sells the rack. So if memory is $2M and GPU is 51%, the $7.8M includes memory that Nvidia buys and integrates. So memory cost affects Nvidia's rack cost (input cost) and thus potentially its margin. Hmm. That could be a thesis: \"How much will memory input costs affect Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack margins?\" But that's a margin question — \"a financial measure's level, growth, margin or guidance for any period, on its own\" — do not open. So no.\nWhere do E1, E2, E3 link then? - E1 ($7.8M rack cost): clean-4 (capex). - E2 ($55k GPU, $5k CPU): Hmm. This is Nvidia's product pricing. No open thesis about Nvidia's pricing/revenue per unit except... none. clean-3 is timing, clean-4 is capex magnitude. Does $55k/GPU inform clean-4? It's part of rack cost. Indirectly. I could link E2 to clean-4 (Nvidia's portion of the rack cost that feeds into capex). Hmm, but the capex is what hyperscalers spend; whether it goes to Nvidia GPUs or memory doesn't change the capex total. So $55k/GPU doesn't inform total capex magnitude. It informs Nvidia's revenue share. No thesis covers Nvidia revenue per unit. \nNow, is there a new thesis worth opening? Let me reconsider the \"memory supply constraints\" angle. Could open a shared_quantity thesis: \"What will the average contract price of LPDDR5X/HBM memory be in 2027, a component that determines the cost of Nvidia's racks and several hyperscalers depend on?\" Hmm. The item doesn't give a contract price forecast or a specific shared quantity for a defined period. It says memory content per rack $2M and 435% jump. That's about the rack, not a market-wide contract price. opening a shared_quantity thesis would require a specific quantity/price outside any one company that several depend on for this or next year, settled by a release. The item doesn't provide that. So no.\nActually, I realize I should be more careful. Let me re-read CONDITION: \"a statement of the company's own financial figures (a reported result or balance, its guidance, or a named party's or consensus estimate) lists its useful figures in figures. Its results, guidance and estimates, margins included, inform an open thesis about its revenue or earnings path when they bear on that path.\" So the memory cost is a named party's estimate of a cost component. It bears on Nvidia's earnings path (margin). So include. Fine.\n\n### d1-memory 18\nWait, but is that right? Let me reconsider the purpose: \"new theses: the important research questions this item raises that no open thesis covers.\" The item raises: is memory supply going to constrain Vera Rubin ramp? That could be a research question. \"Will memory supply constraints delay or limit Vera Rubin volume ramp in 2027?\" That's a real question — it could change Nvidia revenue timing. But it's already partly covered by clean-3 (shipments timing)? Clean-3 is about first shipments by end 2026. Memory constraint affecting ramp is related but asks about volume ramp, arguably distinct. But \"before proposing, name closest open thesis and why separate.\" The memory supply question can move apart from \"first shipment before end 2026\"? First shipment could happen on time while volume ramp is limited by memory. Potentially separate. But is it \"big enough\" and does it fit the four reasons? It's not a product_node (that's first shipments = clean-3), not binding_obligation, not shared_quantity (memory supply could be shared_quantity—\"the amount of DRAM/HBM available in 2027\"—a quantity several companies depend on!). Hmm.\nThis is getting speculative. I think opening a memory shared_quantity thesis is defensible but risky given the item lacks a specific number and clear period/date. The guidelines emphasize not to open vague topics. I'll not open it.\nActually, let me keep it clean: link E1 to clean-4 as cost pressure raising dollar capex per unit. Leave E2 (memory cost), E4 (BOM shares), E5 (procurement) unlinked—they're cost-structure detail, and linking all would overreach. But E2 is the memory cost which is a big driver of the price that supports clean-4... The narrow relevant thesis for \"why rack costs more\" is the same capex question. I could link E2 to clean-4 too. To avoid over-linking, I'll link E1 and E2 to clean-4? Hmm.\nHmm, but E2 is the largest driver of increased system cost. If I link E1 (total price) to clean-4 because higher price = higher capex, then E2 (memory driving that price) equally bears. For consistency I might link E2 too. But okay, let me just link E1 to clean-4 only, since E1 is the headline system price. Actually inconsistency is a problem. Let me reconsider: maybe don't link E1 to clean-4 either. The thesis asks whether aggregate reaches $1T; the item gives no aggregate and no volumes. Higher rack price raises dollars per unit but a fixed budget buys fewer racks, so aggregate could be unchanged. So the rack price does NOT clearly inform the aggregate — it's ambiguous. Therefore linking is not well-supported. I'll NOT link to clean-4. Good—this resolves it.\n\n### d2-memory 7\nAlso: \"Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\" — that's about memory market, other companies. Bears on NVIDIA? Not directly; it's a shared input cost/macro-ish. Samsung/Apple are not NVIDIA's suppliers per se (Samsung is a memory supplier to NVIDIA potentially, but the sentence is about laptops). Hmm, \"The AI buildout and the price of the memory in a laptop are now tied to the same supply squeeze.\" Would an analyst write it in notes? Maybe as context on memory costs. It's a reported movement of a named price (\"Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply\") — kept per the rules (\"a reported movement of a named price... even without a figure\"). Does it inform a thesis? Memory supply constraints bear on NVIDIA's rack cost and supply — no thesis. Could link to... nothing. Hmm, is it material enough? Memory cost is 25-30% of a rack BOM, so contract DDR5 pricing trends bear on NVIDIA's cost structure. I could keep it as evidence with empty links. But it's a bit of a stretch — DRAM/NAND contract pricing. I think it's keepable: a reported movement of a named price in a market relevant to the company's input costs.\nNow consider whether a new thesis is warranted for memory cost / rack BOM. Under section 3, no: a cost-structure question doesn't fit the four reasons (product_node, binding_obligation, shared_quantity, policy_stage). Could \"memory cost per rack\" be a shared_quantity? \"a quantity or price outside any one company that several companies depend on, for this or next calendar year: several companies' combined output or spending, a contract price, an interest rate.\" The contract DDR5 price / DRAM-NAND-HBM supply constraint is a price outside any one company that several companies depend on. Hmm — \"next year's average contract price of a raw material\" is the example. The item says contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and memory supply constrained. But no figure and no specific next-year quantity; \"memory now accounting for 25-30% of BOM\" — that's specific. Could I open a shared_quantity thesis: \"What will the average contract price of DRAM/NAND/HBM be in 2027?\" — no, the item doesn't give a shared quantity for a specific calendar year, and it's not really tied to NVIDIA's value. I think that's overreach. Skip new theses.\nCould the memory cost inform clean-4 (hyperscaler capex)? Higher rack prices → capex dollars per unit capacity. clean-4 asks about combined capex in dollar terms. The rack price affects how much capacity $X buys, which is the analyst's use but clean-4's outcome is the dollar figure. I'd say the memory supply constraint / rack cost doesn't inform the dollar capex question... arguably it informs whether hyperscalers' spend approaches $1T — no. Skip.\nHmm, maybe I should drop E6 entirely as adjacent-market noise. The read-back says: keep when you'd write it in this week's notes because it makes some question's answer more or less likely about the company or its suppliers/competitors. Memory price inflation affects NVIDIA's rack BOM cost — that's supply/input cost. Yes, I'd write it. Keep.\n\n### d2-memory 14\nLet's see if there is any other new thesis candidate: \"Will Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack price be $7.8M?\" Not one of four. \"Will Nvidia charge $55k per Rubin GPU?\" No. \"Will hyperscalers self-source memory modules to lower rack price to $6.7M?\" That's a procurement decision, not one of four. \"Will memory supply constraints delay VR200 volume ramp?\" This is a product node but clean-2 covers first shipments. Not separate. So no new theses.\nNow links: E1: clean-4 only. E2: clean-4 only. E3: [] or clean-4? Let's decide. The BOM composition shift (memory share up, GPU share down) is relevant to hyperscaler capex? It shows memory is a larger portion of spend, which affects where capex goes, but clean-4 is total capex. It doesn't inform total. I'd leave empty. But if an analyst following clean-4 wants to know cost drivers, it's relevant. The relevance could be \"composition of per-rack cost, not aggregate spend.\" The instruction says link if it informs. It informs the composition, not the total. The thesis asks total. So no link. Leave empty. E4: []? The per-GPU/CPU pricing is part of rack cost. It could inform clean-4's cost per rack. But E1 already gives total. E4 is more granular. Does it inform total hyperscaler capex? It is a component. An analyst would use it to model Nvidia revenue, not hyperscaler capex. Leave empty. E5: clean-2. E6: clean-4. E7: clean-2, clean-4.\nShould E2 link to clean-2? The memory content per rack is a feature of the VR200 rack. The thesis clean-2 asks first shipments. It doesn't inform timing. No.\nShould E7 link to clean-2? Yes as supply risk. Could the memory supply constraint make first shipments less likely? It is a risk. The open thesis clean-2 outcome is first shipments by end 2026. The article says first shipments Q3 2026, memory supply tight. It doesn't say Nvidia can't get memory. But it's relevant. Link with caveat. Good.\n\n### d3-memory 1\nHmm, is this worth keeping? The closing check: a reported movement of a named price (contract DDR5 pricing) is kept per rules. Yes, keep. It informs Nvidia's rack input costs → no thesis. Kept, empty links.\nAlso: is there a thesis about Nvidia's memory supply risk being a constraint on Vera Rubin shipments? clean-3 \"will first shipments begin by end of 2026\" — memory supply tightness could affect that. Hmm, arguably memory supply constraints are a scheduling risk for Vera Rubin rack shipments → informs clean-3's timing question. That's a reasonable link: tight memory supply could delay rack shipments. I'll link the memory supply tightness statement to clean-3 with appropriate relevance framing (\"source says memory supply is tight, a scheduling risk for rack builds; inference that it could affect Vera Rubin shipment timing\"). Hmm, be careful not to overreach. I think it's acceptable and useful. Actually the relevance field asks how evidence informs the thesis, distinguishing source claims from inference. So I can link memory supply tightness to clean-3 noting inference.\nNow, does the $7.8M rack price support a new thesis? Section 3 allows shared_quantity \"a contract price\" — no, rack price is Nvidia's own product price, and do-not-open says a financial measure's level... Actually a price point of a product already on sale is not open (\"a feature, content or software update, price point, regional service or variant of a product already on sale\"). Vera Rubin isn't on sale, but product_node covers the generation reaching first shipments, already tracked by clean-3. So no new thesis. Good — no new theses at all.\nLet me double check whether any of the four reasons gives a new thesis: - product_node: Vera Rubin first shipments → clean-3 already covers. Kyber → clean-7. - binding_obligation: none (hyperscaler self-sourcing is hypothetical, not Nvidia's obligation). - shared_quantity: hyperscaler capex → clean-6. Memory price? Not \"for this or next calendar year\" with a figure. No. - policy_stage: none.\n\n### d3-memory 7\nMaybe a new thesis about memory supply as a constraint on AI rack shipments? That's not one of four reasons. So no.\nE1 figures as above. links: [ {\"target_type\":\"existing\",\"target_id\":\"clean-3\",\"relevance\":\"Provides Morgan Stanley's estimated per-rack price and memory content for the Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 generation whose first shipments the thesis tracks; it does not confirm shipment timing.\"}, {\"target_type\":\"existing\",\"target_id\":\"clean-6\",\"relevance\":\"A higher per-rack system cost is a direct input to hyperscaler AI infrastructure budgets the thesis tracks; it does not by itself establish total 2027 capex.\"} ]\nE3: summary: \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs, with contract DDR5 pricing climbing sharply; it says memory supply tightness and lead times for AI racks now deserve the same scheduling attention as accelerator allocation.\" source_passages: \"Only a handful of companies buy racks at this scale. But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\" \"Treat memory supply as a scheduling risk, not only a cost. With memory representing a quarter or more of rack value and supply tight, availability and lead times deserve the same planning attention as accelerator allocation.\" original_party \"MarketScale\" reporting_speaker \"MarketScale\" tier \"third_party_view\" statement_date \"2026-07-06\" statement_date_text null evidence_period \"2026\" figures [] links [clean-3, clean-6]. Relevance clean-3: \"Memory supply tightness and lead times could pose a scheduling risk to Vera Rubin rack shipments the thesis tracks.\" clean-6: \"Rising memory costs are part of the AI infrastructure cost stack that feeds hyperscaler capex budgets.\"\nDo we need to include a new thesis for memory? Let's decide final. I think no new theses. The response requires new_theses array. Empty.\n\n### d3-memory 15\nDo I propose any new thesis? The item is basically a Morgan Stanley BOM analysis about Vera Rubin cost and memory. Vera Rubin shipping is covered by clean-3. Cost/pricing is not an open thesis. Could I propose a thesis on Vera Rubin rack pricing/gross margin? That's a margin/financial measure \"on its own\" — do not open. Memory as shared_quantity — weak, no settlement figure. I don't think a new thesis is warranted. I'll return empty new_theses.\nWait — is there a thesis-worthy question about Nvidia's gross margin / cost structure from memory costs? The rules say don't open a thesis about \"a financial measure's level, growth, margin or guidance for any period, on its own.\" So no.\nWhat about \"Will Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack gross margin be pressured by memory cost inflation?\" That's a margin thesis — not allowed on its own.\nDouble-check no new thesis needed for memory shared_quantity. I'll not open. Good.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
